SLIX musl POSIX shim
====================

Arch-neutral implementation of the musl POSIX surface that runs in
kernel mode (EL1 on aarch64, ring 0 on x86_64) inside the syscall
handler. Each architecture's syscall entry decodes the syscall
number from the saved frame and tail-calls `posix_dispatch` here
with up to six i64 args; the dispatcher routes by the SLIX-local
syscall number (>= 128) to the right `sys_*` handler.

Handlers do their own work in two ways:

- **Local ops** (POSIX fd table bookkeeping, STDIO writes,
  per-process state) execute directly in this layer.
- **Server-backed ops** (sockets via inet, files / pipes / TTY via
  VFS) translate to IPC messages on the matching server port.

The shim runs on the user process's page table (the syscall does
not switch PTBR), so user pointers dereference directly. For VFS
calls that need to copy across address spaces, the shim creates a
kernel-side memory grant via `kernel_grant_create_inline` and
hands the grant id to VFS, which `grant_map`s the page into its
own address space.

Architecture interface — only one function is arch-specific:

- `extern shim_uart_putc(c: int)` — write a byte directly to the
  platform UART. Used for the STDOUT / STDERR fast path until TTY
  routing is wired in. Each arch provides this as a no-mangle
  global symbol from its assembly file (PL011 MMIO on aarch64,
  COM1 port-I/O on x86_64).

Everything else this file uses comes from the shared microkernel
modules (`oskit.ipc`, `oskit.services`, `oskit.kernel`).

    module oskit.posix

    import oskit.ipc.{port_lookup, ipc_send}
    import oskit.services.{sleep, notify_wait, notify_read_self, sleep_or_notify, uptime}
    import oskit.kernel.{current_thread, terminate_current,
        kernel_grant_create_inline, kernel_grant_revoke_inline,
        kernel_get_thread_pid, kernel_get_process_main_tid,
        kernel_continue_current}
    import oskit.arch.{pack_kstat, KSTAT_SIZE}

    extern shim_uart_putc(c: int)


POSIX fd table
==============

Per-process POSIX file descriptor table. Each process gets 64
slots; slots 0/1/2 are lazily initialized to STDIN/STDOUT/STDERR
so a fresh musl process can `write(1, ...)` without any explicit
setup from PM.

Slot kinds:

- `POSIX_FD_FREE` — slot is available for allocation.
- `POSIX_FD_STDIN` — read returns EOF, write is rejected.
- `POSIX_FD_STDOUT` / `POSIX_FD_STDERR` — write goes directly to
  the UART (retained fast-path until TTY routing lands), read is
  rejected.
- `POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET` — `target` holds the inet server's
  socket id; read/write/close/sendto/recvfrom route through
  `ipc_send` to the inet port.
- `POSIX_FD_FILE` — `target` holds a VFS handle (file, TTY,
  pipe end, or TCP via VFS scheme); all read/write/close/lseek
  forward to the VFS port. `pos` is unused (VFS tracks
  position server-side). `flags` carries POSIX flags
  (O_NONBLOCK, etc.) the shim consults locally.

Generation tracking uses `processes[pid].main_tid` as the sentinel:
when a pid slot is reused by a new process, the new `main_tid`
differs from the last-initialized value, which triggers a fresh
table reset. That avoids leaking fd state across process reuse
without a PM-side cleanup hook.

Not handled in this pass: process-exit cleanup of held socket
handles on the inet server side (the inet record still quotes the
terminated thread as owner). Future work tracked under the
posix_fd_bridge memory.

    val POSIX_FD_FREE        = 0
    val POSIX_FD_STDIN       = 1
    val POSIX_FD_STDOUT      = 2
    val POSIX_FD_STDERR      = 3
    val POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET  = 4
    val POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET  = 5
    val POSIX_FD_FILE        = 6
    val POSIX_FD_EPOLL       = 7
    val POSIX_FD_EVENTFD     = 8
    val POSIX_FD_TIMERFD     = 9
    val POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET = 10

    val POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC   = 64


Subset of POSIX/Linux errno values used by the shim.  Returned to
userspace as the *negative* of these — `i64(-EINVAL)`, etc.  Values
are sourced from `linux/include/uapi/asm-generic/errno-base.h` plus
`asm-generic/errno.h`; only those actually returned by some shim
codepath are listed here.  Keep numerically sorted so a future
reader can scan by errno number.

    const ENOENT           = 2
    const EIO              = 5
    const EBADF            = 9
    const EAGAIN           = 11
    const ENOMEM           = 12
    const EFAULT           = 14
    const EEXIST           = 17
    const ENODEV           = 19
    const EINVAL           = 22
    const EMFILE           = 24
    const ENOSPC           = 28
    const ESPIPE           = 29
    const EPIPE            = 32
    const ENAMETOOLONG     = 36
    const ENOSYS           = 38
    const EISCONN          = 56
    const EMSGSIZE         = 90
    const ENOPROTOOPT      = 92
    const EPROTONOSUPPORT  = 93
    const EOPNOTSUPP       = 95
    const EAFNOSUPPORT     = 97
    const EADDRINUSE       = 98
    const ENETUNREACH      = 101
    const ENOTCONN         = 107
    const ETIMEDOUT        = 110
    const ECONNREFUSED     = 111
    const EINPROGRESS      = 115


    struct PosixFd
        kind: int
        target: int
        flags: int
        pos: i64
        // setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF/SO_RCVBUF) requested sizes that
        // arrived before the inet slot existed.  0 = "no override,
        // use the inet-side default".  Applied via INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF
        // immediately after connect/listen establishes the slot.
        pending_sndbuf: int
        pending_rcvbuf: int
        // Persisted setsockopt flag values so getsockopt echoes the
        // actually-requested value instead of zero (Linux-compat for
        // option introspection).  These flags are not yet enforced
        // — REUSEADDR doesn't relax bind, KEEPALIVE has no idle
        // timer, NODELAY would only matter if Nagle existed.  Real
        // enforcement is its own follow-up.
        flag_reuseaddr: int
        flag_broadcast: int
        flag_keepalive: int
        flag_reuseport: int
        flag_nodelay: int
        // SO_LINGER state. `linger_on != 0` activates the option;
        // `linger_secs == 0` (with linger_on) is the "abort-on-close
        // → RST instead of FIN" shortcut, the only enforcement
        // landed so far. Non-zero linger_secs is persisted but its
        // flush-or-timeout semantics aren't enforced yet.
        linger_on: int
        linger_secs: int
        // setsockopt(IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_KEEPIDLE/INTVL/CNT) — caller-
        // requested per-socket keepalive overrides. 0 = "no override,
        // use the inet-side default".  Idle/intvl persisted as the
        // user's seconds value (the conversion to ticks happens at
        // the IPC boundary so getsockopt round-trips the seconds
        // unchanged); cnt is a raw probe count.
        keep_idle_secs: int
        keep_intvl_secs: int
        keep_cnt: int
        // POSIX_FD_EVENTFD state. `eventfd_count` is the running
        // counter; read returns the count and resets it (or
        // decrements to 0 in semaphore mode), write adds to it.
        // `eventfd_semaphore` records EFD_SEMAPHORE so successive
        // reads decrement by 1 instead of returning the full
        // counter at once.
        eventfd_count: i64
        eventfd_semaphore: int
        // POSIX_FD_TIMERFD state. `interval_ticks` is the periodic
        // re-arm in scheduler ticks (100 Hz); 0 means one-shot.
        // `next_tick` is the absolute uptime() tick at which the
        // next expiration is due; 0 means disarmed. `expirations`
        // accumulates fires that haven't been consumed via read()
        // yet; the next read returns the count and resets it.
        timerfd_interval_ticks: int
        timerfd_next_tick: int
        timerfd_expirations: i64
        // SO_ERROR per-fd. Holds the positive errno of the most
        // recent socket-layer failure (ECONNREFUSED, ENETUNREACH,
        // EADDRINUSE, ...) that the kernel/inet surfaced to this
        // socket.  getsockopt(SOL_SOCKET, SO_ERROR) returns it and
        // clears it back to 0 — Linux read-and-reset semantics.
        last_error: int
        // SO_ACCEPTCONN read-side flag. Set when sys_listen succeeds
        // on this TCP fd; getsockopt(SO_ACCEPTCONN) returns it.
        // Stays 1 across the listen socket's lifetime — Linux clears
        // it only on close. Children produced by accept() are fresh
        // fds with is_listen = 0.
        is_listen: int
        // POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET connect() state. `udp_connected != 0`
        // means a default peer was set via sys_connect; sendto with
        // a null addr / zero addrlen then uses the saved peer (the
        // libc `send()` path). Cleared by re-connecting with NULL
        // sockaddr or sa_family == AF_UNSPEC (POSIX dissolve).
        // Peer IP stored as 4 octets to match the inet IPC wire
        // format (network byte order).
        udp_connected: int
        udp_peer_port: int
        udp_peer_a: int
        udp_peer_b: int
        udp_peer_c: int
        udp_peer_d: int
        // SO_RCVTIMEO / SO_SNDTIMEO — millisecond timeouts.
        // 0 = "no timeout" (blocking forever).  Receive rides
        // recv_timeout_ms into INET_CMD_RECVFROM_TIMEOUT.  Send
        // rides send_timeout_ms into INET_CMD_TCP_SEND (TCP only;
        // wire field is u16 so values are clamped to 65535 ms).
        recv_timeout_ms: int
        send_timeout_ms: int

`tcp_user_timeout_ms` is the caller-requested per-connection
RFC 5482 timeout, in milliseconds (the unit RFC 5482 §3 specifies
— the man page is misleading about seconds).  0 means "use the
system default" — slix has no sysctl, so 0 means no cap.

Persisted on the shim fd so `getsockopt` round-trips the user's
exact ms value; pushed to inet via `INET_CMD_TCP_SET_USERTO` when
a slot exists, and replayed on accept-children so listener
settings propagate to passive opens (matches Linux behaviour for
the keepalive options).

        tcp_user_timeout_ms: int

    var posix_fd_table: [1024]PosixFd     // 16 procs * 64 fds
    var posix_fd_last_gen: [16]int        // main_tid when last initialized

    posix_fd_init_pid(pid: int)
        val base = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
        var i = 0
        while i < POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            posix_fd_table[base + i].kind = POSIX_FD_FREE
            posix_fd_table[base + i].target = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flags = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].pos = i64(0)
            posix_fd_table[base + i].pending_sndbuf = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].pending_rcvbuf = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_reuseaddr = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_broadcast = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_keepalive = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_reuseport = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_nodelay = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].linger_on = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].linger_secs = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_idle_secs = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_intvl_secs = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_cnt = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].eventfd_count = i64(0)
            posix_fd_table[base + i].eventfd_semaphore = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_interval_ticks = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_next_tick = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_expirations = i64(0)
            posix_fd_table[base + i].last_error = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].is_listen = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_connected = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_peer_port = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_peer_a = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_peer_b = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_peer_c = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_peer_d = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].recv_timeout_ms = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].send_timeout_ms = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].tcp_user_timeout_ms = 0
            i += 1
        posix_fd_table[base + 0].kind = POSIX_FD_STDIN
        posix_fd_table[base + 1].kind = POSIX_FD_STDOUT
        posix_fd_table[base + 2].kind = POSIX_FD_STDERR

    posix_fd_ensure_init(pid: int)
        val gen = kernel_get_process_main_tid(pid)
        if posix_fd_last_gen[pid] != gen
            posix_fd_init_pid(pid)
            posix_fd_last_gen[pid] = gen

    posix_fd_current_pid() -> int
        kernel_get_thread_pid(current_thread)

    posix_fd_kind(fd: int) -> int
        if fd < 0
            return POSIX_FD_FREE
        if fd >= POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            return POSIX_FD_FREE
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return POSIX_FD_FREE
        if pid >= 16
            return POSIX_FD_FREE
        posix_fd_ensure_init(pid)
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].kind

    posix_fd_target(fd: int) -> int
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].target

    posix_fd_flags(fd: int) -> int
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].flags

    posix_fd_set_flags(fd: int, flags: int)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].flags = flags

    posix_fd_alloc(kind: int, target: int) -> int
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return -1
        if pid >= 16
            return -1
        posix_fd_ensure_init(pid)
        val base = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
        var i = 3
        while i < POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            if posix_fd_table[base + i].kind == POSIX_FD_FREE
                posix_fd_table[base + i].kind = kind
                posix_fd_table[base + i].target = target
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flags = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].pos = i64(0)
                posix_fd_table[base + i].pending_sndbuf = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].pending_rcvbuf = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_reuseaddr = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_broadcast = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_keepalive = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_reuseport = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_nodelay = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].linger_on = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].linger_secs = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_idle_secs = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_intvl_secs = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_cnt = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].eventfd_count = i64(0)
                posix_fd_table[base + i].eventfd_semaphore = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_interval_ticks = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_next_tick = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_expirations = i64(0)
                posix_fd_table[base + i].last_error = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].is_listen = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_connected = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_peer_port = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_peer_a = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_peer_b = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_peer_c = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].udp_peer_d = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].recv_timeout_ms = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].send_timeout_ms = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].tcp_user_timeout_ms = 0
                return i
            i += 1
        -1

Record a positive errno on a socket fd so the next
`getsockopt(SOL_SOCKET, SO_ERROR)` returns it (and clears it).
Caller passes the kernel's negative errno; we store the absolute
value to match Linux's `int *optval` convention.

    posix_fd_set_error(fd: int, neg_errno: i64)
        if fd < 0
            return
        if fd >= POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            return
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return
        if pid >= 16
            return
        var v = int(-neg_errno)
        if v < 0
            v = -v
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].last_error = v

Iteration-based "is there another fd in this process pointing
at the same backend?" check. Used by `sys_close` to avoid issuing
an inet close while a `dup`-ped fd still references the same
inet record. STDIO kinds share kind/target = (1/2/3, 0), so dup
of STDOUT works the same way: don't tear down anything when one
of two pointing at the same target closes.

    posix_fd_other_dup_exists(fd: int, kind: int, target: int) -> int
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return 0
        if pid >= 16
            return 0
        val base = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
        var i = 0
        while i < POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            if i != fd
                if posix_fd_table[base + i].kind == kind
                    if posix_fd_table[base + i].target == target
                        return 1
            i += 1
        0

    posix_fd_clear(fd: int)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return
        if pid >= 16
            return
        if fd < 0
            return
        if fd >= POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            return
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        posix_fd_table[idx].kind = POSIX_FD_FREE
        posix_fd_table[idx].target = 0
        posix_fd_table[idx].flags = 0
        posix_fd_table[idx].pos = i64(0)


PM-mediated cross-process fd transplant
---------------------------------------

`posix_fd_transplant` copies a `PosixFd` slot from the
sender's process into a destination process's slot. Used by
`PM_CMD_TRANSPLANT_FD` (and, in a future commit, by the
arbitrary-kind SCM_RIGHTS receive path) to give a second
process its own fd that points at the same backend handle
as the sender's fd.

The destination slot must currently be `POSIX_FD_FREE` —
the caller is responsible for closing any prior occupant.
Returning `-EEXIST` instead of silently overwriting matches
how `dup3` rejects an active dst.

For `POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET` slots the unix server's
multi-owner ring also needs the destination's main_tid added
so subsequent `unix_fd_valid(target, dst_tid)` checks pass
and so PID_EXIT cleanup walks the ring correctly. Other
backends (inet/vfs/eventfd/timerfd) carry per-fd state
without an explicit ownership ring; the slot copy alone is
enough for Stage 1 — Stage 2 (arbitrary-kind SCM_RIGHTS)
will revisit ownership for inet/vfs.

`sender_tid` is the IPC sender (PM, in normal use); we
resolve their pid via `kernel_get_thread_pid` before doing
the slot lookup.

    const UNIX_CMD_ADD_OWNER_C = 28

    posix_fd_transplant(sender_tid: int, src_fd: int, dst_pid: int, dst_fd: int) -> int
        if src_fd < 0
            return -EBADF
        if src_fd >= POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            return -EBADF
        if dst_fd < 0
            return -EBADF
        if dst_fd >= POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            return -EBADF
        val src_pid = kernel_get_thread_pid(sender_tid)
        if src_pid <= 0
            return -EINVAL
        if src_pid >= 16
            return -EINVAL
        if dst_pid <= 0
            return -EINVAL
        if dst_pid >= 16
            return -EINVAL
        if src_pid == dst_pid
            if src_fd == dst_fd
                return -EINVAL
        posix_fd_ensure_init(src_pid)
        posix_fd_ensure_init(dst_pid)
        val src_idx = src_pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + src_fd
        val dst_idx = dst_pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + dst_fd
        if posix_fd_table[src_idx].kind == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return -EBADF
        if posix_fd_table[dst_idx].kind != POSIX_FD_FREE
            return -EEXIST

Copy the entire PosixFd struct field by field — sysl
doesn't expose a struct assignment that handles per-field
i64 vs int slots in one shot, and the slots carry per-fd
state (eventfd_count, timerfd_*, last_error, etc.) that
must move with the slot.

        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].kind = posix_fd_table[src_idx].kind
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].target = posix_fd_table[src_idx].target
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].flags = posix_fd_table[src_idx].flags
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].pos = posix_fd_table[src_idx].pos
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].pending_sndbuf = posix_fd_table[src_idx].pending_sndbuf
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].pending_rcvbuf = posix_fd_table[src_idx].pending_rcvbuf
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].flag_reuseaddr = posix_fd_table[src_idx].flag_reuseaddr
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].flag_broadcast = posix_fd_table[src_idx].flag_broadcast
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].flag_keepalive = posix_fd_table[src_idx].flag_keepalive
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].flag_reuseport = posix_fd_table[src_idx].flag_reuseport
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].flag_nodelay = posix_fd_table[src_idx].flag_nodelay
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].linger_on = posix_fd_table[src_idx].linger_on
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].linger_secs = posix_fd_table[src_idx].linger_secs
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].keep_idle_secs = posix_fd_table[src_idx].keep_idle_secs
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].keep_intvl_secs = posix_fd_table[src_idx].keep_intvl_secs
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].keep_cnt = posix_fd_table[src_idx].keep_cnt
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].eventfd_count = posix_fd_table[src_idx].eventfd_count
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].eventfd_semaphore = posix_fd_table[src_idx].eventfd_semaphore
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].timerfd_interval_ticks = posix_fd_table[src_idx].timerfd_interval_ticks
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].timerfd_next_tick = posix_fd_table[src_idx].timerfd_next_tick
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].timerfd_expirations = posix_fd_table[src_idx].timerfd_expirations
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].last_error = posix_fd_table[src_idx].last_error
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].is_listen = posix_fd_table[src_idx].is_listen
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].udp_connected = posix_fd_table[src_idx].udp_connected
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].udp_peer_port = posix_fd_table[src_idx].udp_peer_port
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].udp_peer_a = posix_fd_table[src_idx].udp_peer_a
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].udp_peer_b = posix_fd_table[src_idx].udp_peer_b
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].udp_peer_c = posix_fd_table[src_idx].udp_peer_c
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].udp_peer_d = posix_fd_table[src_idx].udp_peer_d
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].recv_timeout_ms = posix_fd_table[src_idx].recv_timeout_ms
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].send_timeout_ms = posix_fd_table[src_idx].send_timeout_ms
        posix_fd_table[dst_idx].tcp_user_timeout_ms = posix_fd_table[src_idx].tcp_user_timeout_ms

For backed-by-server kinds, fan out to the appropriate backend
so its owners ring tracks the destination process's main thread
as a co-owner. Without this the destination process's per-server
`*_fd_valid(target, my_tid)` check would reject every subsequent
op against the slot, and a close from the source process would
tear the slot down out from under the destination.

AF_UNIX uses `UNIX_CMD_ADD_OWNER` (handle = unix-socket slot
index, 1 byte). TCP/UDP use `INET_CMD_ADD_OWNER` (handle = inet
slot index, 2 bytes BE). Phase 2A: VFS support deferred — files
fall through and the source-side close still tears the file down
unless the source process is careful (Phase 2A test scope is
TCP-only).

        val src_kind = posix_fd_table[src_idx].kind
        val dst_main_tid = kernel_get_process_main_tid(dst_pid)
        if src_kind == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            val unix_p = unix_get_port()
            if unix_p >= 0
                if dst_main_tid > 0
                    var msg: [6]byte
                    msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_ADD_OWNER_C)
                    msg[1] = byte(posix_fd_table[src_idx].target & 0xff)
                    msg[2] = byte((dst_main_tid >> 24) & 0xff)
                    msg[3] = byte((dst_main_tid >> 16) & 0xff)
                    msg[4] = byte((dst_main_tid >> 8) & 0xff)
                    msg[5] = byte(dst_main_tid & 0xff)
                    var rep: [1]byte
                    ipc_send(unix_p, &msg[0], 6, &rep[0], 1)
        if src_kind == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            val inet_p = inet_get_port()
            if inet_p >= 0
                if dst_main_tid > 0
                    val target = posix_fd_table[src_idx].target
                    var msg: [8]byte
                    msg[0] = byte(58)
                    msg[1] = byte(1)
                    msg[2] = byte((target >> 8) & 0xff)
                    msg[3] = byte(target & 0xff)
                    msg[4] = byte((dst_main_tid >> 24) & 0xff)
                    msg[5] = byte((dst_main_tid >> 16) & 0xff)
                    msg[6] = byte((dst_main_tid >> 8) & 0xff)
                    msg[7] = byte(dst_main_tid & 0xff)
                    var rep: [1]byte
                    ipc_send(inet_p, &msg[0], 8, &rep[0], 1)
        if src_kind == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            val inet_p = inet_get_port()
            if inet_p >= 0
                if dst_main_tid > 0
                    val target = posix_fd_table[src_idx].target
                    var msg: [8]byte
                    msg[0] = byte(58)
                    msg[1] = byte(0)
                    msg[2] = byte((target >> 8) & 0xff)
                    msg[3] = byte(target & 0xff)
                    msg[4] = byte((dst_main_tid >> 24) & 0xff)
                    msg[5] = byte((dst_main_tid >> 16) & 0xff)
                    msg[6] = byte((dst_main_tid >> 8) & 0xff)
                    msg[7] = byte(dst_main_tid & 0xff)
                    var rep: [1]byte
                    ipc_send(inet_p, &msg[0], 8, &rep[0], 1)

POSIX_FD_FILE has per-pid VFS handles (the `target` field is the
src process's handle into the *src*'s vfs handle table, meaningless
to dst). Ask vfs to mint a fresh dst-side handle pointing at the
same OFT entry; on success we overwrite dst's `target`. If the IPC
fails we leave the slot in its (src-target) state and roll back to
POSIX_FD_FREE so the dst doesn't see a phantom file. VFS_CMD_DUP_HANDLE
wire is `cmd(1) + src_pid_be(4) + src_handle_be(4) + dst_pid_be(4)`.

        if src_kind == POSIX_FD_FILE
            val vfs_p = vfs_get_port()
            if vfs_p >= 0
                val src_handle = posix_fd_table[src_idx].target
                var msg: [13]byte
                msg[0] = byte(25)
                msg[1] = byte((src_pid >> 24) & 0xff)
                msg[2] = byte((src_pid >> 16) & 0xff)
                msg[3] = byte((src_pid >> 8) & 0xff)
                msg[4] = byte(src_pid & 0xff)
                msg[5] = byte((src_handle >> 24) & 0xff)
                msg[6] = byte((src_handle >> 16) & 0xff)
                msg[7] = byte((src_handle >> 8) & 0xff)
                msg[8] = byte(src_handle & 0xff)
                msg[9] = byte((dst_pid >> 24) & 0xff)
                msg[10] = byte((dst_pid >> 16) & 0xff)
                msg[11] = byte((dst_pid >> 8) & 0xff)
                msg[12] = byte(dst_pid & 0xff)
                var rep: [5]byte
                ipc_send(vfs_p, &msg[0], 13, &rep[0], 5)
                if rep[0] == 0
                    val new_handle = (int(rep[1]) << 24) | (int(rep[2]) << 16) | (int(rep[3]) << 8) | int(rep[4])
                    posix_fd_table[dst_idx].target = new_handle
                else
                    posix_fd_table[dst_idx].kind = POSIX_FD_FREE
                    return -EBADF
            else
                posix_fd_table[dst_idx].kind = POSIX_FD_FREE
                return -EBADF
        0


INET client helpers (shim-side)
-------------------------------

Minimal wire helpers that mirror `oskit/servers/inet.lsysl`'s
message layout. The shim talks to the inet server via the same
IPC protocol that `oskit/net/client.lsysl` uses from user space;
we replicate the writers here instead of importing `oskit.net` to
keep the kernel link free of the full socket-API module.

    const INET_CMD_SOCKET      = 1
    const INET_CMD_BIND        = 2
    const INET_CMD_SENDTO      = 3
    const INET_CMD_RECVFROM    = 4
    const INET_CMD_CLOSE       = 5
    const INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT = 10
    const INET_CMD_TCP_SEND    = 11
    const INET_CMD_TCP_RECV    = 12
    const INET_CMD_TCP_CLOSE   = 13
    const INET_CMD_TCP_LISTEN  = 14
    const INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT  = 15
    const INET_CMD_UDP_GETNAME = 24
    const INET_CMD_TCP_GETNAME = 25
    const INET_CMD_RECVFROM_TIMEOUT = 18
    const INET_CMD_TCP_RECV_NB = 26
    const INET_CMD_TCP_SHUTDOWN = 27
    const INET_CMD_POLL        = 28
    const INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB   = 29
    const INET_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB = 30
    const INET_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE = 31
    const INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT_NB = 32
    const INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT_NB = 33
    const INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF  = 34
    const INET_CMD_TCP_GETBUF  = 35
    const INET_CMD_TCP_ABORT_CLOSE = 36
    const INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPALIVE = 37
    const INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPOPT = 39
    const INET_CMD_TCP_GET_KEEPOPT = 40
    const INET_CMD_TCP_GET_ERR     = 41
    const INET_CMD_TCP_GET_INFO    = 43
    const INET_CMD_TCP_SET_USERTO  = 52
    const INET_CMD_TCP_BLACKHOLE_ACKS_TEST = 53
    const INET_CMD_ADD_OWNER_C     = 58

    const INET_MAX_TCP_SEG     = 480

    val POSIX_AF_UNSPEC      = 0
    val POSIX_AF_UNIX        = 1
    val POSIX_AF_INET        = 2
    val POSIX_SOCK_DGRAM     = 2
    val POSIX_SOCK_STREAM    = 1


AF_UNIX wire protocol mirror for the unix server. Track
`oskit/servers/unix.lsysl`. Path is 108 bytes max
(POSIX `sockaddr_un.sun_path[108]`).

    const UNIX_CMD_SOCKET    = 1
    const UNIX_CMD_BIND      = 2
    const UNIX_CMD_CONNECT   = 3
    const UNIX_CMD_LISTEN    = 4
    const UNIX_CMD_ACCEPT    = 5
    const UNIX_CMD_SENDTO    = 6
    const UNIX_CMD_RECVFROM  = 7
    const UNIX_CMD_SEND      = 8
    const UNIX_CMD_RECV      = 9
    const UNIX_CMD_CLOSE     = 10
    const UNIX_CMD_PID_EXIT  = 11
    const UNIX_CMD_SENDMSG   = 12
    const UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG   = 13
    const UNIX_CMD_ACCEPT_NB        = 14
    const UNIX_CMD_RECVFROM_NB      = 15
    const UNIX_CMD_RECV_NB          = 16
    const UNIX_CMD_POLL             = 17
    const UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_SUB        = 18
    const UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB      = 19
    const UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE = 20
    const UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG_NB       = 21
    const UNIX_CMD_GETSOCKNAME      = 22
    const UNIX_CMD_GETPEERNAME      = 23
    const UNIX_CMD_SOCKETPAIR       = 24
    const UNIX_CMD_SHUTDOWN         = 25
    const UNIX_CMD_PEERCRED         = 26
    const UNIX_CMD_DISCONNECT       = 27

    const UNIX_PATH_MAX      = 108
    const UNIX_SCM_MAX_FDS   = 4

SCM_RIGHTS cmsg constants. SOL_SOCKET / SCM_RIGHTS match
Linux UAPI; the cmsghdr is 16 bytes on LP64
(`size_t cmsg_len + int cmsg_level + int cmsg_type`).

    val SHIM_SOL_SOCKET      = 1
    val SHIM_SCM_RIGHTS      = 1
    val SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN    = 16

sendmsg/recvmsg gather/scatter caps. `SHIM_IOV_MAX` bounds
the iovec array we'll walk; `SHIM_MSG_MAX` bounds the
total payload bytes (Ethernet MTU for UDP, plenty for TCP).

    val SHIM_IOV_MAX         = 8
    val SHIM_MSG_MAX         = 1472

socket(2) / accept4(2) flag bits OR'd into `stype` / `flags`.
SOCK_NONBLOCK is honoured (mirrored into the slot's flags field
as `POSIX_O_NONBLOCK`); SOCK_CLOEXEC is accepted and ignored
(no fork/exec yet). Used by both `sys_socket` and `sys_accept4`,
so they live up here near `POSIX_SOCK_DGRAM`.

    val POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK  = 2048
    val POSIX_SOCK_CLOEXEC   = 524288

POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` is Linux octal 04000 = 2048. We only track this
flag today; other open(2) flags (O_APPEND etc.) would need the
shim to honour them, so we mask them off on `F_SETFL`.

`O_CREAT` (0o100) and `O_EXCL` (0o200) are inspected once during
`sys_openat` to drive the create-then-retry path; we don't store
them on the fd so they won't surface from `F_GETFL`. `O_TRUNC`
(0o1000) is parsed but left as a deferred — TFS has no truncate
operation today, so an `open(O_WRONLY|O_TRUNC)` against an
existing file just rewrites starting at offset 0 (the caller is
expected to know whether the residual tail matters).

    val POSIX_O_NONBLOCK     = 2048
    val POSIX_O_CREAT        = 64
    val POSIX_O_EXCL         = 128
    val POSIX_O_TRUNC        = 512

POSIX recv()/send() flags. Currently only DONTWAIT (per-call NB
override) and PEEK (read without dequeuing) are honored — the
others are accepted-and-ignored by the recv path, never inspected
by send.

    val POSIX_MSG_PEEK       = 2
    val POSIX_MSG_DONTWAIT   = 64

fcntl command codes (`<fcntl.h>` in musl arch).

    val POSIX_F_DUPFD        = 0
    val POSIX_F_GETFD        = 1
    val POSIX_F_SETFD        = 2
    val POSIX_F_GETFL        = 3
    val POSIX_F_SETFL        = 4

    var inet_port_cached = -1

    inet_get_port() -> int
        if inet_port_cached < 0
            inet_port_cached = port_lookup("inet")
        inet_port_cached

    var unix_port_cached = -1

    unix_get_port() -> int
        if unix_port_cached < 0
            unix_port_cached = port_lookup("unix")
        unix_port_cached


Sign-extend a wire status byte. The unix server emits
`byte(-EADDRINUSE)` etc., which round-trip back as unsigned
0..255; this restores the negative value.

    unix_status(b: byte) -> int
        val v = int(b) & 0xff
        if v >= 128
            return v - 256
        v

    shim_write_u16(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte(v & 0xff)

    shim_read_u16(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((int(p[off]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff)


VFS client helpers (shim-side)
------------------------------

The shim talks to VFS the same way `oskit/fs/client.lsysl` does:
big-endian wire format, command numbers from `oskit/servers
/vfs.lsysl`. We replicate the writers locally to avoid pulling
the user-space FS module into the kernel link.

VFS read/write protocol uses memory grants — the shim creates a
grant against the *user* buffer (granter_pid = current pid) and
hands the grant id to VFS, which `grant_map`s the page into its
own address space, copies, and returns. The grant is revoked on
the way out so it doesn't leak.

    val VFS_CMD_OPEN_C    = 1
    val VFS_CMD_READ_C    = 2
    val VFS_CMD_WRITE_C   = 3
    val VFS_CMD_CREATE_C  = 4
    val VFS_CMD_UNLINK_C  = 5
    val VFS_CMD_STAT_C    = 6
    val VFS_CMD_MKDIR_C   = 7
    val VFS_CMD_RMDIR_C   = 8
    val VFS_CMD_CHMOD_C   = 11
    val VFS_CMD_RENAME_C  = 12
    val VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C   = 13
    val VFS_CMD_SEEK_C    = 14
    val VFS_CMD_CONNECT_C = 15
    val VFS_CMD_PIPE_C    = 18
    // Phase A2 epoll bridge — mirrors INET_CMD_EPOLL_*.
    val VFS_CMD_POLL_C              = 20
    val VFS_CMD_EPOLL_SUB_C         = 21
    val VFS_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB_C       = 22
    val VFS_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE_C  = 23
    // Phase 4 chunk 2 — fstat by handle.
    val VFS_CMD_FSTAT_C   = 24

`GRANT_READ` / `GRANT_WRITE` mirror the values in `oskit.kernel`'s
private grant table (1 = read by mapper, 2 = write by mapper).

    val SHIM_GRANT_READ  = 1
    val SHIM_GRANT_WRITE = 2

    var vfs_port_cached = -1

    vfs_get_port() -> int
        if vfs_port_cached < 0
            vfs_port_cached = port_lookup("fs")
        vfs_port_cached


    var tty_port_cached = -1

    tty_get_port() -> int
        if tty_port_cached < 0
            tty_port_cached = port_lookup("tty")
        tty_port_cached


TTY IPC commands (must track oskit/drivers/tty/tty.lsysl).
The shim only uses READ + POLL + EPOLL_SUB/UNSUB/INST_CLOSE;
the WRITE/PUTS/SWITCH commands are driven from the legacy ulib.

    val TTY_CMD_READ_C            = 2
    val TTY_CMD_POLL_C            = 5
    val TTY_CMD_EPOLL_SUB_C       = 6
    val TTY_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB_C     = 7
    val TTY_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE_C = 8

Big-endian 32-bit helpers for the VFS wire format.

    shim_write_be_i32(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        p[off + 2] = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 3] = byte(v & 0xff)

    shim_read_be_i32(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((int(p[off]) & 0xff) << 24) | ((int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(p[off + 2]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(p[off + 3]) & 0xff)


epoll instance table (shim-side)
--------------------------------

Per-process level-triggered epoll. Each `POSIX_FD_EPOLL` slot
points at an instance row here; the row holds a small array of
watched (fd, events_mask, user_data) tuples. `events_mask` is
the user's request bits (`EPOLLIN`/`EPOLLOUT`/...); `user_data`
is the 8-byte cookie from `struct epoll_event.data` echoed
back unchanged on every wake.

`epoll_pwait` polls each watched fd's readiness via
`INET_CMD_POLL` (sockets) or by treating files/pipes as
always-ready (a stopgap until VFS grows a polling primitive).
Phase A2 layered an inet→shim notify path on top so indefinite
waits block via `notify_wait()` instead of polling.

The table is sized for 4 instances per process, 32 watched
fds each — way more than the smoke tests need but cheap.
Generation tracking via `posix_fd_last_gen` reuses the same
sentinel as `posix_fd_table` so a stale instance from a
previous tenant of the pid is reset on first touch.

    val POSIX_EPOLL_INSTANCES_PER_PROC = 4
    val POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE = 32
    val POSIX_EPOLL_TABLE_SIZE = 64    // 16 procs * 4 instances

EPOLL_* event bits (must match musl's `<sys/epoll.h>`).

    val POSIX_EPOLLIN       = 0x001
    val POSIX_EPOLLOUT      = 0x004
    val POSIX_EPOLLERR      = 0x008
    val POSIX_EPOLLHUP      = 0x010
    val POSIX_EPOLLONESHOT  = 0x40000000
    val POSIX_EPOLLET       = -2147483648    // 1 << 31, signed view

EPOLL_CTL_* opcodes.

    val POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_ADD = 1
    val POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_DEL = 2
    val POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_MOD = 3

`struct epoll_event` on LP64 little-endian (matches both aarch64
and x86_64 musl layouts): events @0 (4), pad @4 (4), data @8
(8) = 16 bytes total. We store events as int and data as i64;
the shim copies bytes 0..3 and 8..15 of the user struct.

    val POSIX_EPOLL_EVENT_SIZE = 16

    struct EpollEntry
        in_use: int
        watched_fd: int
        events: int
        data_lo: i64
        last_reported: int   // for EPOLLET: bits last delivered
        armed: int           // 0 if EPOLLONESHOT fired and disarmed
        // Cached per-fd fire counter (returned by INET_CMD_POLL /
        // VFS_CMD_POLL). When the live counter advances past this,
        // the fd actually crossed an edge — we clear `last_reported`
        // so EPOLLET re-fires. Initialised to 0 on EPOLL_CTL_ADD,
        // -1 sentinel could've worked but 0 lines up with the
        // server-side counter starting at 0 for fresh slots.
        last_seen_seq: int

    struct EpollInstance
        in_use: int
        owner_gen: int     // matches kernel_get_process_main_tid
        entries: [32]EpollEntry

    var epoll_table: [64]EpollInstance

    epoll_inst_init(idx: int)
        epoll_table[idx].in_use = 0
        epoll_table[idx].owner_gen = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].in_use = 0
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].watched_fd = 0
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].events = 0
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].data_lo = i64(0)
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].last_reported = 0
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].armed = 0
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].last_seen_seq = 0
            i += 1

    epoll_alloc_inst() -> int
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return -1
        if pid >= 16
            return -1
        val gen = kernel_get_process_main_tid(pid)
        val base = pid * POSIX_EPOLL_INSTANCES_PER_PROC
        var i = 0
        while i < POSIX_EPOLL_INSTANCES_PER_PROC
            if epoll_table[base + i].in_use == 0
                epoll_inst_init(base + i)
                epoll_table[base + i].in_use = 1
                epoll_table[base + i].owner_gen = gen
                return base + i
            // Stale instance from a dead tenant — reclaim it.
            if epoll_table[base + i].owner_gen != gen
                epoll_inst_init(base + i)
                epoll_table[base + i].in_use = 1
                epoll_table[base + i].owner_gen = gen
                return base + i
            i += 1
        -1

    epoll_inst_release(idx: int)
        if idx < 0
            return
        if idx >= POSIX_EPOLL_TABLE_SIZE
            return
        epoll_inst_init(idx)


POSIX shim dispatch. SLIX-local syscall numbers start at 128; the
musl arch headers (`slix/musl/arch/<arch>-slix/bits/syscall.h.in`)
define the same numbers cross-arch. Handlers take the raw user-
space args and return bytes-written / 0-on-success / -errno as
i64, which the arch's syscall entry writes into the saved arg-0
register.

All fd-carrying syscalls route through the per-process fd table
above; see `posix_fd_kind` / `posix_fd_target`. Socket syscalls
translate to inet-server IPC, giving musl programs direct access
to the same UDP stack that native sysl programs use via
`oskit.net.client`.

SYS_FD_TRANSPLANT (85) — kernel-syscall entry for
`posix_fd_transplant`. Registered via `register_syscall6`
from the arch boot file; PM is the only intended caller
(see `pm_handle_transplant_fd`). Args:
  a0 = sender_tid (the IPC sender PM is acting on behalf of)
  a1 = src_fd
  a2 = dst_pid
  a3 = dst_fd
Returns 0 on success, negative errno on failure.

    sys_fd_transplant_handler(a0: i64, a1: i64, a2: i64, a3: i64, a4: i64, a5: i64) -> i64
        val r = posix_fd_transplant(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2), int(a3))
        kernel_continue_current()
        i64(r)


    posix_dispatch(num: int, a0: i64, a1: i64, a2: i64, a3: i64, a4: i64, a5: i64) -> i64
        if num == 128
            return sys_write(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 129
            terminate_current(int(a0))
            return i64(0)
        if num == 130
            return sys_read(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 131
            return sys_accept(int(a0), a1, a2)
        if num == 132
            return sys_accept4(int(a0), a1, a2, int(a3))
        if num == 135
            return sys_bind(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 147
            return sys_close(int(a0))
        if num == 148
            return sys_connect(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 151
            return sys_dup(int(a0))
        if num == 152
            return sys_dup3(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2))
        if num == 217
            return sys_listen(int(a0), int(a1))
        if num == 276
            return sys_recvfrom(int(a0), a1, int(a2), int(a3), a4, a5)
        if num == 278
            return sys_recvmsg(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 306
            return sys_sendmsg(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 307
            return sys_sendto(int(a0), a1, int(a2), int(a3), a4, int(a5))
        if num == 171
            return sys_fcntl(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2))
        if num == 192
            return sys_getpeername(int(a0), a1, a2)
        if num == 203
            return sys_getsockname(int(a0), a1, a2)
        if num == 204
            return sys_getsockopt(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2), a3, a4)
        if num == 326
            return sys_setsockopt(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2), a3, int(a4))
        if num == 334
            return sys_shutdown(int(a0), int(a1))
        if num == 337
            return sys_socket(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2))
        if num == 338
            return sys_socketpair(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2), a3)
        if num == 254
            return sys_openat(int(a0), a1, int(a2), int(a3))
        if num == 178
            return sys_fstat(int(a0), a1)
        if num == 252
            return sys_newfstatat(int(a0), a1, a2, int(a3))
        if num == 167
            return sys_fchmodat(int(a0), a1, int(a2), int(a3))
        if num == 227
            return sys_mkdirat(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 281
            return sys_renameat(int(a0), a1, int(a2), a3)
        if num == 282
            // renameat2 — flags arg in a4 is ignored (RENAME_EXCHANGE /
            // RENAME_NOREPLACE not supported yet).
            return sys_renameat(int(a0), a1, int(a2), a3)
        if num == 365
            return sys_unlinkat(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 221
            return sys_lseek(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 256
            return sys_pipe2(a0, int(a1))
        if num == 153
            return sys_epoll_create1(int(a0))
        if num == 154
            return sys_epoll_ctl(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2), a3)
        if num == 155
            return sys_epoll_pwait(int(a0), a1, int(a2), int(a3))
        if num == 258
            return sys_ppoll(a0, int(a1), a2)
        if num == 156
            return sys_eventfd2(int(a0), int(a1))
        if num == 356
            return sys_timerfd_create(int(a0), int(a1))
        if num == 357
            return sys_timerfd_gettime(int(a0), a1)
        if num == 358
            return sys_timerfd_settime(int(a0), int(a1), a2, a3)
        if num == 142
            return sys_clock_getres(int(a0), a1)
        if num == 143
            return sys_clock_gettime(int(a0), a1)
        if num == 144
            return sys_clock_nanosleep(int(a0), int(a1), a2, a3)
        if num == 206
            return sys_gettimeofday(a0, a1)
        if num == 251
            return sys_nanosleep(a0, a1)
        if num == 260
            return sys_pread64(int(a0), a1, int(a2), a3)
        if num == 268
            return sys_pwrite64(int(a0), a1, int(a2), a3)
        // Defensive stubs — universally probed by libc/jemalloc/
        // glibc-compat shims. Returning 0 (or zeros) is safe.
        if num == 159
            // faccessat(dirfd, path, mode, flags) — POSIX path
            // existence + permission probe. Slix has no real
            // permission bits yet, so we treat success-of-open
            // as "exists and accessible". F_OK / R_OK / W_OK /
            // X_OK collapse into the same probe.
            return sys_faccessat(int(a0), a1, int(a2), int(a3))
        if num == 160
            // faccessat2 — same as faccessat with an explicit
            // flags arg in a4 instead of a3. We map to the same
            // helper (slix doesn't honor AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW yet).
            return sys_faccessat(int(a0), a1, int(a2), int(a3))
        if num == 223
            // madvise(addr, len, advice) — unconditional 0. Slix
            // has no swap or page reclaim, so MADV_DONTNEED /
            // MADV_FREE / MADV_RANDOM are all no-ops.
            return i64(0)
        if num == 299
            // sched_yield — slix has no real scheduler hint path;
            // return 0 (Linux semantics). The caller will be
            // re-entered next tick anyway.
            return i64(0)
        if num == 259
            // prctl(option, ...) — Linux has dozens of subops. We
            // accept and 0-return PR_SET_NAME (15) / PR_GET_NAME
            // (16) since they're commonly called by pthread setup;
            // anything else is -EINVAL so libc fallback paths fire.
            if int(a0) == 15
                return i64(0)
            if int(a0) == 16
                return i64(0)
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if num == 201
            // getrusage(who, *rusage) — zero-fill the 144-byte
            // struct so RUSAGE_SELF / RUSAGE_CHILDREN both report
            // "no resources used yet". Real per-process accounting
            // is its own follow-up.
            if a1 == i64(0)
                return i64(-EFAULT)
            val rp: *byte = *byte(a1)
            var ri = 0
            while ri < 144
                rp[ri] = byte(0)
                ri += 1
            return i64(0)
        if num == 274
            return sys_readv(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 372
            return sys_writev(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 136
            return sys_brk(a0)
        // Process / thread identity. Multi-threading isn't a thing
        // in slix yet, so gettid == main thread of current process,
        // getppid == 1 (init), and the uid/gid family all return 0
        // (we boot as root). These are heavily probed by libc init
        // and any program that logs its PID.
        if num == 187
            return i64(0)               // getegid
        if num == 188
            return i64(0)               // geteuid
        if num == 189
            return i64(0)               // getgid
        if num == 194
            return i64(posix_fd_current_pid())  // getpid
        if num == 195
            return i64(1)               // getppid → init
        if num == 205
            return i64(current_thread)  // gettid
        if num == 207
            return i64(0)               // getuid
        if num == 197
            return sys_getrandom(a0, int(a1), int(a2))
        // fsync/fdatasync/sync/syncfs: slix has no on-disk
        // persistence yet (TFS is in RAM), so there's nothing to
        // flush. Returning 0 lets programs that defensively call
        // these after writes (sqlite, log writers, ...) keep
        // moving instead of crashing on -ENOSYS. fsync/fdatasync/
        // syncfs validate fd; sync takes no args.
        if num == 172
            if posix_fd_kind(int(a0)) == POSIX_FD_FREE
                return i64(-EBADF)
            return i64(0)
        if num == 180
            if posix_fd_kind(int(a0)) == POSIX_FD_FREE
                return i64(-EBADF)
            return i64(0)
        if num == 345
            return i64(0)
        if num == 347
            if posix_fd_kind(int(a0)) == POSIX_FD_FREE
                return i64(-EBADF)
            return i64(0)
        i64(-ENOSYS)

SYS_WRITE. Looks up `fd` in the process's POSIX fd table and
dispatches by kind. STDOUT/STDERR take the direct UART fast-path
via the arch-provided `shim_uart_putc`; TCP sockets chunk at
`INET_MAX_TCP_SEG=480` and loop until the full payload is
buffered. UDP sockets are rejected (use `sendto`). Any FREE slot
→ -EBADF. The user's buffer lives in the current ptbr which the
kernel inherits, so pointers dereference directly without
vm_copy_from.

    sys_write(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_STDOUT || k == POSIX_FD_STDERR
            var p: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var i = 0
            while i < len
                shim_uart_putc(int(p[i]))
                i += 1
            return i64(len)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            val pidw = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val styw = int(posix_fd_table[pidw * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].pos)
            if styw == POSIX_SOCK_DGRAM
                return sys_sendto_unix_dgram(fd, buf, len, i64(0), 0)
            return sys_send_unix_stream(fd, buf, len)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if inet_fd < 0
                return i64(-ENOTCONN)
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-ENODEV)
            val pid_w = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val tidx_w = pid_w * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
            // SO_SNDTIMEO clamp: u16 wire field maxes at 65535 ms.
            // Larger values fall back to "no timeout" (treated as 0
            // on the inet side) — Linux semantics for blocking writes
            // beyond a minute are functionally indistinguishable from
            // forever, so this loses no visible precision.
            var tmo_w = posix_fd_table[tidx_w].send_timeout_ms
            if tmo_w < 0 || tmo_w > 65535
                tmo_w = 0
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var sent_total = 0
            while sent_total < len
                var chunk = len - sent_total
                if chunk > INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
                    chunk = INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
                var msg: [487]byte
                msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SEND)
                shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
                shim_write_u16(msg, 3, chunk)
                shim_write_u16(msg, 5, tmo_w)
                var i = 0
                while i < chunk
                    msg[7 + i] = ubuf[sent_total + i]
                    i += 1
                var reply: [3]byte
                val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 7 + chunk, reply, 3)
                if r < 0
                    if sent_total > 0
                        return i64(sent_total)
                    return i64(-EIO)
                if reply[0] == 2
                    // SO_SNDTIMEO expired while parked.  Linux returns
                    // EAGAIN (not ETIMEDOUT) for SO_SNDTIMEO on TCP
                    // writes; honour partial progress when present.
                    if sent_total > 0
                        return i64(sent_total)
                    return i64(-EAGAIN)
                if reply[0] != 0
                    if sent_total > 0
                        return i64(sent_total)
                    // Connection torn down — consult pending_error so
                    // TCP_USER_TIMEOUT / keepalive-giveup / RST surface
                    // as the right errno (ETIMEDOUT / ECONNRESET / …)
                    // instead of a generic EPIPE.  Linux delivers the
                    // recorded sk_err on the next syscall and clears
                    // it; matching that here keeps Node-style "see
                    // ETIMEDOUT once on the next send" behaviour.
                    var perr = 0
                    val ger = shim_inet_get_tcp_err(inet_fd, perr)
                    if ger == 0
                        if perr != 0
                            return i64(-perr)
                    return i64(-EPIPE)
                val n = shim_read_u16(reply, 1)
                if n <= 0
                    // Phase 1: blocking TCP_SEND now parks in inet
                    // until send_buf has room, then replies (0,0) as
                    // a wake — so the next loop iteration retries
                    // the same chunk against the freshly-opened
                    // window.  NB callers still get -EAGAIN if
                    // nothing has been queued yet, otherwise the
                    // partial count.
                    if (posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK) != 0
                        if sent_total == 0
                            return i64(-EAGAIN)
                        return i64(sent_total)
                else
                    sent_total += n
            return i64(sent_total)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FILE
            return shim_vfs_write(posix_fd_target(fd), buf, len)
        if k == POSIX_FD_EVENTFD
            // Linux requires len >= 8.
            if len < 8 || buf == i64(0)
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var add = i64(0)
            var bi = 0
            while bi < 8
                add = add | (i64(int(ubuf[bi]) & 0xff) << (bi * 8))
                bi += 1
            // Linux rejects the value u64::MAX (-1 as i64) — that
            // bit pattern is reserved as "invalid".
            if add == i64(-1)
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            val pid_w = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val widx = pid_w * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
            // Counter is unsigned u64-1 max; we treat i64 sign as
            // "high bit set" → near-overflow.  Accept the add if
            // current + add < 2^64 - 1.  Practically: detect
            // overflow on signed addition by checking whether the
            // sum wraps below either operand (works because we
            // never exceed i64::MAX before this point).
            val cur = posix_fd_table[widx].eventfd_count
            val sum = cur + add
            // Overflow detection — same operands both unsigned-wise.
            if u64(sum) < u64(cur)
                if (posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK) != 0
                    return i64(-EAGAIN)
                // Blocking mode: slix has no inter-thread notify
                // path that wakes a write blocked on counter
                // headroom yet, so return -EAGAIN even in blocking
                // mode (matches musl when no waiter exists). Real
                // behaviour for the threaded "writer waits for
                // reader" case lands with the inter-thread notify
                // chunk.
                return i64(-EAGAIN)
            posix_fd_table[widx].eventfd_count = sum
            return i64(8)
        i64(-EBADF)

SYS_READ. STDIN returns 0 (EOF) — no kernel-side keyboard path
yet; TTY routing is on the bridge memory's todo list. TCP sockets
block in the inet server until data arrives or peer FINs. Write-
only STDIO kinds and UDP (which should use recvfrom) are rejected.
File-kind reads (`POSIX_FD_FILE`) forward to VFS, which dispatches
on its OFT type — files seek server-side, pipes block, TTY
handles read from the TTY driver.

    sys_read(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_STDIN
            // Route through the TTY server. Currently every slix
            // process is bound to console 0 — multi-tty isn't a
            // thing yet. Returns 1 byte per call (TTY_CMD_READ
            // hands back one char); musl's read loops as needed.
            // O_NONBLOCK pre-checks via TTY_CMD_POLL and returns
            // -EAGAIN if no byte is queued, so a non-blocking
            // stdin read never sits inside TTY_CMD_READ's blocking
            // path. Blocking mode falls through to TTY_CMD_READ
            // which parks the caller on the tty server's
            // pending_reader slot until input arrives.
            if len <= 0
                return i64(0)
            if buf == i64(0)
                return i64(-EFAULT)
            val port = tty_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-ENODEV)
            if (posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK) != 0
                var pmsg: [2]byte
                pmsg[0] = byte(TTY_CMD_POLL_C)
                pmsg[1] = byte(0)
                var preply: [7]byte
                val pr = ipc_send(port, pmsg, 2, preply, 7)
                if pr < 0
                    return i64(-EIO)
                if preply[0] != 0
                    return i64(-EIO)
                val pbits = (int(preply[1]) & 0xff) | ((int(preply[2]) & 0xff) << 8)
                if (pbits & POSIX_EPOLLIN) == 0
                    return i64(-EAGAIN)
            var msg: [2]byte
            msg[0] = byte(TTY_CMD_READ_C)
            msg[1] = byte(0)
            var reply: [2]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 2)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            if reply[0] != 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            ubuf[0] = reply[1]
            return i64(1)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            val pidr = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val styr = int(posix_fd_table[pidr * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].pos)
            if styr == POSIX_SOCK_DGRAM
                return sys_recvfrom_unix_dgram(fd, buf, len, 0, i64(0), i64(0))
            return sys_recv_unix_stream(fd, buf, len, 0)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if inet_fd < 0
                return i64(-ENOTCONN)
            if len <= 0
                return i64(0)
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-ENODEV)
            var cap_len = len
            if cap_len > INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
                cap_len = INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
            val nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
            var msg: [5]byte
            if nb != 0
                msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_RECV_NB)
            else
                msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_RECV)
            shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
            shim_write_u16(msg, 3, cap_len)
            var reply: [540]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 540)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            if nb != 0
                val raw = int(reply[0]) & 0xff
                if raw != 0
                    if raw >= 128
                        return i64(raw - 256)
                    return i64(raw)
            if reply[0] != 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            val rlen = shim_read_u16(reply, 1)
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var i = 0
            while i < rlen
                ubuf[i] = reply[3 + i]
                i += 1
            return i64(rlen)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FILE
            return shim_vfs_read(posix_fd_target(fd), buf, len)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TIMERFD
            if len < 8 || buf == i64(0)
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            val pid_t = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val tidx = pid_t * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
            timerfd_advance(tidx)
            val cnt = posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations
            if cnt == i64(0)
                if (posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK) != 0
                    return i64(-EAGAIN)
                // Blocking read on timerfd needs an in-shim sleep
                // loop until the next deadline. Simpler than the
                // socket case since we know exactly when to wake.
                // Loop: while expirations==0, sleep_or_notify until
                // next_tick. notify_wait() if disarmed (would block
                // forever).
                while posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations == i64(0)
                    if posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick == 0
                        // Disarmed timer in blocking mode: Linux blocks
                        // forever waiting for someone else to settime.
                        // Slix has no concurrent settime path yet, so
                        // EAGAIN here matches the practical behaviour.
                        return i64(-EAGAIN)
                    val now_b = int(uptime())
                    val wait_ticks = posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick - now_b
                    if wait_ticks > 0
                        sleep_or_notify(wait_ticks)
                    timerfd_advance(tidx)
            val deliver = posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations = i64(0)
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var bi = 0
            while bi < 8
                ubuf[bi] = byte(int((deliver >> i64(bi * 8))) & 0xff)
                bi += 1
            return i64(8)
        if k == POSIX_FD_EVENTFD
            if len < 8 || buf == i64(0)
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            val pid_r = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val ridx = pid_r * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
            val cur = posix_fd_table[ridx].eventfd_count
            if cur == i64(0)
                if (posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK) != 0
                    return i64(-EAGAIN)
                // Blocking semantics: see write-side note above —
                // slix doesn't yet have an inter-thread notify
                // for eventfd, so return -EAGAIN in blocking mode
                // too. Single-threaded user code that writes
                // before reading hits the cur != 0 path naturally.
                return i64(-EAGAIN)
            // Semaphore mode hands back 1 per read; default mode
            // returns the full counter and resets to 0.
            var deliver = cur
            var remain = i64(0)
            if posix_fd_table[ridx].eventfd_semaphore != 0
                deliver = i64(1)
                remain = cur - i64(1)
            posix_fd_table[ridx].eventfd_count = remain
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var bi = 0
            while bi < 8
                ubuf[bi] = byte(int((deliver >> i64(bi * 8))) & 0xff)
                bi += 1
            return i64(8)
        i64(-EBADF)

VFS read/write helpers. Both create a grant against the user's
buffer (granter_pid = current pid), send the appropriate VFS
command, and revoke the grant on the way out. The shim is in
kernel mode with the user's PT active, so `u64(buf)` is a valid
user vaddr that VFS can resolve via `svc_grant_map`. Length is
unbounded — VFS streams as much as it has buffered and reports
the actual count.

    shim_vfs_read(handle: int, buf: i64, len: int) -> i64
        if len <= 0
            return i64(0)
        if handle < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val gid = kernel_grant_create_inline(pid, u64(buf), len, SHIM_GRANT_WRITE)
        if gid < 0
            return i64(-ENOMEM)
        var msg: [13]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_READ_C)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 1, handle)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 5, len)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 9, gid)
        var reply: [5]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 13, reply, 5)
        kernel_grant_revoke_inline(pid, gid)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        i64(shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1))

    shim_vfs_write(handle: int, buf: i64, len: int) -> i64
        if len <= 0
            return i64(0)
        if handle < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val gid = kernel_grant_create_inline(pid, u64(buf), len, SHIM_GRANT_READ)
        if gid < 0
            return i64(-ENOMEM)
        var msg: [13]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_WRITE_C)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 1, handle)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 5, len)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 9, gid)
        var reply: [5]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 13, reply, 5)
        kernel_grant_revoke_inline(pid, gid)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        i64(shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1))

SYS_CLOSE. For socket kinds, tell the inet server to drop the
backend record; the fd slot is cleared either way so a subsequent
op returns -EBADF. Pre-connect / pre-listen TCP sockets (target
still -1) skip the inet call — no backend was ever allocated.

    sys_close(fd: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        // If this fd was duped, another slot still owns the inet
        // record — clear our slot but leave the backend alone.
        val has_dup = posix_fd_other_dup_exists(fd, k, target)
        if has_dup == 0
            if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
                val port = inet_get_port()
                if port >= 0
                    var msg: [2]byte
                    msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_CLOSE)
                    msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
                    var reply: [1]byte
                    ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 1)
            if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                if target >= 0
                    val port = inet_get_port()
                    if port >= 0
                        // SO_LINGER l_onoff=1 + l_linger=0: send RST
                        // instead of FIN (Linux's "abort close"). Any
                        // other linger configuration falls through to
                        // the standard FIN close — flush-or-timeout
                        // semantics aren't enforced yet.
                        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
                        val cidx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                        val linger_on = posix_fd_table[cidx].linger_on
                        val linger_secs = posix_fd_table[cidx].linger_secs
                        var msg: [3]byte
                        if linger_on != 0 && linger_secs == 0
                            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_ABORT_CLOSE)
                        else
                            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_CLOSE)
                        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, target)
                        var reply: [1]byte
                        ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 1)
            if k == POSIX_FD_FILE
                val port = vfs_get_port()
                if port >= 0
                    var msg: [5]byte
                    msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C)
                    shim_write_be_i32(msg, 1, target)
                    var reply: [1]byte
                    ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 1)
            if k == POSIX_FD_EPOLL
                epoll_inst_close_all(target)
                epoll_inst_release(target)
            if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
                shim_unix_close(target)
        posix_fd_clear(fd)
        i64(0)

SYS_SOCKET. Supports AF_INET + SOCK_DGRAM/SOCK_STREAM. Datagram
sockets eagerly ask inet for a UDP slot. Stream sockets return an
unconnected POSIX fd (target = -1); the inet-side socket is
allocated lazily by `sys_connect` or `sys_listen` so we don't have
to map a "pre-connect" state into the inet protocol.

`stype` may be OR'd with `SOCK_CLOEXEC` and/or `SOCK_NONBLOCK` —
musl's resolver creates its DNS socket as
`SOCK_DGRAM|SOCK_CLOEXEC|SOCK_NONBLOCK`, mirroring the same
atomic creation pattern as `accept4`. We mask the flag bits off
before the `stype` discriminator and re-apply `SOCK_NONBLOCK` to
the new fd's flags. `SOCK_CLOEXEC` is accepted-and-ignored (no
exec yet).

    sys_socket(family: int, stype: int, proto: int) -> i64
        if family == POSIX_AF_UNIX
            return sys_socket_unix(stype)
        if family != POSIX_AF_INET
            return i64(-EAFNOSUPPORT)
        val sock_flag_mask = POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK | POSIX_SOCK_CLOEXEC
        val sock_flags = stype & sock_flag_mask
        val base_type = stype & ~sock_flag_mask
        if base_type == POSIX_SOCK_DGRAM
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-ENODEV)
            var msg: [4]byte
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_SOCKET)
            msg[1] = byte(family & 0xff)
            msg[2] = byte(base_type & 0xff)
            msg[3] = byte(proto & 0xff)
            var reply: [2]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 2)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            // inet rejects with status != 0 either when the global
            // pool is exhausted or when the per-tid cap is hit.
            // Both surfaces map to -EMFILE.
            if reply[0] != 0
                return i64(-EMFILE)
            val inet_fd = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
            val posix_fd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET, inet_fd)
            if posix_fd < 0
                var close_msg: [2]byte
                close_msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_CLOSE)
                close_msg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
                var close_rep: [1]byte
                ipc_send(port, close_msg, 2, close_rep, 1)
                return i64(-EMFILE)
            if (sock_flags & POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK) != 0
                val cur = posix_fd_flags(posix_fd)
                posix_fd_set_flags(posix_fd, cur | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
            return i64(posix_fd)
        if base_type == POSIX_SOCK_STREAM
            val posix_fd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET, -1)
            if posix_fd < 0
                return i64(-EMFILE)
            if (sock_flags & POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK) != 0
                val cur = posix_fd_flags(posix_fd)
                posix_fd_set_flags(posix_fd, cur | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
            return i64(posix_fd)
        i64(-EPROTONOSUPPORT)


AF_UNIX socket family
---------------------

The unix server (oskit/servers/unix.lsysl) is a self-contained
path-keyed registry — no NIC, no filesystem integration. Every
AF_UNIX socket maps to a single server-side slot identified by
a small integer (`target`). Both DGRAM and STREAM allocate the
slot eagerly at `socket(2)` time, unlike the AF_INET TCP path
which defers allocation to listen/connect.

`sockaddr_un` layout (Linux):
  offset 0..1 : sa_family (u16 native, AF_UNIX = 1)
  offset 2..  : sun_path (NUL-terminated C string, ≤108 bytes)

For now the path is interpreted as a C-string starting at
offset 2: bytes are taken up to the first NUL or to addrlen-2,
whichever comes first. Linux abstract-namespace (leading-NUL)
support is deferred — slix's registry is already an abstract
namespace, so a leading NUL just confuses key matching.

    sys_socket_unix(stype: int) -> i64
        val sock_flag_mask = POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK | POSIX_SOCK_CLOEXEC
        val sock_flags = stype & sock_flag_mask
        val base_type = stype & ~sock_flag_mask
        if base_type != POSIX_SOCK_DGRAM
            if base_type != POSIX_SOCK_STREAM
                return i64(-EPROTONOSUPPORT)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [2]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_SOCKET)
        msg[1] = byte(base_type & 0xff)
        var reply: [2]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 2)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(unix_status(reply[0]))
        val unix_fd = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        val posix_fd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET, unix_fd)
        if posix_fd < 0
            var close_msg: [2]byte
            close_msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_CLOSE)
            close_msg[1] = byte(unix_fd & 0xff)
            var close_rep: [1]byte
            ipc_send(port, close_msg, 2, close_rep, 1)
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        // Stash stype in pos so subsequent helpers can route DGRAM
        // vs STREAM without a second IPC.
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + posix_fd].pos = i64(base_type)
        if (sock_flags & POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK) != 0
            val cur = posix_fd_flags(posix_fd)
            posix_fd_set_flags(posix_fd, cur | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
        i64(posix_fd)


sys_socketpair for AF_UNIX SOCK_STREAM. Asks the unix server to
allocate two pre-connected slots, then parks each in a fresh
posix-fd slot and writes the pair into `sv_ptr[0..1]` (an
`int[2]` in user memory, matching musl's `socketpair(int sv[2])`
prototype).

`type` may carry `SOCK_NONBLOCK` / `SOCK_CLOEXEC` flags; we mask
them off, propagate `O_NONBLOCK` to both new fds, and accept-and
-ignore CLOEXEC. STREAM and DGRAM are both supported; the server
links the two slots via `peer_idx` and the DGRAM SENDTO path
treats `plen == 0` as "default to peer_idx". `-EAFNOSUPPORT`
for non-AF_UNIX domains (no AF_INET socketpair — Linux itself
returns EOPNOTSUPP there too).

If allocating either posix-fd slot fails, the unix-server slots
are reclaimed via `shim_unix_close` so the server doesn't leak.

    sys_socketpair(family: int, stype: int, proto: int,
            sv_ptr: i64) -> i64
        if family != POSIX_AF_UNIX
            return i64(-EAFNOSUPPORT)
        if sv_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val sock_flag_mask = POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK | POSIX_SOCK_CLOEXEC
        val sock_flags = stype & sock_flag_mask
        val base_type = stype & ~sock_flag_mask
        if base_type != POSIX_SOCK_STREAM
            if base_type != POSIX_SOCK_DGRAM
                return i64(-EOPNOTSUPP)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [2]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_SOCKETPAIR)
        msg[1] = byte(base_type & 0xff)
        var reply: [3]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 3)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(unix_status(reply[0]))
        val ufd0 = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        val ufd1 = int(reply[2]) & 0xff
        val pfd0 = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET, ufd0)
        if pfd0 < 0
            shim_unix_close(ufd0)
            shim_unix_close(ufd1)
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        val pfd1 = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET, ufd1)
        if pfd1 < 0
            sys_close(pfd0)
            shim_unix_close(ufd1)
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + pfd0].pos = i64(base_type)
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + pfd1].pos = i64(base_type)
        if (sock_flags & POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK) != 0
            val cur0 = posix_fd_flags(pfd0)
            posix_fd_set_flags(pfd0, cur0 | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
            val cur1 = posix_fd_flags(pfd1)
            posix_fd_set_flags(pfd1, cur1 | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
        var fdp: *int = *int(sv_ptr)
        fdp[0] = pfd0
        fdp[1] = pfd1
        i64(0)


Read sun_path into `out` and return the effective length.
addrlen ≤ 2 → -1.

Two flavors:

  - **Abstract namespace** (Linux): sun_path[0] is NUL and the
    rest of the bytes up to addrlen are the key (no NUL
    terminator inside; embedded NULs are part of the name).
    We copy the leading NUL plus the remaining bytes into
    `out`, so two abstract sockets named `\0foo` and `\0fo` are
    distinct keys, and the abstract namespace is automatically
    disjoint from filesystem-style keys (which never start
    with NUL).
  - **Filesystem path**: sun_path[0] is non-NUL and the bytes
    up to the first NUL form the key (C-string convention).

Returns -1 if the parsed key is empty or exceeds
UNIX_PATH_MAX.

    shim_unix_read_path(addr_ptr: i64, addrlen: int,
            out: *byte, out_cap: int) -> int
        if addrlen < 3
            return -1
        val src: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
        var avail = addrlen - 2
        if avail > out_cap
            avail = out_cap
        if avail > UNIX_PATH_MAX
            avail = UNIX_PATH_MAX
        if avail <= 0
            return -1
        if src[2] == byte(0)
            // Abstract namespace: copy leading NUL + body verbatim
            // (no NUL-terminator scan; embedded NULs are name bytes).
            var n = 0
            while n < avail
                out[n] = src[2 + n]
                n += 1
            if n <= 1
                return -1
            return n
        var n = 0
        var done = 0
        while done == 0
            if n >= avail
                done = 1
            else
                val b = src[2 + n]
                if b == byte(0)
                    done = 1
                else
                    out[n] = b
                    n += 1
        if n <= 0
            return -1
        n


Issue UNIX_CMD_CLOSE for `target`.

    shim_unix_close(target: int)
        if target < 0
            return
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [2]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_CLOSE)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 1)


sys_bind for AF_UNIX. Sends the path key to the unix server.

    sys_bind_unix(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen: int) -> i64
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if target < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        var path: [108]byte
        val plen = shim_unix_read_path(addr_ptr, addrlen, &path[0], 108)
        if plen <= 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [128]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_BIND)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(plen & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < plen
            msg[3 + i] = path[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 3 + plen, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            val status = unix_status(reply[0])
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(status))
            return i64(status)
        i64(0)


sys_listen for AF_UNIX. STREAM only.

    sys_listen_unix(fd: int, backlog: int) -> i64
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if target < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var bl = backlog
        if bl < 0
            bl = 0
        if bl > 0xff
            bl = 0xff
        var msg: [3]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_LISTEN)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(bl & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(unix_status(reply[0]))
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].is_listen = 1
        i64(0)


sys_accept for AF_UNIX. STREAM only. Allocates a fresh
posix fd backed by the new server-end slot. If the listener fd
has `O_NONBLOCK` set, switches to `UNIX_CMD_ACCEPT_NB` so an
empty backlog returns -EAGAIN instead of parking.

    sys_accept_unix(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64) -> i64
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if target < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        val nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        var msg: [2]byte
        if nb != 0
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_ACCEPT_NB)
        else
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_ACCEPT)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        var reply: [128]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 128)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(unix_status(reply[0]))
        val server_end = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        val plen = int(reply[2]) & 0xff
        val new_posix_fd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET, server_end)
        if new_posix_fd < 0
            shim_unix_close(server_end)
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + new_posix_fd].pos = i64(POSIX_SOCK_STREAM)

The reply carries `plen + path[]` from the connector's bound
path (or `plen=0` if the connector was unbound). Fill the
caller's sockaddr_un with `family(2) + path[plen] + NUL` —
matching Linux's "len includes the NUL when the path is
non-empty" convention, and leave `addrlen = 2` for an
unbound connector so callers can distinguish.

        if addr_ptr != i64(0)
            val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
            a[0] = byte(POSIX_AF_UNIX & 0xff)
            a[1] = byte(0)
            var pcopy = plen
            if pcopy > UNIX_PATH_MAX
                pcopy = UNIX_PATH_MAX
            var i = 0
            while i < pcopy
                a[2 + i] = reply[3 + i]
                i += 1
            if 2 + pcopy < 110
                a[2 + pcopy] = byte(0)
        if addrlen_ptr != i64(0)
            val alp: *i32 = *i32(addrlen_ptr)
            if plen == 0
                *alp = i32(2)
            else
                *alp = i32(2 + plen + 1)
        i64(new_posix_fd)


sys_connect for AF_UNIX. Routes STREAM and DGRAM connect()
(both reuse UNIX_CMD_CONNECT — the server branches on stype).

If `sun_family == AF_UNSPEC`, this is a Linux dissolve: clear
the DGRAM socket's default peer via UNIX_CMD_DISCONNECT, so a
subsequent `send()` (no addr) fails with -ENOTCONN while
explicit `sendto(addr)` keeps working.

    sys_connect_unix(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen: int) -> i64
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if target < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if addrlen < 2
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if addr_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val sa: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
        val fam = (int(sa[0]) & 0xff) | ((int(sa[1]) & 0xff) << 8)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        if fam == POSIX_AF_UNSPEC
            var dmsg: [2]byte
            dmsg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_DISCONNECT)
            dmsg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
            var dreply: [1]byte
            val dr = ipc_send(port, dmsg, 2, dreply, 1)
            if dr < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            if dreply[0] != 0
                val status = unix_status(dreply[0])
                posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(status))
                return i64(status)
            return i64(0)
        var path: [108]byte
        val plen = shim_unix_read_path(addr_ptr, addrlen, &path[0], 108)
        if plen <= 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        var msg: [128]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_CONNECT)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(plen & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < plen
            msg[3 + i] = path[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 3 + plen, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            val status = unix_status(reply[0])
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(status))
            return i64(status)
        i64(0)


DGRAM sendto for AF_UNIX.

    sys_sendto_unix_dgram(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int,
            addr_ptr: i64, addrlen: int) -> i64
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if target < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if len < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        var dlen = len
        if dlen > 256
            dlen = 256
        var path: [108]byte
        var plen = 0
        if addr_ptr != i64(0)
            plen = shim_unix_read_path(addr_ptr, addrlen, &path[0], 108)
            if plen <= 0
                return i64(-EINVAL)

`addr_ptr == NULL` is the "no destination" form used by
`send(2)` / `write(2)` on a connected DGRAM socket
(socketpair pairs, or any future `connect(2)` on DGRAM).
We forward `plen == 0` to the unix server and let it route
via `peer_idx` (returns -ENOTCONN if unset).

        val data: *byte = *byte(buf)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [512]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_SENDTO)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(plen & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < plen
            msg[3 + i] = path[i]
            i += 1
        msg[3 + plen]     = byte((dlen >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[3 + plen + 1] = byte(dlen & 0xff)
        i = 0
        while i < dlen
            msg[3 + plen + 2 + i] = data[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [3]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 3 + plen + 2 + dlen, reply, 3)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            val status = unix_status(reply[0])
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(status))
            return i64(status)
        val nsent = ((int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2]) & 0xff)
        i64(nsent)


STREAM send for AF_UNIX. Used by both `sendto` (with NULL
addr) and `write` on a STREAM unix fd.

    sys_send_unix_stream(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int) -> i64
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if target < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if len < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        var dlen = len
        if dlen > 2046
            dlen = 2046
        val data: *byte = *byte(buf)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [2050]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_SEND)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte((dlen >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[3] = byte(dlen & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < dlen
            msg[4 + i] = data[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [3]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4 + dlen, reply, 3)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            val status = unix_status(reply[0])
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(status))
            return i64(status)
        val nsent = ((int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2]) & 0xff)
        i64(nsent)


DGRAM recvfrom for AF_UNIX. If the fd is `O_NONBLOCK` (or
`MSG_DONTWAIT` is in flags) and the queue is empty, returns
-EAGAIN via `UNIX_CMD_RECVFROM_NB`.

    sys_recvfrom_unix_dgram(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int, flags: int,
            addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64) -> i64
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if target < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        var maxlen = len
        if maxlen < 0
            maxlen = 0
        if maxlen > 256
            maxlen = 256
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        if (flags & POSIX_MSG_DONTWAIT) != 0
            nb = POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        var msg: [4]byte
        if nb != 0
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_RECVFROM_NB)
        else
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_RECVFROM)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte((maxlen >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[3] = byte(maxlen & 0xff)
        var reply: [512]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 512)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            val status = unix_status(reply[0])
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(status))
            return i64(status)
        val plen = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        val dlen = ((int(reply[2 + plen]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2 + plen + 1]) & 0xff)
        var dcopy = dlen
        if dcopy > maxlen
            dcopy = maxlen
        val dst: *byte = *byte(buf)
        var i = 0
        while i < dcopy
            dst[i] = reply[2 + plen + 2 + i]
            i += 1
        if addr_ptr != i64(0)
            val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
            a[0] = byte(POSIX_AF_UNIX & 0xff)
            a[1] = byte(0)
            var pcopy = plen
            if pcopy > UNIX_PATH_MAX
                pcopy = UNIX_PATH_MAX
            i = 0
            while i < pcopy
                a[2 + i] = reply[2 + i]
                i += 1
            // NUL-terminate within bounds for C-string callers.
            if 2 + pcopy < 110
                a[2 + pcopy] = byte(0)
        if addrlen_ptr != i64(0)
            val alp: *i32 = *i32(addrlen_ptr)
            *alp = i32(2 + plen)
        i64(dcopy)


STREAM recv for AF_UNIX. Used by `read` (flags=0) and
`recvfrom`/`recvmsg` (flags forwarded). If the fd is
`O_NONBLOCK` (or `MSG_DONTWAIT` is in flags) and rx_buf is
empty *and* the peer is still alive, switches to
`UNIX_CMD_RECV_NB` so the server returns -EAGAIN instead of
parking. EOF (peer closed, no data) still returns 0.

    sys_recv_unix_stream(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int, flags: int) -> i64
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if target < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        var maxlen = len
        if maxlen < 0
            maxlen = 0
        if maxlen > 2046
            maxlen = 2046
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        if (flags & POSIX_MSG_DONTWAIT) != 0
            nb = POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        var msg: [4]byte
        if nb != 0
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_RECV_NB)
        else
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_RECV)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte((maxlen >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[3] = byte(maxlen & 0xff)
        var reply: [2050]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 2050)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            val status = unix_status(reply[0])
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(status))
            return i64(status)
        val dlen = ((int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2]) & 0xff)
        var dcopy = dlen
        if dcopy > maxlen
            dcopy = maxlen
        val dst: *byte = *byte(buf)
        var i = 0
        while i < dcopy
            dst[i] = reply[3 + i]
            i += 1
        i64(dcopy)


SCM_RIGHTS sendmsg path. Walks the user's `msg_control`
buffer for a single SOL_SOCKET/SCM_RIGHTS cmsg and resolves
each fd into a unix-server slot index. Other cmsg types
are silently skipped (matches Linux: unknown cmsgs aren't
an error). Returns the number of slots written into
`slots[]` on success, -1 on parse error or if any fd isn't
a unix socket.

    shim_unix_extract_scm_rights(ctrl_ptr: i64, ctrl_len: int,
            slots: *byte) -> int
        if ctrl_ptr == i64(0)
            return 0
        if ctrl_len < SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN
            return 0
        val cm: *byte = *byte(ctrl_ptr)
        var off = 0
        var nfds = 0
        while off + SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN <= ctrl_len
            val cmsg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(cm, off))
            if cmsg_len < SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN
                return -1
            if off + cmsg_len > ctrl_len
                return -1
            val cmsg_level = shim_read_i32(cm, off + 8)
            val cmsg_type  = shim_read_i32(cm, off + 12)
            if cmsg_level == SHIM_SOL_SOCKET
                if cmsg_type == SHIM_SCM_RIGHTS
                    val fd_count = (cmsg_len - SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN) / 4
                    var i = 0
                    while i < fd_count
                        if nfds >= UNIX_SCM_MAX_FDS
                            return -1
                        val user_fd = shim_read_i32(cm,
                            off + SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN + i * 4)
                        val k = posix_fd_kind(user_fd)
                        if k != POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
                            return -1
                        val slot_idx = posix_fd_target(user_fd)
                        slots[nfds] = byte(slot_idx & 0xff)
                        nfds += 1
                        i += 1
            // Advance to next cmsg, aligned to 8 bytes (sizeof size_t).
            var advance = cmsg_len
            advance = (advance + 7) & (~7)
            off += advance
        nfds


sys_sendmsg helper for AF_UNIX. Gathers the iovec into a
single buffer, parses any SCM_RIGHTS cmsg for fd-passing,
and dispatches:
- DGRAM: UNIX_CMD_SENDMSG with path + nfds + slots + payload.
- STREAM with no fds: falls through to sys_write (existing
  UNIX_CMD_SEND path).
- STREAM with fds: returns -EOPNOTSUPP — Session 2 wires
  SCM_RIGHTS through DGRAM only.

    sys_sendmsg_unix(fd: int, msghdr_ptr: i64, flags: int) -> i64
        if msghdr_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val mh: *byte = *byte(msghdr_ptr)
        val name_ptr = shim_read_ptr(mh, 0)
        val name_len = shim_read_i32(mh, 8)
        val iov_ptr  = shim_read_ptr(mh, 16)
        val iov_len  = shim_read_i32(mh, 24)
        val ctrl_ptr = shim_read_ptr(mh, 32)
        val ctrl_len = shim_read_i32(mh, 40)
        if iov_len < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iov_len > SHIM_IOV_MAX
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iov_len > 0
            if iov_ptr == i64(0)
                return i64(-EFAULT)
        var slots: [4]byte
        val nfds = shim_unix_extract_scm_rights(ctrl_ptr, ctrl_len,
            &slots[0])
        if nfds < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val stype = int(posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].pos)
        if stype == POSIX_SOCK_STREAM
            if nfds == 0
                // No cmsg → fall back to plain stream write.
                var gather_p: [2048]byte
                var gtotal_p = 0
                val iov_base_p: *byte = *byte(iov_ptr)
                var ii_p = 0
                while ii_p < iov_len
                    val ent_off_p = ii_p * 16
                    val base_p    = shim_read_ptr(iov_base_p, ent_off_p)
                    val seg_len_p = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base_p, ent_off_p + 8))
                    if seg_len_p > 0
                        if base_p == i64(0)
                            return i64(-EFAULT)
                        val src_p: *byte = *byte(base_p)
                        var jj_p = 0
                        while jj_p < seg_len_p
                            if gtotal_p < 2046
                                gather_p[gtotal_p] = src_p[jj_p]
                                gtotal_p += 1
                            jj_p += 1
                    ii_p += 1
                return sys_send_unix_stream(fd, i64(&gather_p[0]), gtotal_p)
            // STREAM with cmsg → UNIX_CMD_SENDMSG, plen=0 (target is
            // peer; path unused). Cap data at 256 to match the
            // DGRAM SENDMSG payload budget.
            var gather_s: [256]byte
            var gtotal_s = 0
            val iov_base_s: *byte = *byte(iov_ptr)
            var ii_s = 0
            while ii_s < iov_len
                val ent_off_s = ii_s * 16
                val base_s    = shim_read_ptr(iov_base_s, ent_off_s)
                val seg_len_s = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base_s, ent_off_s + 8))
                if seg_len_s > 0
                    if base_s == i64(0)
                        return i64(-EFAULT)
                    val src_s: *byte = *byte(base_s)
                    var jj_s = 0
                    while jj_s < seg_len_s
                        if gtotal_s < 256
                            gather_s[gtotal_s] = src_s[jj_s]
                            gtotal_s += 1
                        jj_s += 1
                ii_s += 1
            val target_s = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if target_s < 0
                return i64(-EBADF)
            val port_s = unix_get_port()
            if port_s < 0
                return i64(-ENODEV)
            var msg_s: [512]byte
            msg_s[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_SENDMSG)
            msg_s[1] = byte(target_s & 0xff)
            msg_s[2] = byte(0)
            msg_s[3] = byte(nfds & 0xff)
            var i_s = 0
            while i_s < nfds
                msg_s[4 + i_s] = slots[i_s]
                i_s += 1
            val slots_off_s = 4 + nfds
            msg_s[slots_off_s]     = byte((gtotal_s >> 8) & 0xff)
            msg_s[slots_off_s + 1] = byte(gtotal_s & 0xff)
            i_s = 0
            while i_s < gtotal_s
                msg_s[slots_off_s + 2 + i_s] = gather_s[i_s]
                i_s += 1
            var reply_s: [3]byte
            val rs = ipc_send(port_s, msg_s, slots_off_s + 2 + gtotal_s,
                reply_s, 3)
            if rs < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            if reply_s[0] != 0
                val status_s = unix_status(reply_s[0])
                posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(status_s))
                return i64(status_s)
            val ns_hi = (int(reply_s[1]) & 0xff) << 8
            val ns_lo = int(reply_s[2]) & 0xff
            val nsent_s = ns_hi | ns_lo
            return i64(nsent_s)

DGRAM path: name is required, gather iovec, build the
SENDMSG wire message.

        if name_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        var path: [108]byte
        val plen = shim_unix_read_path(name_ptr, name_len, &path[0], 108)
        if plen <= 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        var gather: [256]byte
        var total = 0
        val iov_base: *byte = *byte(iov_ptr)
        var ii = 0
        while ii < iov_len
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val base    = shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off)
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len > 0
                if base == i64(0)
                    return i64(-EFAULT)
                val src: *byte = *byte(base)
                var jj = 0
                while jj < seg_len
                    if total < 256
                        gather[total] = src[jj]
                        total += 1
                    jj += 1
            ii += 1
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if target < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)

Wire layout: cmd(1) + fd(1) + plen(1) + path(P) + nfds(1)
+ slots(F) + dlen_be(2) + data(D). For our caps (path 108
+ 4 fds + 256 data) the worst case is 374 bytes — round to
512 for headroom, matching the server's UNIX_MSG_BUF.

        var msg: [512]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_SENDMSG)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(plen & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < plen
            msg[3 + i] = path[i]
            i += 1
        msg[3 + plen] = byte(nfds & 0xff)
        i = 0
        while i < nfds
            msg[3 + plen + 1 + i] = slots[i]
            i += 1
        val slots_off = 3 + plen + 1 + nfds
        msg[slots_off]     = byte((total >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[slots_off + 1] = byte(total & 0xff)
        i = 0
        while i < total
            msg[slots_off + 2 + i] = gather[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [3]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, slots_off + 2 + total, reply, 3)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            val status = unix_status(reply[0])
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(status))
            return i64(status)
        val nsent = ((int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2]) & 0xff)
        i64(nsent)


STREAM recvmsg helper. Reuses the same UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG wire
as DGRAM (server's `unix_handle_recvmsg_stream` returns plen=0
in the reply since STREAM has no per-message src). Allocates
a posix_fd per transferred slot with `pos = POSIX_SOCK_STREAM`
so future ops on the inherited fd dispatch as STREAM.

A zero-byte reply (`status=0, plen=0, nfds=0, dlen=0`)
indicates clean EOF (peer closed or `shut_wr`). Returns 0 to
the caller; iovec / cmsg / namelen left untouched.

    sys_recvmsg_unix_stream(fd: int, mh: *byte, ctrl_ptr: i64,
            ctrl_len: int, iov_ptr: i64, iov_len: int,
            flags: int) -> i64
        var max_nfds = 0
        if ctrl_ptr != i64(0)
            if ctrl_len >= SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN
                max_nfds = (ctrl_len - SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN) / 4
                if max_nfds > UNIX_SCM_MAX_FDS
                    max_nfds = UNIX_SCM_MAX_FDS
                if max_nfds < 0
                    max_nfds = 0
        var cap = 0
        val iov_base: *byte = *byte(iov_ptr)
        var ii = 0
        while ii < iov_len
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len > 0
                cap += seg_len
            ii += 1
        if cap > 2046
            cap = 2046
        if cap < 0
            cap = 0
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if target < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        if (flags & POSIX_MSG_DONTWAIT) != 0
            nb = POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        var msg: [5]byte
        if nb != 0
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG_NB)
        else
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(max_nfds & 0xff)
        msg[3] = byte((cap >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[4] = byte(cap & 0xff)
        var reply: [2070]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 2070)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            val status = unix_status(reply[0])
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(status))
            return i64(status)

Reply layout: status(1) + plen(1)=0 + nfds(1) + slots(F) +
stypes(F) + dlen_be(2) + data(D). plen is always 0 on
STREAM. stypes carries the server-side stype of each
transferred slot so the new posix_fd's `pos` matches.

        val src_plen = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        val nfds_in_reply = int(reply[2 + src_plen]) & 0xff
        val slots_off = 2 + src_plen + 1
        val stypes_off = slots_off + nfds_in_reply
        val dl_off = stypes_off + nfds_in_reply
        val dl_hi = (int(reply[dl_off]) & 0xff) << 8
        val dl_lo = int(reply[dl_off + 1]) & 0xff
        val dlen = dl_hi | dl_lo
        val data_off = dl_off + 2

Allocate posix_fds for each transferred slot; mark them
with the per-slot stype the server reported. Same two-pass
dedup-on-close pattern as the DGRAM recvmsg path.

        var new_fds: [4]int
        var n_new = 0
        var nf = 0
        while nf < nfds_in_reply
            val sidx = int(reply[slots_off + nf]) & 0xff
            val sst  = int(reply[stypes_off + nf]) & 0xff
            val pfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET, sidx)
            if pfd >= 0
                val pid2 = posix_fd_current_pid()
                posix_fd_table[pid2 * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + pfd].pos = i64(sst)
                new_fds[n_new] = pfd
                n_new += 1
            nf += 1

        var sidx_closed: [4]int
        var n_closed = 0
        nf = 0
        while nf < nfds_in_reply
            val sidx = int(reply[slots_off + nf]) & 0xff
            var has_alloc = 0
            var i = 0
            while i < n_new
                if posix_fd_target(new_fds[i]) == sidx
                    has_alloc = 1
                    break
                i += 1
            if has_alloc == 0
                var dup = 0
                i = 0
                while i < n_closed
                    if sidx_closed[i] == sidx
                        dup = 1
                        break
                    i += 1
                if dup == 0
                    shim_unix_close(sidx)
                    sidx_closed[n_closed] = sidx
                    n_closed += 1
            nf += 1

Scatter data into the iovec.

        var written = 0
        ii = 0
        while ii < iov_len
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val base    = shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off)
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len > 0
                if base != i64(0)
                    val dst: *byte = *byte(base)
                    var take = dlen - written
                    if take > seg_len
                        take = seg_len
                    if take < 0
                        take = 0
                    var jj = 0
                    while jj < take
                        dst[jj] = reply[data_off + written + jj]
                        jj += 1
                    written += take
            ii += 1

Build SCM_RIGHTS cmsg in the user's control buffer if fds
were granted.

        var ctrl_used = 0
        if n_new > 0
            if ctrl_ptr != i64(0)
                val cb: *byte = *byte(ctrl_ptr)
                val cmsg_len_v = SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN + n_new * 4
                if cmsg_len_v <= ctrl_len
                    var i = 0
                    while i < 8
                        cb[i] = byte((cmsg_len_v >> (i * 8)) & 0xff)
                        i += 1
                    shim_write_i32(cb, 8, SHIM_SOL_SOCKET)
                    shim_write_i32(cb, 12, SHIM_SCM_RIGHTS)
                    var fi = 0
                    while fi < n_new
                        shim_write_i32(cb, SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN + fi * 4, new_fds[fi])
                        fi += 1
                    ctrl_used = cmsg_len_v
        shim_write_i32(mh, 40, ctrl_used)
        shim_write_i32(mh, 48, 0)
        i64(written)


sys_recvmsg helper for AF_UNIX. Issues UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG,
unpacks the reply (src_path + ancillary fds + data),
allocates a fresh posix_fd entry per transferred slot,
scatters data into the iovec, and writes a single
SOL_SOCKET/SCM_RIGHTS cmsg into the user's `msg_control`
buffer. Returns the number of payload bytes received.

STREAM dispatches to `sys_recvmsg_unix_stream`; DGRAM stays
in this function.

    sys_recvmsg_unix(fd: int, msghdr_ptr: i64, flags: int) -> i64
        if msghdr_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val mh: *byte = *byte(msghdr_ptr)
        val name_ptr = shim_read_ptr(mh, 0)
        val iov_ptr  = shim_read_ptr(mh, 16)
        val iov_len  = shim_read_i32(mh, 24)
        val ctrl_ptr = shim_read_ptr(mh, 32)
        val ctrl_len = shim_read_i32(mh, 40)
        if iov_len < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iov_len > SHIM_IOV_MAX
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iov_len > 0
            if iov_ptr == i64(0)
                return i64(-EFAULT)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val stype = int(posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].pos)
        if stype == POSIX_SOCK_STREAM
            return sys_recvmsg_unix_stream(fd, mh, ctrl_ptr, ctrl_len,
                iov_ptr, iov_len, flags)

DGRAM path: budget the cmsg buffer for max_nfds, send the
RECVMSG cmd, parse the reply.

        var max_nfds = 0
        if ctrl_ptr != i64(0)
            if ctrl_len >= SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN
                max_nfds = (ctrl_len - SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN) / 4
                if max_nfds > UNIX_SCM_MAX_FDS
                    max_nfds = UNIX_SCM_MAX_FDS
                if max_nfds < 0
                    max_nfds = 0
        var cap = 0
        val iov_base: *byte = *byte(iov_ptr)
        var ii = 0
        while ii < iov_len
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len > 0
                cap += seg_len
            ii += 1
        if cap > 256
            cap = 256
        if cap < 0
            cap = 0
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if target < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        if (flags & POSIX_MSG_DONTWAIT) != 0
            nb = POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        var msg: [5]byte
        if nb != 0
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG_NB)
        else
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_RECVMSG)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(max_nfds & 0xff)
        msg[3] = byte((cap >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[4] = byte(cap & 0xff)
        var reply: [600]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 600)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            val status = unix_status(reply[0])
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(status))
            return i64(status)

Reply layout: status(1) + src_plen(1) + src_path(P) +
nfds(1) + slots(F) + stypes(F) + dlen_be(2) + data(D).
The stypes block carries the server-side stype of each
transferred slot (1 = STREAM, 2 = DGRAM) so the new
posix_fd's `pos` matches the underlying server slot.

        val src_plen = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        val nfds_in_reply = int(reply[2 + src_plen]) & 0xff
        val slots_off = 2 + src_plen + 1
        val stypes_off = slots_off + nfds_in_reply
        val dlen_off = stypes_off + nfds_in_reply
        val dl_hi = (int(reply[dlen_off]) & 0xff) << 8
        val dl_lo = int(reply[dlen_off + 1]) & 0xff
        val dlen = dl_hi | dl_lo
        val data_off = dlen_off + 2

Allocate posix_fds for each transferred slot. Linux's
SCM_RIGHTS leaves the receiver responsible for closing
each new fd. If `posix_fd_alloc` fails (EMFILE) for a
particular slot, the receiver's owners-ring co-ownership
(added by the server during RECVMSG) needs to be
released — but only if NO other allocation in this batch
succeeded for the same sidx. With duplicate cmsg fds, an
early success + later fail must not call `UNIX_CMD_CLOSE`
or it would strip the receiver from owners and dangle the
earlier successful posix_fd.

Two-pass: first try every alloc; then walk the cmsg again
and call CLOSE only for sidx values that ended up with
zero successful allocs (de-duped so same sidx isn't closed
twice).

        var new_fds: [4]int
        var n_new = 0
        var nf = 0
        while nf < nfds_in_reply
            val sidx = int(reply[slots_off + nf]) & 0xff
            val sst  = int(reply[stypes_off + nf]) & 0xff
            val pfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET, sidx)
            if pfd >= 0
                val pid2 = posix_fd_current_pid()
                posix_fd_table[pid2 * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + pfd].pos = i64(sst)
                new_fds[n_new] = pfd
                n_new += 1
            nf += 1

        var sidx_closed: [4]int
        var n_closed = 0
        nf = 0
        while nf < nfds_in_reply
            val sidx = int(reply[slots_off + nf]) & 0xff
            var has_alloc = 0
            var i = 0
            while i < n_new
                if posix_fd_target(new_fds[i]) == sidx
                    has_alloc = 1
                    break
                i += 1
            if has_alloc == 0
                var dup = 0
                i = 0
                while i < n_closed
                    if sidx_closed[i] == sidx
                        dup = 1
                        break
                    i += 1
                if dup == 0
                    shim_unix_close(sidx)
                    sidx_closed[n_closed] = sidx
                    n_closed += 1
            nf += 1

Scatter data into the iovec.

        var written = 0
        ii = 0
        while ii < iov_len
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val base    = shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off)
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len > 0
                if base != i64(0)
                    val dst: *byte = *byte(base)
                    var take = dlen - written
                    if take > seg_len
                        take = seg_len
                    if take < 0
                        take = 0
                    var jj = 0
                    while jj < take
                        dst[jj] = reply[data_off + written + jj]
                        jj += 1
                    written += take
            ii += 1

Write src_path into msg_name as a sockaddr_un (family +
NUL-terminated path). Update msg_namelen.

        if name_ptr != i64(0)
            val a: *byte = *byte(name_ptr)
            a[0] = byte(POSIX_AF_UNIX & 0xff)
            a[1] = byte(0)
            var pcopy = src_plen
            if pcopy > UNIX_PATH_MAX
                pcopy = UNIX_PATH_MAX
            var i = 0
            while i < pcopy
                a[2 + i] = reply[2 + i]
                i += 1
            if 2 + pcopy < 110
                a[2 + pcopy] = byte(0)
            shim_write_i32(mh, 8, 2 + src_plen)

Build SCM_RIGHTS cmsg in the user's control buffer if
fds were granted and the buffer is non-null.

        var ctrl_used = 0
        if n_new > 0
            if ctrl_ptr != i64(0)
                val cb: *byte = *byte(ctrl_ptr)
                val cmsg_len_v = SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN + n_new * 4
                if cmsg_len_v <= ctrl_len
                    var i = 0
                    while i < 8
                        cb[i] = byte((cmsg_len_v >> (i * 8)) & 0xff)
                        i += 1
                    shim_write_i32(cb, 8, SHIM_SOL_SOCKET)
                    shim_write_i32(cb, 12, SHIM_SCM_RIGHTS)
                    var fi = 0
                    while fi < n_new
                        shim_write_i32(cb, SHIM_CMSG_HDR_LEN + fi * 4, new_fds[fi])
                        fi += 1
                    ctrl_used = cmsg_len_v
        shim_write_i32(mh, 40, ctrl_used)
        shim_write_i32(mh, 48, 0)
        i64(written)


SYS_BIND. For UDP sockets, forward to inet's BIND. For TCP
sockets, stash the bound port in `slot.pos` so a subsequent
`listen()` can use it — inet allocates the actual listen slot
lazily in `sys_listen`. The user's `sockaddr_in` is 16 bytes:
family(2), port(2, BE), addr(4), zero(8).

    sys_bind(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            return sys_bind_unix(fd, addr_ptr, addrlen)
        if addrlen < 16
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
        val sin_port = shim_read_u16(a, 2)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
            posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].pos = i64(sin_port)
            return i64(0)
        if k != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [9]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_BIND)
        msg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(POSIX_AF_INET)
        msg[3] = byte(sin_port >> 8)
        msg[4] = byte(sin_port & 0xff)
        msg[5] = a[4]
        msg[6] = a[5]
        msg[7] = a[6]
        msg[8] = a[7]
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 9, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-EIO))
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-EADDRINUSE))
            return i64(-EADDRINUSE)
        i64(0)

TCP variant of `sendto`.  `addr_ptr` is ignored — TCP is
connected, the peer was set at connect time. The body mirrors
sys_write's TCP loop (chunked at INET_MAX_TCP_SEG) but honors
POSIX MSG_DONTWAIT (0x40) for the EAGAIN-on-empty-send branch.

    sys_sendto_tcp(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int, flags: int) -> i64
        val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if inet_fd < 0
            return i64(-ENOTCONN)
        if len < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if len == 0
            return i64(0)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        val nb_eff = (posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK) | (flags & 0x40)
        val pid_t = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val tidx_t = pid_t * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        var tmo_t = posix_fd_table[tidx_t].send_timeout_ms
        if tmo_t < 0 || tmo_t > 65535
            tmo_t = 0
        val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
        var sent_total = 0
        while sent_total < len
            var chunk = len - sent_total
            if chunk > INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
                chunk = INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
            var msg: [487]byte
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SEND)
            shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
            shim_write_u16(msg, 3, chunk)
            shim_write_u16(msg, 5, tmo_t)
            var i = 0
            while i < chunk
                msg[7 + i] = ubuf[sent_total + i]
                i += 1
            var reply: [3]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 7 + chunk, reply, 3)
            if r < 0
                if sent_total > 0
                    return i64(sent_total)
                return i64(-EIO)
            if reply[0] == 2
                // SO_SNDTIMEO expired while parked — see sys_write.
                if sent_total > 0
                    return i64(sent_total)
                return i64(-EAGAIN)
            if reply[0] != 0
                if sent_total > 0
                    return i64(sent_total)
                // See sys_write for the pending_error rationale —
                // surface ETIMEDOUT / ECONNRESET / etc. instead of
                // a generic EPIPE when the connection was torn
                // down by an asynchronous error.
                var perr = 0
                val ger = shim_inet_get_tcp_err(inet_fd, perr)
                if ger == 0
                    if perr != 0
                        return i64(-perr)
                return i64(-EPIPE)
            val n = shim_read_u16(reply, 1)
            if n <= 0
                // Same parked-wake semantics as sys_write — see
                // there for the full rationale.
                if nb_eff != 0
                    if sent_total == 0
                        return i64(-EAGAIN)
                    return i64(sent_total)
            else
                sent_total += n
        i64(sent_total)


SYS_SENDTO. Args: (fd, buf, len, flags, dst_addr, addrlen).
Copies the payload into an in-kernel staging buffer (avoids
re-reading user memory during ipc_deliver's cross-PT walk) and
forwards to the inet server. `flags` is mostly ignored;
MSG_DONTWAIT (0x40) is honored for TCP via sys_sendto_tcp.

    sys_sendto(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int, flags: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen: int) -> i64
        val k0 = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k0 == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val stype = int(posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].pos)
            if stype == POSIX_SOCK_STREAM
                return sys_send_unix_stream(fd, buf, len)
            return sys_sendto_unix_dgram(fd, buf, len, addr_ptr, addrlen)
        if k0 == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return sys_sendto_tcp(fd, buf, len, flags)
        if k0 != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if len < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if len > 1472
            return i64(-EMSGSIZE)
        val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        // libc `send()` lowers to sendto with addr=NULL+addrlen=0;
        // resolve the peer from the prior sys_connect_udp. No
        // connected peer at this point is -ENOTCONN.
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        var dst_port: int = 0
        var ip0: byte = byte(0)
        var ip1: byte = byte(0)
        var ip2: byte = byte(0)
        var ip3: byte = byte(0)
        if addr_ptr == i64(0)
            if posix_fd_table[idx].udp_connected == 0
                return i64(-ENOTCONN)
            dst_port = posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_port
            ip0 = byte(posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_a & 0xff)
            ip1 = byte(posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_b & 0xff)
            ip2 = byte(posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_c & 0xff)
            ip3 = byte(posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_d & 0xff)
        else
            if addrlen < 16
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
            dst_port = shim_read_u16(a, 2)
            ip0 = a[4]
            ip1 = a[5]
            ip2 = a[6]
            ip3 = a[7]
        val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
        var msg: [1483]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_SENDTO)
        msg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(POSIX_AF_INET)
        msg[3] = byte(dst_port >> 8)
        msg[4] = byte(dst_port & 0xff)
        msg[5] = ip0
        msg[6] = ip1
        msg[7] = ip2
        msg[8] = ip3
        msg[9] = byte((len >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[10] = byte(len & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < len
            msg[11 + i] = ubuf[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [3]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 11 + len, reply, 3)
        if r < 0
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-EIO))
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-ENETUNREACH))
            return i64(-ENETUNREACH)
        i64(shim_read_u16(reply, 1))

SYS_RECVFROM. Args: (fd, buf, len, flags, src_addr, addrlen_ptr).
Blocks until a datagram is available. Writes the source's family
+ port + addr into `src_addr` (16-byte sockaddr_in) if non-null,
updates `*addrlen_ptr` to 16, and copies the payload into `buf`.

TCP variant of `recvfrom`.  Uses the same INET_CMD_TCP_RECV /
INET_CMD_TCP_RECV_NB IPC as `sys_read` but honors POSIX
MSG_DONTWAIT (0x40) — flag-based per-call NB override even when
the fd is in blocking mode. `addr_ptr` is filled with zeros if
non-null; Linux's recvfrom on TCP doesn't reliably populate the
src-addr for stream sockets, and apps that want the connected
peer call `getpeername`.

    sys_recvfrom_tcp(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int, flags: int,
                     addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64) -> i64
        val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if inet_fd < 0
            return i64(-ENOTCONN)
        if len <= 0
            return i64(0)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var cap_len = len
        if cap_len > INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
            cap_len = INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
        var nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        if (flags & 0x40) != 0
            nb = POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        var msg: [5]byte
        if nb != 0
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_RECV_NB)
        else
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_RECV)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 3, cap_len)
        var reply: [540]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 540)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if nb != 0
            val raw = int(reply[0]) & 0xff
            if raw != 0
                if raw >= 128
                    return i64(raw - 256)
                return i64(raw)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        val rlen = shim_read_u16(reply, 1)
        val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
        var i = 0
        while i < rlen
            ubuf[i] = reply[3 + i]
            i += 1
        // addr_ptr/addrlen_ptr write: Linux leaves the fields
        // undefined for TCP recvfrom but most callers expect them
        // unmodified-but-valid. Zero-fill if addr_ptr was supplied.
        if addr_ptr != i64(0)
            var zero: [4]byte
            zero[0] = byte(0)
            zero[1] = byte(0)
            zero[2] = byte(0)
            zero[3] = byte(0)
            shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr, 0, &zero[0])
            shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, 16)
        i64(rlen)


    sys_recvfrom(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int, flags: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64) -> i64
        val k0 = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k0 == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val stype = int(posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].pos)
            if stype == POSIX_SOCK_STREAM
                return sys_recv_unix_stream(fd, buf, len, flags)
            return sys_recvfrom_unix_dgram(fd, buf, len, flags, addr_ptr, addrlen_ptr)
        if k0 == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return sys_recvfrom_tcp(fd, buf, len, flags, addr_ptr, addrlen_ptr)
        if k0 != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if len < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        var cap_len = len
        if cap_len > 1472
            cap_len = 1472
        // MSG_DONTWAIT (0x40) overrides the fd's blocking mode for
        // this single call — Linux semantics. libuv uses it to
        // avoid the fcntl race when the fd's mode is shared with
        // other pollers.
        var nonblock = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        if (flags & POSIX_MSG_DONTWAIT) != 0
            nonblock = POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        // MSG_PEEK (0x02): read the head of the inet queue but leave
        // it queued so a follow-up recv (without PEEK) returns the
        // same bytes. Forwarded to the inet server as a per-call byte
        // appended to the existing RECVFROM / RECVFROM_TIMEOUT wire
        // formats.
        var peek_byte = byte(0)
        if (flags & POSIX_MSG_PEEK) != 0
            peek_byte = byte(1)
        val rcvtimeo_ms = posix_fd_table[idx].recv_timeout_ms
        var reply: [1500]byte
        if nonblock != 0
            // Non-blocking: reuse RECVFROM_TIMEOUT with a zero
            // timeout — the inet handler will reply with status=1
            // (no data) immediately if the queue is empty.
            var nmsg: [9]byte
            nmsg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_RECVFROM_TIMEOUT)
            nmsg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
            nmsg[2] = byte((cap_len >> 8) & 0xff)
            nmsg[3] = byte(cap_len & 0xff)
            nmsg[4] = byte(0)
            nmsg[5] = byte(0)
            nmsg[6] = byte(0)
            nmsg[7] = byte(0)
            nmsg[8] = peek_byte
            val nr = ipc_send(port, nmsg, 9, reply, 1500)
            if nr < 0
                posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-EIO))
                return i64(-EIO)
            if reply[0] == byte(1)
                return i64(-EAGAIN)
            if reply[0] == byte(2)
                posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-ECONNREFUSED))
                return i64(-ECONNREFUSED)        // ICMP error
            if reply[0] != 0
                posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-EIO))
                return i64(-EIO)
        else if rcvtimeo_ms > 0
            // Blocking with SO_RCVTIMEO: bounded wait. Linux returns
            // -EAGAIN (or -EWOULDBLOCK, same value) on timeout — same
            // shape as O_NONBLOCK on empty queue.
            var tmsg: [9]byte
            tmsg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_RECVFROM_TIMEOUT)
            tmsg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
            tmsg[2] = byte((cap_len >> 8) & 0xff)
            tmsg[3] = byte(cap_len & 0xff)
            tmsg[4] = byte((rcvtimeo_ms >> 24) & 0xff)
            tmsg[5] = byte((rcvtimeo_ms >> 16) & 0xff)
            tmsg[6] = byte((rcvtimeo_ms >> 8) & 0xff)
            tmsg[7] = byte(rcvtimeo_ms & 0xff)
            tmsg[8] = peek_byte
            val tr = ipc_send(port, tmsg, 9, reply, 1500)
            if tr < 0
                posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-EIO))
                return i64(-EIO)
            if reply[0] == byte(1)
                return i64(-EAGAIN)         // timed out
            if reply[0] == byte(2)
                posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-ECONNREFUSED))
                return i64(-ECONNREFUSED)        // ICMP error
            if reply[0] != 0
                posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-EIO))
                return i64(-EIO)
        else
            var msg: [5]byte
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_RECVFROM)
            msg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
            msg[2] = byte((cap_len >> 8) & 0xff)
            msg[3] = byte(cap_len & 0xff)
            msg[4] = peek_byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 1500)
            if r < 0
                posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-EIO))
                return i64(-EIO)
            if reply[0] == byte(2)
                posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-ECONNREFUSED))
                return i64(-ECONNREFUSED)        // ICMP error
            if reply[0] != 0
                posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-EIO))
                return i64(-EIO)
        // Connected-UDP filter (Linux semantics): if a peer was
        // saved via sys_connect_udp and the source doesn't match,
        // recurse to drop the datagram and try again. Each non-
        // matching datagram is already dequeued from inet (the
        // peek-stripped recurse below handles the MSG_PEEK case so
        // we don't busy-loop on a queued mismatched head).
        if posix_fd_table[idx].udp_connected != 0
            val r_port = shim_read_u16(reply, 2)
            var matches = 1
            if r_port != posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_port
                matches = 0
            if (int(reply[4]) & 0xff) != posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_a
                matches = 0
            if (int(reply[5]) & 0xff) != posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_b
                matches = 0
            if (int(reply[6]) & 0xff) != posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_c
                matches = 0
            if (int(reply[7]) & 0xff) != posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_d
                matches = 0
            if matches == 0
                // First, dequeue the mismatched head if MSG_PEEK left
                // it in the queue. Strip the peek bit and issue a
                // throwaway non-blocking RECVFROM_TIMEOUT(0) — Linux
                // discards mismatched datagrams from the kernel queue
                // regardless of the user's MSG_PEEK flag.
                if (flags & POSIX_MSG_PEEK) != 0
                    var dmsg: [9]byte
                    dmsg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_RECVFROM_TIMEOUT)
                    dmsg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
                    dmsg[2] = byte((cap_len >> 8) & 0xff)
                    dmsg[3] = byte(cap_len & 0xff)
                    dmsg[4] = byte(0)
                    dmsg[5] = byte(0)
                    dmsg[6] = byte(0)
                    dmsg[7] = byte(0)
                    dmsg[8] = byte(0)
                    var dreply: [1500]byte
                    val dr = ipc_send(port, dmsg, 9, dreply, 1500)
                    // Best-effort drop; ignore reply status.
                return sys_recvfrom(fd, buf, len, flags, addr_ptr, addrlen_ptr)
        val src_port = shim_read_u16(reply, 2)
        val payload_len = shim_read_u16(reply, 8)
        if addr_ptr != i64(0)
            val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
            a[0] = byte(POSIX_AF_INET & 0xff)
            a[1] = byte((POSIX_AF_INET >> 8) & 0xff)
            a[2] = byte(src_port >> 8)
            a[3] = byte(src_port & 0xff)
            a[4] = reply[4]
            a[5] = reply[5]
            a[6] = reply[6]
            a[7] = reply[7]
            var z = 8
            while z < 16
                a[z] = byte(0)
                z += 1
        if addrlen_ptr != i64(0)
            val alp: *i32 = *i32(addrlen_ptr)
            *alp = i32(16)
        val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
        var copy_len = payload_len
        if copy_len > len
            copy_len = len
        var i = 0
        while i < copy_len
            ubuf[i] = reply[10 + i]
            i += 1
        i64(copy_len)

SYS_SENDMSG / SYS_RECVMSG. MVP that handles `msg_iov` gather/
scatter and `msg_name` for UDP datagrams. Ancillary data
(`msg_control`) is ignored — programs that pass control buffers
won't observe them coming back. `msg_flags` is currently a
no-op; O_NONBLOCK still comes from the fd's flags via fcntl.

LP64 little-endian struct layout (matches musl's `<sys/socket.h>`
on both aarch64 and x86_64):

```
struct msghdr {
    void         *msg_name;        // off  0, 8 bytes
    socklen_t     msg_namelen;     // off  8, 4 bytes (+ 4 pad)
    struct iovec *msg_iov;         // off 16, 8 bytes
    int           msg_iovlen;      // off 24, 4 bytes (+ 4 __pad1)
    void         *msg_control;     // off 32, 8 bytes
    socklen_t     msg_controllen;  // off 40, 4 bytes (+ 4 pad)
    int           msg_flags;       // off 48, 4 bytes
};
struct iovec { void *iov_base; size_t iov_len; }; // 16 bytes
```

To keep the gather/scatter buffer bounded, we cap `iovlen` at
`SHIM_IOV_MAX = 8` and total bytes at `SHIM_MSG_MAX = 1472` —
matches the inet UDP datagram cap (full Ethernet MTU) and is
plenty for the TCP chunk-at-a-time path. The constants
themselves live up next to the other shim-wide caps so AF_UNIX
helpers can use them without a forward reference.

    shim_read_ptr(p: *byte, off: int) -> i64
        var v: i64 = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < 8
            v = v | (i64(int(p[off + i]) & 0xff) << (i * 8))
            i += 1
        v

    shim_read_i32(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        (int(p[off]) & 0xff) | ((int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff) << 8) | ((int(p[off + 2]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(p[off + 3]) & 0xff) << 24)

    shim_write_i32(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte(v & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 2] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        p[off + 3] = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)

    sys_sendmsg(fd: int, msghdr_ptr: i64, flags: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            return sys_sendmsg_unix(fd, msghdr_ptr, flags)
        if k != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                return i64(-EBADF)
        if msghdr_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val mh: *byte = *byte(msghdr_ptr)
        val name_ptr  = shim_read_ptr(mh, 0)
        val name_len  = shim_read_i32(mh, 8)
        val iov_ptr   = shim_read_ptr(mh, 16)
        val iov_len   = shim_read_i32(mh, 24)
        if iov_len < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iov_len > SHIM_IOV_MAX
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iov_len > 0
            if iov_ptr == i64(0)
                return i64(-EFAULT)
        var gather: [1472]byte
        var total = 0
        val iov_base: *byte = *byte(iov_ptr)
        var ii = 0
        while ii < iov_len
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val base    = shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off)
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len > 0
                if base == i64(0)
                    return i64(-EFAULT)
                val src: *byte = *byte(base)
                var jj = 0
                while jj < seg_len
                    if total < SHIM_MSG_MAX
                        gather[total] = src[jj]
                        total += 1
                    jj += 1
            ii += 1
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            if name_ptr == i64(0)
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            if name_len < 16
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            return sys_sendto(fd, i64(&gather[0]), total, flags, name_ptr, 16)
        sys_write(fd, i64(&gather[0]), total)

    sys_recvmsg(fd: int, msghdr_ptr: i64, flags: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            return sys_recvmsg_unix(fd, msghdr_ptr, flags)
        if k != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                return i64(-EBADF)
        if msghdr_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val mh: *byte = *byte(msghdr_ptr)
        val name_ptr  = shim_read_ptr(mh, 0)
        val iov_ptr   = shim_read_ptr(mh, 16)
        val iov_len   = shim_read_i32(mh, 24)
        if iov_len < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iov_len > SHIM_IOV_MAX
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iov_len > 0
            if iov_ptr == i64(0)
                return i64(-EFAULT)
        // Compute total scatter capacity across iovs.
        var cap = 0
        val iov_base: *byte = *byte(iov_ptr)
        var ii = 0
        while ii < iov_len
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len > 0
                cap += seg_len
            ii += 1
        if cap > SHIM_MSG_MAX
            cap = SHIM_MSG_MAX
        var scratch: [1472]byte
        // Stage the receive into the scratch buffer; sys_recvfrom
        // / sys_read writes there, then we scatter into the iovs.
        var got: i64 = i64(0)
        var src_addr: [16]byte
        var src_len: i32 = i32(16)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            // For UDP, write src into msg_name if the user gave one.
            if name_ptr != i64(0)
                got = sys_recvfrom(fd, i64(&scratch[0]), cap, flags,
                    name_ptr, msghdr_ptr + i64(8))
            else
                got = sys_recvfrom(fd, i64(&scratch[0]), cap, flags,
                    i64(&src_addr[0]), i64(&src_len))
        else
            got = sys_read(fd, i64(&scratch[0]), cap)
        if got < i64(0)
            return got
        // Scatter into iovs.
        val nbytes = int(got)
        var written = 0
        ii = 0
        while ii < iov_len
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val base    = shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off)
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len > 0
                if base != i64(0)
                    val dst: *byte = *byte(base)
                    var take = nbytes - written
                    if take > seg_len
                        take = seg_len
                    var jj = 0
                    while jj < take
                        dst[jj] = scratch[written + jj]
                        jj += 1
                    written += take
            ii += 1
        // Clear msg_controllen and msg_flags in the user's struct
        // (signal "no ancillary, no flags" cleanly).
        shim_write_i32(mh, 40, 0)
        shim_write_i32(mh, 48, 0)
        i64(nbytes)

SYS_CONNECT. TCP active open. Issues `INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT` which
parks the caller until ESTABLISHED; on success the inet server
replies with its socket id. We stash that id as the slot's backend
target so subsequent read/write/close route to the right TCP
record. The user's sockaddr_in gives dst_ip (4 bytes) and
dst_port (BE u16) at the usual offsets.

UDP variant of `connect()` — POSIX semantics: set a default peer
that future sendto-with-NULL-addr (libc `send()`) and the recv
filter (deferred — currently we deliver from any peer) use.
NULL addr or sa_family == AF_UNSPEC dissolves the binding.
Returns 0 on success, -EAFNOSUPPORT if the address family is
something other than AF_INET / AF_UNSPEC.

    sys_connect_udp(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen: int) -> i64
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        if addr_ptr == i64(0)
            posix_fd_table[idx].udp_connected = 0
            return i64(0)
        if addrlen < 2
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
        val family = (int(a[0]) & 0xff) | ((int(a[1]) & 0xff) << 8)
        if family == 0
            // AF_UNSPEC dissolve.
            posix_fd_table[idx].udp_connected = 0
            return i64(0)
        if family != POSIX_AF_INET
            return i64(-EAFNOSUPPORT)
        if addrlen < 16
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val peer_port = ((int(a[2]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(a[3]) & 0xff)
        posix_fd_table[idx].udp_connected = 1
        posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_port = peer_port
        posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_a = int(a[4]) & 0xff
        posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_b = int(a[5]) & 0xff
        posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_c = int(a[6]) & 0xff
        posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_d = int(a[7]) & 0xff
        i64(0)


    sys_connect(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen: int) -> i64
        val k0 = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k0 == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            return sys_connect_unix(fd, addr_ptr, addrlen)
        if k0 == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            return sys_connect_udp(fd, addr_ptr, addrlen)
        if k0 != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if addrlen < 16
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if posix_fd_target(fd) >= 0
            return i64(-EISCONN)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
        val nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        val pending_snd = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_sndbuf
        val pending_rcv = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_rcvbuf
        var msg: [11]byte
        if nb != 0
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT_NB)
        else
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT)
        msg[1] = a[4]
        msg[2] = a[5]
        msg[3] = a[6]
        msg[4] = a[7]
        msg[5] = a[2]                   // sin_port high byte (already BE)
        msg[6] = a[3]                   // sin_port low byte
        // SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF the caller stashed pre-connect via
        // setsockopt. 0 means "use the inet-side default".
        var snd_clamped = pending_snd
        if snd_clamped > 0xffff
            snd_clamped = 0xffff
        var rcv_clamped = pending_rcv
        if rcv_clamped > 0xffff
            rcv_clamped = 0xffff
        shim_write_u16(msg, 7, snd_clamped)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 9, rcv_clamped)
        var reply: [2]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 11, reply, 2)
        if r < 0
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-EIO))
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            posix_fd_set_error(fd, i64(-ECONNREFUSED))
            return i64(-ECONNREFUSED)
        val inet_fd = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        posix_fd_table[idx].target = inet_fd
        // Apply pending SO_KEEPALIVE now that the slot exists.
        // Bufsize already shipped through the extended CONNECT
        // wire; keepalive uses a follow-up IPC since it's a
        // post-handshake property (only fires in ESTABLISHED).
        if posix_fd_table[idx].flag_keepalive != 0
            shim_inet_set_keepalive(inet_fd, 1)
        // Apply persisted TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL / TCP_KEEPCNT.
        // Same shape as keepalive: pre-slot setsockopt landed only
        // on the shim, so the slot starts with defaults until we
        // replay the per-option IPC here.
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_idle_secs > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(inet_fd, 0,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_idle_secs * 100)
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_intvl_secs > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(inet_fd, 1,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_intvl_secs * 100)
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_cnt > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(inet_fd, 2,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_cnt)
        // Apply pending TCP_USER_TIMEOUT — same shape as keepalive,
        // just one IPC.  No-op when the user never set it (zero
        // skipped on the inet side).
        if posix_fd_table[idx].tcp_user_timeout_ms > 0
            shim_inet_set_userto(inet_fd,
                posix_fd_table[idx].tcp_user_timeout_ms)
        // Non-blocking connect: surface -EINPROGRESS so the user
        // can poll EPOLLOUT for completion. The slot is already
        // SYN_SENT; once it reaches ESTABLISHED the existing
        // inet_epoll_fire path delivers the readiness wake.
        if nb != 0
            return i64(-EINPROGRESS)
        i64(0)

SYS_LISTEN. Requires a prior bind() to have set the local port in
`slot.pos`. Forwards to `INET_CMD_TCP_LISTEN` and stashes the
returned listen fd as the backend target. `backlog` is advisory —
inet uses a fixed `TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG` internally.

    sys_listen(fd: int, backlog: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            return sys_listen_unix(fd, backlog)
        if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if posix_fd_target(fd) >= 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)             // already listening or connected
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        val local_port = int(posix_fd_table[idx].pos)
        if local_port <= 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)             // must bind first
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [9]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_LISTEN)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, local_port)
        // Pass user's `backlog` through; inet caps at TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG
        // and normalises 0 to 1. Negative values are clamped to 1 too.
        var bl = backlog
        if bl < 0
            bl = 0
        if bl > 0xffff
            bl = 0xffff
        shim_write_u16(msg, 3, bl)
        // Pre-listen SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF — accepted children inherit
        // the listening socket's sizes (Linux semantics).
        var snd_clamped = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_sndbuf
        if snd_clamped > 0xffff
            snd_clamped = 0xffff
        var rcv_clamped = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_rcvbuf
        if rcv_clamped > 0xffff
            rcv_clamped = 0xffff
        shim_write_u16(msg, 5, snd_clamped)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 7, rcv_clamped)
        var reply: [2]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 9, reply, 2)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-EADDRINUSE)
        val listen_fd = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        posix_fd_table[idx].target = listen_fd
        posix_fd_table[idx].is_listen = 1
        // Apply pending SO_KEEPALIVE — accepted children inherit
        // the listener's flag via inet's handle_passive_syn.
        if posix_fd_table[idx].flag_keepalive != 0
            shim_inet_set_keepalive(listen_fd, 1)
        // Apply pending TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL / TCP_KEEPCNT —
        // accepted children also inherit these via
        // inet_tcp_handle_passive_syn.
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_idle_secs > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(listen_fd, 0,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_idle_secs * 100)
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_intvl_secs > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(listen_fd, 1,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_intvl_secs * 100)
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_cnt > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(listen_fd, 2,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_cnt)
        // Apply pending TCP_USER_TIMEOUT.  The listener slot itself
        // never enters ESTABLISHED so the timer never fires on it,
        // but accepted children inherit `user_timeout_ticks` from
        // the listener via `inet_tcp_handle_passive_syn`.
        if posix_fd_table[idx].tcp_user_timeout_ms > 0
            shim_inet_set_userto(listen_fd,
                posix_fd_table[idx].tcp_user_timeout_ms)
        i64(0)

SYS_ACCEPT. Blocks until a child connection arrives unless the
listen fd is `O_NONBLOCK`, in which case the inet NB variant
returns -EAGAIN immediately when the queue is empty. inet replies
with the child's socket id + peer's ip/port; we allocate a fresh
POSIX slot for the child, populate `*addr` with the peer's
sockaddr_in (if non-null), and return the new POSIX fd. Inet may
also send data + FIN before accept returns, so the child can be in
CLOSE_WAIT on arrival — read/close paths handle that already.

    sys_accept(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            return sys_accept_unix(fd, addr_ptr, addrlen_ptr)
        if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val listen_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if listen_fd < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        val nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        var msg: [3]byte
        if nb != 0
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT_NB)
        else
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, listen_fd)
        var reply: [8]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 8)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] == byte(-11)
            // NB variant returns -EAGAIN when queue is empty.
            return i64(-EAGAIN)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        val child_inet_fd = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        val peer_port = shim_read_u16(reply, 6)
        if addr_ptr != i64(0)
            val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
            a[0] = byte(POSIX_AF_INET & 0xff)
            a[1] = byte(0)
            a[2] = byte(peer_port >> 8)
            a[3] = byte(peer_port & 0xff)
            a[4] = reply[2]
            a[5] = reply[3]
            a[6] = reply[4]
            a[7] = reply[5]
            var z = 8
            while z < 16
                a[z] = byte(0)
                z += 1
        if addrlen_ptr != i64(0)
            val alp: *i32 = *i32(addrlen_ptr)
            *alp = i32(16)
        val new_fd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET, child_inet_fd)
        if new_fd < 0
            var close_msg: [3]byte
            close_msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_CLOSE)
            shim_write_u16(close_msg, 1, child_inet_fd)
            var close_rep: [1]byte
            ipc_send(port, close_msg, 3, close_rep, 1)
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        i64(new_fd)

SYS_ACCEPT4.  Linux's accept(2) variant that takes a `flags`
argument so the accepted child fd can be born non-blocking
(`SOCK_NONBLOCK = 0x800` = same value as `O_NONBLOCK`) and/or
close-on-exec (`SOCK_CLOEXEC = 0x80000`) atomically — without
the racy `accept` + `fcntl(F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK)` dance servers
otherwise have to do.  The shim is single-threaded so the race
isn't observable here either way, but matching the contract
keeps libuv / Node.js / glibc happy.

`SOCK_NONBLOCK` is honoured: the child PosixFd carries
`POSIX_O_NONBLOCK` from birth, so its first read/write/etc.
already routes through the non-blocking inet path.
`SOCK_CLOEXEC` is accept-and-ignore for now (no fork/exec
story yet — see `feedback_no_workarounds_build_it.md` for the
follow-up arc).  Unknown flag bits return -EINVAL.

    sys_accept4(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64, flags: int) -> i64
        val known = POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK | POSIX_SOCK_CLOEXEC
        if (flags & ~known) != 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val r = sys_accept(fd, addr_ptr, addrlen_ptr)
        if r < i64(0)
            return r
        val new_fd = int(r)
        if (flags & POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK) != 0
            val cur = posix_fd_flags(new_fd)
            posix_fd_set_flags(new_fd, cur | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
        i64(new_fd)


SYS_SHUTDOWN. Real half-close via `INET_CMD_TCP_SHUTDOWN`. `how`
values (Linux): 0 = SHUT_RD (no-op — we don't track a read-shut
state, reads continue), 1 = SHUT_WR (inet emits FIN via
`inet_tcp_do_shutdown_wr`, leaves socket readable until peer
FINs, does NOT park the caller), 2 = SHUT_RDWR (treated as
SHUT_WR for now — a proper SHUT_RDWR would also drop buffered
recv data). Not valid on non-TCP fds.

    sys_shutdown(fd: int, how: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            // Linux accepts shutdown on UDP as a no-op; some apps
            // call shutdown(udp_fd, SHUT_WR) before close as a
            // best-effort flush hint.
            return i64(0)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if target < 0
                return i64(-EBADF)
            if how < 0
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            if how > 2
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            val uport = unix_get_port()
            if uport < 0
                return i64(-ENODEV)
            var umsg: [3]byte
            umsg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_SHUTDOWN)
            umsg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
            umsg[2] = byte(how & 0xff)
            var ureply: [1]byte
            val ur = ipc_send(uport, umsg, 3, ureply, 1)
            if ur < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            if ureply[0] != 0
                return i64(unix_status(ureply[0]))
            return i64(0)
        if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if inet_fd < 0
            return i64(-ENOTCONN)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [4]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SHUTDOWN)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        msg[3] = byte(how & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        i64(0)

Send INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPALIVE.  Returns 0 / negative errno.
Used at setsockopt(SO_KEEPALIVE) time on a TCP fd that already
has an inet slot, and at connect/listen time to apply a flag the
caller stashed pre-slot.

    shim_inet_set_keepalive(inet_fd: int, on: int) -> int
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return -5
        var msg: [4]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPALIVE)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        msg[3] = byte(on & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return -5
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -22
        0


Send INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF for one buffer.  `is_send` selects which.
Returns 0 on success, negative errno on failure.

    shim_inet_setbuf(inet_fd: int, is_send: bool, size: int) -> int
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return -5
        var msg: [6]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        if is_send
            msg[3] = byte(0)
        else
            msg[3] = byte(1)
        var sz = size
        if sz > 0xffff
            sz = 0xffff
        shim_write_u16(msg, 4, sz)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 6, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return -5
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -22
        0

Send INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPOPT.  `kind` selects IDLE (0), INTVL
(1), or CNT (2).  `value` is in *ticks* for IDLE/INTVL — this
helper does NOT do unit conversion; the caller has already
converted seconds→ticks. Returns 0 on success, negative errno on
failure.

    shim_inet_set_keepopt(inet_fd: int, kind: int, value: int) -> int
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return -5
        var msg: [8]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPOPT)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        msg[3] = byte(kind & 0xff)
        msg[4] = byte((value >> 24) & 0xff)
        msg[5] = byte((value >> 16) & 0xff)
        msg[6] = byte((value >>  8) & 0xff)
        msg[7] = byte( value        & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 8, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return -5
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -22
        0


Send INET_CMD_TCP_SET_USERTO with `timeout_ms` packed as a u32 big-
endian.  Inet rounds milliseconds up to 10-ms ticks.  Returns 0 on
success, negative errno on failure.

    shim_inet_set_userto(inet_fd: int, timeout_ms: int) -> int
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return -5
        var ms = timeout_ms
        if ms < 0
            ms = 0
        var msg: [7]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SET_USERTO)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        msg[3] = byte((ms >> 24) & 0xff)
        msg[4] = byte((ms >> 16) & 0xff)
        msg[5] = byte((ms >>  8) & 0xff)
        msg[6] = byte( ms        & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 7, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return -5
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -22
        0


Send INET_CMD_TCP_BLACKHOLE_ACKS_TEST — slix-only test injector
that arms (`on=1`) or disarms (`on=0`) the ACK blackhole on
`inet_fd`.  Used by `slix/test/musrto.c`; not part of the POSIX
surface.

    shim_inet_set_blackhole_acks(inet_fd: int, on: int) -> int
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return -5
        var msg: [4]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_BLACKHOLE_ACKS_TEST)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        msg[3] = byte(on & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return -5
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -22
        0


Send INET_CMD_TCP_GET_KEEPOPT.  Returns the per-socket value in
*ticks* (or count for CNT) on success via the `out v` parameter,
or a negative errno via the function return.  Caller-side does
the ticks→seconds conversion before handing back to userspace.

    shim_inet_get_keepopt(inet_fd: int, kind: int, out v: int) -> int
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return -5
        var msg: [4]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_GET_KEEPOPT)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        msg[3] = byte(kind & 0xff)
        var reply: [5]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 5)
        if r < 0
            return -5
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -22
        val b1 = (int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 24
        val b2 = (int(reply[2]) & 0xff) << 16
        val b3 = (int(reply[3]) & 0xff) <<  8
        val b4 =  int(reply[4]) & 0xff
        v = b1 | b2 | b3 | b4
        0


Send `INET_CMD_TCP_GET_ERR`.  Returns 0 on success with the
positive errno written into `out err` (0 = no pending error), or
a negative errno on IPC failure.  inet clears its side as part
of the read so the SO_ERROR read-and-clear contract holds even
across the slot teardown.

    shim_inet_get_tcp_err(inet_fd: int, out err: int) -> int
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return -5
        var msg: [3]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_GET_ERR)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        var reply: [2]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 2)
        if r < 0
            return -5
        if reply[0] != 0
            err = 0
            return 0
        err = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        0


SYS_SETSOCKOPT / SYS_GETSOCKOPT. The inet server tracks
`SO_SNDBUF` / `SO_RCVBUF` (since Phase E bufsize chunk).  The
remaining accept-list options (SO_REUSEADDR, SO_BROADCAST,
SO_KEEPALIVE, SO_REUSEPORT, TCP_NODELAY) are persisted on the
shim fd so getsockopt echoes the actually-set value (Linux-compat
introspection) but their behavioural enforcement is a follow-up:

- SO_REUSEADDR — slix is already permissive (no strict TIME_WAIT
  bind-block today), so the flag is informational.
- SO_BROADCAST — UDP send always allows broadcast destinations.
- SO_KEEPALIVE — no idle timer wired in inet yet.
- SO_REUSEPORT — same as REUSEADDR for the no-strict-bind case.
- TCP_NODELAY — slix doesn't implement Nagle, so segments already
  go out immediately; the flag is effectively pinned to "on".

Anything outside the accept-list returns `-ENOPROTOOPT`.

Linux `SOL_SOCKET = 1`; `SO_REUSEADDR = 2`, `SO_ERROR = 4`,
`SO_BROADCAST = 6`, `SO_SNDBUF = 7`, `SO_RCVBUF = 8`,
`SO_KEEPALIVE = 9`, `SO_REUSEPORT = 15`. `IPPROTO_TCP = 6`;
`TCP_NODELAY = 1`.

Read a 4-byte i32 out of the user's optval buffer.  Bounds + null-
pointer checks return non-zero on failure (caller should propagate
as -EINVAL).

    shim_sockopt_read_int(optval: i64, optlen: int, out v: int) -> int
        if optlen < 4 || optval == i64(0)
            return -1
        val vp: *i32 = *i32(optval)
        v = int(*vp)
        0

    sys_setsockopt(fd: int, level: int, optname: int, optval: i64, optlen: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                return i64(-EBADF)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val tidx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd

`level == 0x534c` is the slix-only test channel ("SL").  Used by
`slix/test/musrto.c` to arm the per-slot ACK blackhole so the
TCP_USER_TIMEOUT smoke test can simulate a peer that's stopped
ACKing on slix's synchronous loopback fastpath.  Not part of any
POSIX protocol; the hardcoded literal sits well outside the
assigned `SOL_*` / `IPPROTO_*` ranges (max ~300).

`optname == 1` is "set ACK blackhole on this slot's child", with
`*(int*)optval` as the on/off value.  Returns -ENOPROTOOPT for
non-TCP fds and -ENOTCONN for fds without an inet slot — there's
no resurrection on this test surface.

        if level == 0x534c
            if optname == 1
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    return i64(-ENOPROTOOPT)
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                val bh_inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                if bh_inet_fd < 0
                    return i64(-ENOTCONN)
                val rc = shim_inet_set_blackhole_acks(bh_inet_fd, v)
                if rc != 0
                    return i64(rc)
                return i64(0)
            return i64(-ENOPROTOOPT)
        if level == 1
            if optname == 2
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                posix_fd_table[tidx].flag_reuseaddr = v
                return i64(0)
            if optname == 6
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                posix_fd_table[tidx].flag_broadcast = v
                return i64(0)
            if optname == 9
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                posix_fd_table[tidx].flag_keepalive = v
                // Push to inet immediately when a slot exists so
                // the probe/giveup cycle either starts (v != 0) or
                // stops (v == 0) right now. Pre-slot calls just
                // persist; sys_connect / sys_listen apply later.
                if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    val ka_inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                    if ka_inet_fd >= 0
                        shim_inet_set_keepalive(ka_inet_fd, v)
                return i64(0)
            if optname == 15
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                posix_fd_table[tidx].flag_reuseport = v
                return i64(0)
            // SO_LINGER. Wire is `struct linger { i32 l_onoff;
            // i32 l_linger; }` — 8 bytes.  Persist both fields;
            // sys_close consults them at close time to pick
            // FIN-vs-RST.  Negative l_linger is clamped to 0
            // (matches Linux glibc behaviour).
            if optname == 13
                if optlen < 8 || optval == i64(0)
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                val lp_on: *i32 = *i32(optval)
                val lp_secs: *i32 = *i32(optval + i64(4))
                var l_on = int(*lp_on)
                var l_secs = int(*lp_secs)
                if l_secs < 0
                    l_secs = 0
                posix_fd_table[tidx].linger_on = l_on
                posix_fd_table[tidx].linger_secs = l_secs
                return i64(0)
            // SO_RCVTIMEO (20) / SO_SNDTIMEO (21).  16-byte timeval
            // {tv_sec(8), tv_usec(8)}.  Persisted as ms on the shim
            // fd; sys_recvfrom honors recv_timeout_ms via the
            // RECVFROM_TIMEOUT IPC, and sys_write / sys_sendto_tcp
            // ride send_timeout_ms into INET_CMD_TCP_SEND so inet's
            // park-with-deadline replies status=2 → -EAGAIN on
            // expiry.  UDP sendto remains unenforced (rarely blocks).
            if optname == 20 || optname == 21
                if optlen < 16 || optval == i64(0)
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                val tv_sec_lo = shim_read_ptr(*byte(optval), 0)
                val tv_usec_lo = shim_read_ptr(*byte(optval), 8)
                var ms = int(tv_sec_lo) * 1000 + (int(tv_usec_lo) / 1000)
                if ms < 0
                    ms = 0
                if optname == 20
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].recv_timeout_ms = ms
                else
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].send_timeout_ms = ms
                return i64(0)
            // SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF.  Persist on the shim fd so the
            // value survives until connect/listen creates an inet
            // slot, then apply via INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF if a slot
            // already exists. UDP buffers aren't sized per-socket
            // today, so silently accept and ignore for UDP (matches
            // Linux's tolerance).
            if optname == 7 || optname == 8
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    return i64(0)
                var requested = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, requested) != 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                if requested < 0
                    requested = 0
                if optname == 7
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].pending_sndbuf = requested
                else
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].pending_rcvbuf = requested
                val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                if inet_fd < 0
                    return i64(0)
                val rc = shim_inet_setbuf(inet_fd, optname == 7, requested)
                if rc != 0
                    return i64(rc)
                return i64(0)
        if level == 6
            if optname == 1
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                posix_fd_table[tidx].flag_nodelay = v
                return i64(0)
            // TCP_KEEPIDLE (4) / TCP_KEEPINTVL (5) — seconds.
            // TCP_KEEPCNT (6) — count.
            if optname == 4 || optname == 5 || optname == 6
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    return i64(-ENOPROTOOPT)
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                if v < 0
                    v = 0
                if optname == 4
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].keep_idle_secs = v
                else if optname == 5
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].keep_intvl_secs = v
                else
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].keep_cnt = v
                // Push to inet immediately if a slot exists; pre-slot
                // calls just persist on the shim fd. Slot creation
                // (sys_connect / sys_listen) replays them via the
                // same path.
                val ko_inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                if ko_inet_fd >= 0
                    var kind = 0
                    if optname == 5
                        kind = 1
                    else if optname == 6
                        kind = 2
                    var ticks = v
                    if optname != 6
                        ticks = v * 100        // sec → tick @ 100 Hz
                    val rc = shim_inet_set_keepopt(ko_inet_fd, kind, ticks)
                    if rc != 0
                        return i64(rc)
                return i64(0)
            // TCP_USER_TIMEOUT (Linux level=6, optname=18) —
            // milliseconds (RFC 5482 §3 "ms" — the man page is
            // misleading on the unit).  Persist on the shim fd as
            // ms so getsockopt round-trips the user's value;
            // push to inet when a slot exists, replay on slot
            // creation otherwise.
            if optname == 18
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    return i64(-ENOPROTOOPT)
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                if v < 0
                    v = 0
                posix_fd_table[tidx].tcp_user_timeout_ms = v
                val ut_inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                if ut_inet_fd >= 0
                    val rc = shim_inet_set_userto(ut_inet_fd, v)
                    if rc != 0
                        return i64(rc)
                return i64(0)
        i64(-ENOPROTOOPT)

`val` is a pointer to the option buffer; `len_ptr` points at an
i32 that the caller seeded with the buffer capacity. We write 0
For SO_SNDBUF/SO_RCVBUF the value comes from inet (or the persisted
pending size when there's no slot yet).  For the other accept-list
flags (SO_REUSEADDR / SO_BROADCAST / SO_KEEPALIVE / SO_REUSEPORT /
TCP_NODELAY) we echo the actually-set value out of the shim fd
table.  SO_ERROR returns the positive errno of the most recent
socket-layer failure (clearing it to 0) — Linux read-and-reset
semantics.  `*len_ptr = 4` in every accepted case.  `-ENOPROTOOPT`
is returned for anything outside the accept-list; the option buffer
is left untouched in that case.

    sys_getsockopt(fd: int, level: int, optname: int, optval: i64, len_ptr: i64) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)

`SO_PEERCRED` (level=SOL_SOCKET=1, optname=17) is the one
SOL_SOCKET option meaningful on AF_UNIX STREAM today. Route
it to UNIX_CMD_PEERCRED and write the 12-byte struct ucred
into the user buffer.

        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            if level == 1
                if optname == 17
                    if len_ptr == i64(0)
                        return i64(-EFAULT)
                    val plp: *i32 = *i32(len_ptr)
                    val pcap = int(*plp)
                    if pcap < 12
                        return i64(-EINVAL)
                    if optval == i64(0)
                        return i64(-EFAULT)
                    val ptarget = posix_fd_target(fd)
                    if ptarget < 0
                        return i64(-EBADF)
                    val pport = unix_get_port()
                    if pport < 0
                        return i64(-ENODEV)
                    var pmsg: [2]byte
                    pmsg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_PEERCRED)
                    pmsg[1] = byte(ptarget & 0xff)
                    var preply: [13]byte
                    val pr = ipc_send(pport, pmsg, 2, preply, 13)
                    if pr < 0
                        return i64(-EIO)
                    if preply[0] != 0
                        return i64(unix_status(preply[0]))
                    val pop: *byte = *byte(optval)
                    var pi = 0
                    while pi < 12
                        pop[pi] = preply[1 + pi]
                        pi += 1
                    *plp = i32(12)
                    return i64(0)
            return i64(-ENOPROTOOPT)
        if k != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                return i64(-EBADF)
        var accept = 0
        if level == 1
            if optname == 2
                accept = 1
            if optname == 3
                accept = 1
            if optname == 4
                accept = 1
            if optname == 6
                accept = 1
            if optname == 7
                accept = 1
            if optname == 8
                accept = 1
            if optname == 9
                accept = 1
            if optname == 13
                accept = 1
            if optname == 15
                accept = 1
            if optname == 20
                accept = 1
            if optname == 21
                accept = 1
            if optname == 30
                accept = 1
            if optname == 38
                accept = 1
            if optname == 39
                accept = 1
        if level == 6
            if optname == 1
                accept = 1
            if optname == 4 || optname == 5 || optname == 6
                accept = 1
            if optname == 11
                accept = 1
            if optname == 18
                accept = 1
        if accept == 0
            return i64(-ENOPROTOOPT)
        if len_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val lp: *i32 = *i32(len_ptr)
        val cap = int(*lp)
        // SO_LINGER returns 8 bytes, TCP_INFO can fill up to 104
        // (we truncate to caller's cap), everything else is an int.
        var need = 4
        if optname == 13
            need = 8
        if level == 1
            if optname == 20 || optname == 21
                need = 16
        if level == 6
            if optname == 11
                need = 1                // Linux fills as much as cap
        if cap < need
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if optval == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        // SO_LINGER: write back the persisted struct.  Pre-connect
        // / post-close fds carry the user's most recent set call
        // (or zero-init).  No inet round-trip needed since the
        // shim is the source of truth for linger state.
        if level == 1
            if optname == 13
                val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
                val lidx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                val on_ptr: *i32 = *i32(optval)
                val secs_ptr: *i32 = *i32(optval + i64(4))
                *on_ptr = i32(posix_fd_table[lidx].linger_on)
                *secs_ptr = i32(posix_fd_table[lidx].linger_secs)
                *lp = i32(8)
                return i64(0)
            if optname == 20 || optname == 21
                val tpid = posix_fd_current_pid()
                val tidx2 = tpid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                var ms = posix_fd_table[tidx2].recv_timeout_ms
                if optname == 21
                    ms = posix_fd_table[tidx2].send_timeout_ms
                val tsec = i64(ms / 1000)
                val tusec = i64((ms % 1000) * 1000)
                val tp: *byte = *byte(optval)
                var ti = 0
                while ti < 8
                    tp[ti] = byte(int((tsec >> i64(ti * 8))) & 0xff)
                    ti += 1
                ti = 0
                while ti < 8
                    tp[8 + ti] = byte(int((tusec >> i64(ti * 8))) & 0xff)
                    ti += 1
                *lp = i32(16)
                return i64(0)
        // TCP_INFO (level=IPPROTO_TCP=6, optname=11). Fetch the
        // 104-byte struct from inet via INET_CMD_TCP_GET_INFO and
        // copy as much as the caller's buffer holds — Linux
        // truncates silently rather than rejecting smaller bufs.
        if level == 6
            if optname == 11
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    return i64(-ENOPROTOOPT)
                val info_inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                if info_inet_fd < 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)         // pre-connect TCP fd
                val iport = inet_get_port()
                if iport < 0
                    return i64(-EIO)
                var imsg: [3]byte
                imsg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_GET_INFO)
                shim_write_u16(imsg, 1, info_inet_fd)
                var ireply: [105]byte
                val ir = ipc_send(iport, imsg, 3, ireply, 105)
                if ir < 0
                    return i64(-EIO)
                if ireply[0] != 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                val ubuf: *byte = *byte(optval)
                var cw = cap
                if cw > 104
                    cw = 104
                var ci = 0
                while ci < cw
                    ubuf[ci] = ireply[1 + ci]
                    ci += 1
                *lp = i32(cw)
                return i64(0)
        val vp: *i32 = *i32(optval)
        // Read-only introspection getsockopts. SO_TYPE / SO_PROTOCOL
        // are derived from the fd kind; SO_DOMAIN is always AF_INET
        // (slix has no AF_UNIX or AF_INET6); SO_ACCEPTCONN reflects
        // the per-fd is_listen flag set in sys_listen. Real Linux
        // also reports these and several libraries probe via them
        // (e.g. libuv's socket-type detection on accept'd fds).
        if level == 1
            if optname == 3
                // SO_TYPE: SOCK_STREAM = 1, SOCK_DGRAM = 2
                if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    *vp = i32(1)
                else
                    *vp = i32(2)
                *lp = i32(4)
                return i64(0)
            if optname == 30
                // SO_ACCEPTCONN: 1 iff post-listen, else 0
                val pid_ac = posix_fd_current_pid()
                val acidx = pid_ac * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                *vp = i32(posix_fd_table[acidx].is_listen)
                *lp = i32(4)
                return i64(0)
            if optname == 38
                // SO_PROTOCOL: IPPROTO_TCP = 6, IPPROTO_UDP = 17
                if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    *vp = i32(6)
                else
                    *vp = i32(17)
                *lp = i32(4)
                return i64(0)
            if optname == 39
                // SO_DOMAIN: always AF_INET (2)
                *vp = i32(2)
                *lp = i32(4)
                return i64(0)
        // SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF: ask the inet server for the actual
        // per-socket size.  Pre-connect TCP fds (no inet slot yet)
        // report the pending value the caller stashed via setsockopt
        // (or the inet-side default) so a setsockopt + getsockopt
        // round-trip on a fresh fd echoes the requested size — code
        // that introspects its own option ahead of bind/connect
        // wants a sane number.
        if level == 1
            if optname == 7 || optname == 8
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    *vp = i32(0)
                    *lp = i32(4)
                    return i64(0)
                val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                if inet_fd < 0
                    val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
                    val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                    var pending = 0
                    if optname == 7
                        pending = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_sndbuf
                        if pending == 0
                            pending = 4096
                    else
                        pending = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_rcvbuf
                        if pending == 0
                            pending = 1024
                    *vp = i32(pending)
                    *lp = i32(4)
                    return i64(0)
                val port = inet_get_port()
                if port < 0
                    return i64(-EIO)
                var msg: [4]byte
                msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_GETBUF)
                shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
                msg[3] = byte(0)
                if optname == 8
                    msg[3] = byte(1)
                var reply: [3]byte
                val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 3)
                if r < 0
                    return i64(-EIO)
                if reply[0] != 0
                    return i64(-EINVAL)
                *vp = i32(shim_read_u16(reply, 1))
                *lp = i32(4)
                return i64(0)
        // TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL / TCP_KEEPCNT.  Always answer
        // from the shim's persisted value — that's the source of
        // truth for what the user set, and round-tripping through
        // the inet ticks representation would lose the original
        // seconds (60 sec → 6000 ticks → 60 sec is fine, but small
        // values can drift if the user passes 0 / 1 in seconds and
        // we'd return the default rather than the persisted 0).
        if level == 6
            if optname == 4 || optname == 5 || optname == 6
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    *vp = i32(0)
                    *lp = i32(4)
                    return i64(0)
                val pid_k = posix_fd_current_pid()
                val kidx = pid_k * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                var kv = 0
                if optname == 4
                    kv = posix_fd_table[kidx].keep_idle_secs
                else if optname == 5
                    kv = posix_fd_table[kidx].keep_intvl_secs
                else
                    kv = posix_fd_table[kidx].keep_cnt
                *vp = i32(kv)
                *lp = i32(4)
                return i64(0)
            if optname == 18
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    *vp = i32(0)
                    *lp = i32(4)
                    return i64(0)
                val pid_u = posix_fd_current_pid()
                val uidx = pid_u * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                *vp = i32(posix_fd_table[uidx].tcp_user_timeout_ms)
                *lp = i32(4)
                return i64(0)
        // SO_ERROR — Linux read-and-clear semantics.  Returns the
        // positive errno of the most recent socket-layer failure
        // (set by sys_connect / sys_bind / sys_sendto / sys_recvfrom
        // when they return an error to the user) and resets to 0.
        // A fresh socket reads back 0; a socket whose previous
        // syscall failed reads back the errno once.
        //
        // For TCP sockets we also consult inet's `pending_error`
        // channel via `INET_CMD_TCP_GET_ERR`. That covers the
        // asynchronous-failure path where a connection error (RST in
        // SYN_SENT, post-handshake RST, retransmit/keepalive timeout)
        // happens *after* the user has stopped issuing syscalls and
        // is sitting in epoll — the shim's local `last_error` won't
        // catch those because no syscall returned the errno. Only
        // query when local is 0 so the local "most recent syscall
        // failure" has priority (and one read still clears both
        // sources atomically — inet clears as part of the read).
        if level == 1
            if optname == 4
                val pid_e = posix_fd_current_pid()
                val eidx = pid_e * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                var e_val = posix_fd_table[eidx].last_error
                posix_fd_table[eidx].last_error = 0
                if e_val == 0
                    if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                        val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                        if inet_fd >= 0
                            var ie = 0
                            val rr = shim_inet_get_tcp_err(inet_fd, ie)
                            if rr == 0
                                e_val = ie
                *vp = i32(e_val)
                *lp = i32(4)
                return i64(0)
        // Echo the persisted flag value for the accept-list options
        // (SO_REUSEADDR / SO_BROADCAST / SO_KEEPALIVE / SO_REUSEPORT
        // / TCP_NODELAY).
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val gidx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        var echoed = 0
        if level == 1
            if optname == 2
                echoed = posix_fd_table[gidx].flag_reuseaddr
            if optname == 6
                echoed = posix_fd_table[gidx].flag_broadcast
            if optname == 9
                echoed = posix_fd_table[gidx].flag_keepalive
            if optname == 15
                echoed = posix_fd_table[gidx].flag_reuseport
        if level == 6
            if optname == 1
                echoed = posix_fd_table[gidx].flag_nodelay
        *vp = i32(echoed)
        *lp = i32(4)
        i64(0)

SYS_GETSOCKNAME / SYS_GETPEERNAME. Both return a 16-byte
`sockaddr_in` at `addr_ptr` and write the length (always 16)
into `*addrlen_ptr`. For UDP: getsockname reports the bound
endpoint (or 0.0.0.0:0 if unbound); getpeername short-circuits
with -ENOTCONN since we don't support connected UDP. For TCP:
both flavours ask the inet server via INET_CMD_TCP_GETNAME and
relay its status byte. Unallocated TCP sockets (no inet-side
slot yet — e.g. right after socket(SOCK_STREAM) before
connect/listen) report `0.0.0.0:0` for getsockname and
-ENOTCONN for getpeername.

Shared helper that drives the actual IPC + sockaddr formatting;
the two entry points differ only in "which" flag and in UDP's
peer short-circuit.

    shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr: i64, port: int, ip_octets: *byte)
        if addr_ptr == i64(0)
            return
        val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
        a[0] = byte(POSIX_AF_INET & 0xff)
        a[1] = byte(0)
        a[2] = byte(port >> 8)
        a[3] = byte(port & 0xff)
        a[4] = ip_octets[0]
        a[5] = ip_octets[1]
        a[6] = ip_octets[2]
        a[7] = ip_octets[3]
        var z = 8
        while z < 16
            a[z] = byte(0)
            z += 1

    shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr: i64, n: int)
        if addrlen_ptr == i64(0)
            return
        val alp: *i32 = *i32(addrlen_ptr)
        *alp = i32(n)

Write a `sockaddr_un` into the caller's buffer and return the
addrlen value Linux would set:
  - empty path → addrlen = 2 (just the family field).
  - filesystem path (non-empty, path[0] != 0) → addrlen =
    2 + plen + 1 (family + path + trailing NUL).
  - abstract path (plen >= 1, path[0] == 0) → addrlen =
    2 + plen (family + path including the leading NUL, NO
    trailing NUL — Linux preserves abstract names exactly).

The path is bounded to UNIX_PATH_MAX bytes; the trailing NUL
on the filesystem branch is written when there's room (the
receive buffer is sockaddr_un which is 110 bytes, so
2 + UNIX_PATH_MAX still fits).

    shim_write_sockaddr_un(addr_ptr: i64, path: *byte, plen: int) -> int
        var abstract_ns = 0
        if plen >= 1
            if path[0] == byte(0)
                abstract_ns = 1
        if addr_ptr != i64(0)
            val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
            a[0] = byte(POSIX_AF_UNIX & 0xff)
            a[1] = byte(0)
            var pcopy = plen
            if pcopy > UNIX_PATH_MAX
                pcopy = UNIX_PATH_MAX
            var i = 0
            while i < pcopy
                a[2 + i] = path[i]
                i += 1
            if abstract_ns == 0
                if 2 + pcopy < 110
                    a[2 + pcopy] = byte(0)
        if plen == 0
            return 2
        if abstract_ns != 0
            return 2 + plen
        2 + plen + 1


    sys_getsockname(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if inet_fd < 0
                return i64(-EBADF)
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-ENODEV)
            var msg: [2]byte
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_UDP_GETNAME)
            msg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
            var reply: [7]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 7)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            if reply[0] != 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            val p = ((int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2]) & 0xff)
            shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr, p, &reply[3])
            shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, 16)
            return i64(0)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            val tcp_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if tcp_fd < 0
                // Unallocated TCP socket → 0.0.0.0:0 for getsockname.
                var zero: [4]byte
                zero[0] = byte(0)
                zero[1] = byte(0)
                zero[2] = byte(0)
                zero[3] = byte(0)
                shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr, 0, &zero[0])
                shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, 16)
                return i64(0)
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-ENODEV)
            var msg: [4]byte
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_GETNAME)
            shim_write_u16(msg, 1, tcp_fd)
            msg[3] = byte(0)               // which=0 → local
            var reply: [7]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 7)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            val raw = int(reply[0]) & 0xff
            if raw != 0
                if raw >= 128
                    return i64(raw - 256)
                return i64(raw)
            val p = ((int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2]) & 0xff)
            shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr, p, &reply[3])
            shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, 16)
            return i64(0)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            val unix_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if unix_fd < 0
                return i64(-EBADF)
            val port = unix_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-ENODEV)
            var msg: [2]byte
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_GETSOCKNAME)
            msg[1] = byte(unix_fd & 0xff)
            var reply: [128]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 128)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            val status = i8(reply[0])
            if int(status) != 0
                return i64(int(status))
            val plen = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
            val al = shim_write_sockaddr_un(addr_ptr, &reply[2], plen)
            shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, al)
            return i64(0)
        i64(-EBADF)

    sys_getpeername(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            // Linux: connected UDP fd returns its saved peer; an
            // unconnected UDP fd returns -ENOTCONN. The shim's
            // udp_connected/peer fields are the source of truth
            // since inet doesn't track per-socket peers for UDP.
            val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
            if posix_fd_table[idx].udp_connected == 0
                return i64(-ENOTCONN)
            var oct: [4]byte
            oct[0] = byte(posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_a & 0xff)
            oct[1] = byte(posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_b & 0xff)
            oct[2] = byte(posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_c & 0xff)
            oct[3] = byte(posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_d & 0xff)
            shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr, posix_fd_table[idx].udp_peer_port, &oct[0])
            shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, 16)
            return i64(0)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            val unix_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if unix_fd < 0
                return i64(-EBADF)
            val port = unix_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-ENODEV)
            var msg: [2]byte
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_GETPEERNAME)
            msg[1] = byte(unix_fd & 0xff)
            var reply: [128]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 128)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            val status = i8(reply[0])
            if int(status) != 0
                return i64(int(status))
            val plen = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
            val al = shim_write_sockaddr_un(addr_ptr, &reply[2], plen)
            shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, al)
            return i64(0)
        if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val tcp_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if tcp_fd < 0
            return i64(-ENOTCONN)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [4]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_GETNAME)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, tcp_fd)
        msg[3] = byte(1)                   // which=1 → peer
        var reply: [7]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 7)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        val status = i8(reply[0])
        if int(status) != 0
            return i64(int(status))
        val p = ((int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2]) & 0xff)
        shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr, p, &reply[3])
        shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, 16)
        i64(0)

SYS_DUP / SYS_DUP3. POSIX fd aliasing for the socket fd table.
The new fd shares `kind`, `target`, and `flags` with the old —
the inet record (or stdio kind) lives across both. Real refcount
is implicit: `sys_close` scans the rest of the table and skips
the inet close if any other slot still references the same
target. CLOEXEC (`flags & 1` in the dup3 call) is accepted but
not tracked, since SLIX has no `exec` yet.

`dup` allocates the lowest free fd via the standard table walk;
`dup3` writes the explicit `newfd`, closing whatever was there
first (also dup-aware via `sys_close`). dup3 with `oldfd ==
newfd` reports -EINVAL per Linux.

    sys_dup(oldfd: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(oldfd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return i64(-EBADF)
        val target = posix_fd_target(oldfd)
        val flags = posix_fd_flags(oldfd)
        val newfd = posix_fd_alloc(k, target)
        if newfd < 0
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        posix_fd_set_flags(newfd, flags)
        i64(newfd)

    sys_dup3(oldfd: int, newfd: int, dup_flags: int) -> i64
        if oldfd == newfd
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val k = posix_fd_kind(oldfd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if newfd < 0
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if newfd >= POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if posix_fd_kind(newfd) != POSIX_FD_FREE
            val _ = sys_close(newfd)
        val target = posix_fd_target(oldfd)
        val ofl = posix_fd_flags(oldfd)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + newfd
        posix_fd_table[idx].kind = k
        posix_fd_table[idx].target = target
        posix_fd_table[idx].flags = ofl
        posix_fd_table[idx].pos = i64(0)
        i64(newfd)

SYS_FCNTL. MVP dispatch for the handful of cmd codes musl programs
actually exercise before doing I/O:

- `F_GETFL` (3): return the slot's stored flags. Only O_NONBLOCK is
  meaningful today; other bits echo back whatever was set so
  `getfl | O_NONBLOCK` round-trips cleanly.
- `F_SETFL` (4): store `arg & O_NONBLOCK` in the slot. We mask so
  the shim can't be tricked into remembering unsupported flags and
  acting surprisingly later.
- `F_GETFD` (1) / `F_SETFD` (2): file-descriptor flags (mainly
  FD_CLOEXEC). SLIX has no `exec` yet, so CLOEXEC is a no-op.
  GETFD returns 0; SETFD accepts any value and returns 0. The
  day an exec path lands, this is the place to start tracking.
- `F_DUPFD` (0): not implemented — socket dup requires a separate
  fd-aliasing design (see POSIX-fd-bridge memory). Returns -EINVAL.

Unknown command → -EINVAL so real misuse surfaces.

    sys_fcntl(fd: int, cmd: int, arg: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if cmd == POSIX_F_GETFL
            return i64(posix_fd_flags(fd))
        if cmd == POSIX_F_SETFL
            posix_fd_set_flags(fd, arg & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
            return i64(0)
        if cmd == POSIX_F_GETFD
            return i64(0)
        if cmd == POSIX_F_SETFD
            return i64(0)
        if cmd == POSIX_F_DUPFD
            return i64(-EINVAL)             // no dup yet
        i64(-EINVAL)

SYS_OPENAT. The user passes `(dirfd, pathname, flags, mode)`. We
ignore `dirfd` (musl always sends `AT_FDCWD = -100` once the cwd
arc lands; for now the path must be absolute) and `mode` (file
creation isn't routed yet). The path is null-terminated in user
memory; we copy it into a stack buffer and forward to VFS as
`VFS_CMD_OPEN_C` (path-based, file scheme). Scheme-prefixed
paths ("tty:0", "tcp:host:port") would need `VFS_CMD_CONNECT_C`
instead, but that path requires VFS to route by command byte
which it doesn't today — adding it is a follow-up if we need
TTY/socket-via-open. The returned VFS handle is parked in a
fresh `POSIX_FD_FILE` slot.

SYS_FACCESSAT (159) / SYS_FACCESSAT2 (160). Path probe: does
the file exist + is it accessible. Slix has no real permission
model yet, so we treat "VFS opens it" as "accessible" — F_OK
(0) / R_OK (4) / W_OK (2) / X_OK (1) collapse into one probe.

Returns 0 on success, -ENOENT on miss.

    sys_faccessat(dirfd: int, path_ptr: i64, mode: int, flags: int) -> i64
        if path_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var path: [128]byte
        val up: *byte = *byte(path_ptr)
        var n = 0
        while n < 127
            val c = up[n]
            if c == byte(0)
                break
            path[n] = c
            n += 1
        var msg: [129]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_OPEN_C)
        var i = 0
        while i < n
            msg[1 + i] = path[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [9]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 1 + n, reply, 9)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-ENOENT)
        // Open succeeded — close the handle immediately, we just
        // wanted the existence check.
        val handle = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1)
        var cmsg: [5]byte
        cmsg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C)
        shim_write_be_i32(cmsg, 1, handle)
        var crep: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, cmsg, 5, crep, 1)
        i64(0)


    sys_openat(dirfd: int, path_ptr: i64, oflags: int, mode: int) -> i64
        if path_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var path: [128]byte
        val up: *byte = *byte(path_ptr)
        var n = 0
        while n < 127
            val c = up[n]
            if c == byte(0)
                break
            path[n] = c
            n += 1
        var msg: [129]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_OPEN_C)
        var i = 0
        while i < n
            msg[1 + i] = path[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [9]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 1 + n, reply, 9)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        val want_create = (oflags & POSIX_O_CREAT) != 0
        val want_excl   = (oflags & POSIX_O_EXCL)  != 0
        if reply[0] != 0
            if !want_create
                return i64(-ENOENT)
            // File doesn't exist and O_CREAT is set — ask VFS to
            // create it (TFS_CMD_CREATE goes via vfs_path_create
            // server-side, splitting the path into parent_ino +
            // name_in_parent), then retry the open. Mode bits get
            // the regular-file ftype (1) ORed in by TFS itself.
            val perm = mode & 0xFFF
            var cm: [131]byte
            cm[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CREATE_C)
            cm[1] = byte(1)             // TFS ftype = REG
            cm[2] = byte((perm >> 8) & 0xFF)
            cm[3] = byte(perm & 0xFF)
            var k = 0
            while k < n
                cm[4 + k] = path[k]
                k += 1
            var crep: [5]byte
            val cr = ipc_send(port, cm, 4 + n, crep, 5)
            if cr < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            if crep[0] != 0
                return i64(-ENOENT)          // parent missing
            // Now reopen — if THIS fails the FS is corrupt, but
            // -ENOENT is still the best Linux can return.
            var msg2: [129]byte
            msg2[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_OPEN_C)
            var j = 0
            while j < n
                msg2[1 + j] = path[j]
                j += 1
            val r2 = ipc_send(port, msg2, 1 + n, reply, 9)
            if r2 < 0
                return i64(-EIO)
            if reply[0] != 0
                return i64(-ENOENT)
        else if want_create && want_excl
            // File already exists and the caller asked for
            // exclusive creation — release the just-acquired VFS
            // handle before reporting -EEXIST so the slot doesn't
            // leak.
            val h = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1)
            var cmsg: [5]byte
            cmsg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C)
            shim_write_be_i32(cmsg, 1, h)
            var crep: [1]byte
            ipc_send(port, cmsg, 5, crep, 1)
            return i64(-EEXIST)
        val handle = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1)
        val pfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_FILE, handle)
        if pfd < 0
            // Couldn't park the handle — VFS already counted it,
            // so close the backend before reporting EMFILE.
            var cmsg: [5]byte
            cmsg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C)
            shim_write_be_i32(cmsg, 1, handle)
            var crep: [1]byte
            ipc_send(port, cmsg, 5, crep, 1)
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        posix_fd_set_flags(pfd, oflags & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
        i64(pfd)

SYS_LSEEK. VFS tracks file position server-side, so we just
forward `whence` (0=SEEK_SET, 1=SEEK_CUR; SEEK_END not yet
supported by VFS) and the offset. VFS replies with the new
absolute position. Non-file kinds (sockets, STDIO) report ESPIPE
the way Linux does for unseekable fds.

    sys_lseek(fd: int, offset: i64, whence: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if k != POSIX_FD_FILE
            return i64(-ESPIPE)
        val handle = posix_fd_target(fd)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [10]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_SEEK_C)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 1, handle)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 5, int(offset))
        msg[9] = byte(whence)
        var reply: [5]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 10, reply, 5)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        i64(shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1))

SYS_PIPE2. Asks VFS to allocate a pipe and hand back two
handles; we install both in fresh `POSIX_FD_FILE` slots and
write the pair into `pipefd_array[0..1]` in user memory. Flags
(`O_NONBLOCK` / `O_CLOEXEC`) are stored on each slot — non-block
takes effect on subsequent reads, CLOEXEC is a placeholder until
exec lands.

    sys_pipe2(pipefd_array: i64, pipe_flags: int) -> i64
        if pipefd_array == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [1]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_PIPE_C)
        var reply: [9]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 1, reply, 9)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        val rh = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1)
        val wh = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 5)
        val rfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_FILE, rh)
        val wfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_FILE, wh)
        if rfd < 0
            // Couldn't park the handles — close the backends so
            // VFS doesn't leak the pipe.
            var c1: [5]byte
            c1[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C)
            shim_write_be_i32(c1, 1, rh)
            var crep: [1]byte
            ipc_send(port, c1, 5, crep, 1)
            shim_write_be_i32(c1, 1, wh)
            ipc_send(port, c1, 5, crep, 1)
            if wfd >= 0
                posix_fd_clear(wfd)
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        if wfd < 0
            sys_close(rfd)
            var c1: [5]byte
            c1[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C)
            shim_write_be_i32(c1, 1, wh)
            var crep: [1]byte
            ipc_send(port, c1, 5, crep, 1)
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        val nb = pipe_flags & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        if nb != 0
            posix_fd_set_flags(rfd, nb)
            posix_fd_set_flags(wfd, nb)
        var fdp: *int = *int(pipefd_array)
        fdp[0] = rfd
        fdp[1] = wfd
        i64(0)

### Stat family

`stat` / `lstat` / `fstat` all funnel through musl's
`fstatat_kstat` path on slix. With the kstat header carrying
64-bit time fields, musl skips the `statx` first-attempt branch
and goes straight to the dedicated syscalls. We handle two:

- `SYS_fstat (178)` — fd-based; routes to `VFS_CMD_FSTAT`.
- `SYS_newfstatat (252)` — path-based with optional dirfd +
  flags. `AT_EMPTY_PATH` (0x1000) with `fd >= 0` and an empty
  path means "stat this fd"; we redirect to the fstat path.
  Otherwise we ignore `dirfd` (musl always passes `AT_FDCWD`
  before the cwd arc lands) and route to `VFS_CMD_STAT`.

`AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW` (0x100) is accepted-and-ignored — TFS
has no symlink concept yet, so the with-follow and no-follow
paths are identical.

VFS replies in TFS's encoded form: `mode = (ftype << 12) | perm`
where `ftype ∈ {1=REG, 2=DIR, 3=CHR, 5=FIFO, 6=SOCK}`. musl
uses Linux's encoded form (`S_IFREG = 0o100000`, etc.), so we
remap before packing the kstat. Anything outside the known
TFS table falls through to S_IFREG so a probe-only caller
(e.g. `fstat` on a freshly-opened pipe via `OFT_TYPE_PIPE`)
gets a sensible mode.

`AT_FDCWD` is musl's "interpret path as cwd-relative" sentinel,
defined as `-100`. We don't actually have a cwd yet; absolute
paths work, relative paths don't. The dirfd argument is
ignored either way.

    val POSIX_AT_FDCWD       = -100
    val POSIX_AT_EMPTY_PATH  = 0x1000
    val POSIX_AT_REMOVEDIR   = 0x200

    val POSIX_S_IFREG = 0x8000
    val POSIX_S_IFDIR = 0x4000
    val POSIX_S_IFCHR = 0x2000
    val POSIX_S_IFIFO = 0x1000
    val POSIX_S_IFBLK = 0x6000
    val POSIX_S_IFSOCK = 0xC000

    shim_translate_tfs_mode(tfs_mode: int) -> int
        val ftype = (tfs_mode >> 12) & 0xF
        val perm  = tfs_mode & 0xFFF
        var ifmt = POSIX_S_IFREG
        if ftype == 1
            ifmt = POSIX_S_IFREG
        else if ftype == 2
            ifmt = POSIX_S_IFDIR
        else if ftype == 3
            ifmt = POSIX_S_IFCHR
        else if ftype == 5
            ifmt = POSIX_S_IFIFO
        else if ftype == 6
            ifmt = POSIX_S_IFSOCK
        ifmt | perm

Take a 29-byte VFS stat reply and a user buffer; translate the
mode and pack the per-arch kstat. Returns 0 on success, -ENOENT
if VFS reported an error.

    shim_pack_stat_reply(reply: *byte, st_ptr: i64) -> i64
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-ENOENT)
        if st_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val tfs_mode = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1)
        val nlinks   = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 5)
        val uid      = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 9)
        val gid      = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 13)
        val size     = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 17)
        val mode     = shim_translate_tfs_mode(tfs_mode)
        val out: *byte = *byte(st_ptr)
        // The arch-specific packer rewrites the whole struct,
        // including its trailing pad. Inode comes from VFS's
        // OPEN reply; STAT itself doesn't carry one yet. Fill
        // 0 for now — `S_ISREG` and `st_size` are what real
        // callers need first.
        pack_kstat(out, mode, nlinks, uid, gid, i64(size), i64(0))
        i64(0)

    sys_fstat(fd: int, st_ptr: i64) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return i64(-EBADF)
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_FILE
            // Sockets / epoll / eventfd / timerfd: musl probes
            // these with fstat to populate libc's `_isatty` and
            // friends. Synthesise S_IFSOCK or S_IFREG with 0
            // size so the probe succeeds. Sticking with S_IFREG
            // is safest — Linux returns the same for sockets'
            // `fstat` on the `socket()` fd (the kernel reports
            // a synthetic socketfs inode).
            if st_ptr == i64(0)
                return i64(-EFAULT)
            val out: *byte = *byte(st_ptr)
            var mode = POSIX_S_IFREG | 0x1B6
            val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
            if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
                mode = POSIX_S_IFSOCK | 0x1B6
            else if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                mode = POSIX_S_IFSOCK | 0x1B6
            else if k == POSIX_FD_STDIN
                mode = POSIX_S_IFCHR | 0x1B6
            else if k == POSIX_FD_STDOUT
                mode = POSIX_S_IFCHR | 0x1B6
            else if k == POSIX_FD_STDERR
                mode = POSIX_S_IFCHR | 0x1B6
            pack_kstat(out, mode, 1, 0, 0, i64(0), i64(0))
            return i64(0)
        val handle = posix_fd_target(fd)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_FSTAT_C)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 1, handle)
        var reply: [29]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 29)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        shim_pack_stat_reply(reply, st_ptr)

    sys_newfstatat(dirfd: int, path_ptr: i64, st_ptr: i64, flag: int) -> i64
        // `AT_EMPTY_PATH` + valid fd + empty path = stat the fd.
        // musl's `fstat()` arrives via this path on arches that
        // lack a dedicated SYS_fstat — slix-musl does have one,
        // so this branch is mostly a defence-in-depth.
        if (flag & POSIX_AT_EMPTY_PATH) != 0
            if dirfd >= 0
                if path_ptr != i64(0)
                    val pp: *byte = *byte(path_ptr)
                    if pp[0] == byte(0)
                        return sys_fstat(dirfd, st_ptr)
                else
                    return sys_fstat(dirfd, st_ptr)
        if path_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var path: [128]byte
        val up: *byte = *byte(path_ptr)
        var n = 0
        while n < 127
            val c = up[n]
            if c == byte(0)
                break
            path[n] = c
            n += 1
        var msg: [129]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_STAT_C)
        var i = 0
        while i < n
            msg[1 + i] = path[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [29]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 1 + n, reply, 29)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        shim_pack_stat_reply(reply, st_ptr)

Phase 4 chunk 3 — `mkdir` / `unlink` / `rename` / `chmod`. musl
on slix routes the legacy syscalls through the `at`-suffixed
ones (`mkdir(p, m)` → `mkdirat(AT_FDCWD, p, m)`,
`rmdir(p)` → `unlinkat(AT_FDCWD, p, AT_REMOVEDIR)`, etc.), so
these four entry points are sufficient.

The shim copies the path into a stack buffer, builds the
matching VFS wire format, sends to the `fs` port, and returns
0/-ENOENT/-EFAULT/-ENODEV. `dirfd` is currently ignored — the
shim has no per-process cwd, so paths must be absolute.

`shim_copy_path_terminated(up, dst, max)` returns the byte
count copied (excluding the NUL); -1 on overflow. Single
helper so each wrapper below can stay short. The 128-byte
local stack buffer is reused everywhere — keeps the wrappers
parallel and matches `sys_openat` / `sys_newfstatat`.

    shim_copy_path_terminated(up: *byte, dst: *byte, max: int) -> int
        var n = 0
        while n < max - 1
            val c = up[n]
            if c == byte(0)
                return n
            dst[n] = c
            n += 1
        -1                      // overflow

`SYS_MKDIRAT` (227). Mode is the lower 12 bits — we forward it
verbatim to TFS (which stores the perm half of `tfs_mode` and
ORs with `ftype = 2 = DIR` server-side). VFS reply is
`[status(1), ino(4)]`; we ignore the ino and just return 0 on
success.

    sys_mkdirat(dirfd: int, path_ptr: i64, mode: int) -> i64
        if path_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var path: [128]byte
        val up: *byte = *byte(path_ptr)
        val n = shim_copy_path_terminated(up, path, 128)
        if n < 0
            return i64(-ENAMETOOLONG)
        val perm = mode & 0xFFF
        var msg: [131]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_MKDIR_C)
        msg[1] = byte((perm >> 8) & 0xFF)
        msg[2] = byte(perm & 0xFF)
        var i = 0
        while i < n
            msg[3 + i] = path[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [5]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 3 + n, reply, 5)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-ENOENT)              // parent missing or name in use
        i64(0)

`SYS_UNLINKAT` (365). `flags=AT_REMOVEDIR` (0x200) routes to
the directory remover; otherwise unlink the regular file at
`path`. Both VFS commands accept `[cmd(1), path(N)]` and reply
`[status(1)]`.

    sys_unlinkat(dirfd: int, path_ptr: i64, flags: int) -> i64
        if path_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var path: [128]byte
        val up: *byte = *byte(path_ptr)
        val n = shim_copy_path_terminated(up, path, 128)
        if n < 0
            return i64(-ENAMETOOLONG)
        var msg: [129]byte
        if (flags & POSIX_AT_REMOVEDIR) != 0
            msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_RMDIR_C)
        else
            msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_UNLINK_C)
        var i = 0
        while i < n
            msg[1 + i] = path[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 1 + n, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-ENOENT)
        i64(0)

`SYS_RENAMEAT` (281) and `SYS_RENAMEAT2` (282). The wire
format wedges both names into a single message:
`[cmd(1), old_len(1), old(N), new(M)]`. We bound each path at
120 bytes so the joint buffer stays under 256 (caller cap is
~512 in practice, but VFS itself reads into a 128-byte recv
buf so two 120s + header just fits the round trip).
`renameat2` ignores its `flags` arg — `RENAME_EXCHANGE` /
`RENAME_NOREPLACE` aren't supported by TFS yet.

    sys_renameat(olddirfd: int, oldpath_ptr: i64,
                 newdirfd: int, newpath_ptr: i64) -> i64
        if oldpath_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        if newpath_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var old: [120]byte
        var nw: [120]byte
        val oldp: *byte = *byte(oldpath_ptr)
        val newp: *byte = *byte(newpath_ptr)
        val ol = shim_copy_path_terminated(oldp, old, 120)
        if ol < 0
            return i64(-ENAMETOOLONG)
        val nl = shim_copy_path_terminated(newp, nw, 120)
        if nl < 0
            return i64(-ENAMETOOLONG)
        if ol > 255
            return i64(-ENAMETOOLONG)
        var msg: [248]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_RENAME_C)
        msg[1] = byte(ol)
        var i = 0
        while i < ol
            msg[2 + i] = old[i]
            i += 1
        var j = 0
        while j < nl
            msg[2 + ol + j] = nw[j]
            j += 1
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2 + ol + nl, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-ENOENT)
        i64(0)

`SYS_FCHMODAT` (167). `flags=AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW` (0x100) is
ignored — TFS doesn't have symlinks. The wire format is the
same shape as `MKDIR_C`: `[cmd(1), perm_hi(1), perm_lo(1),
path(N)]`.

    sys_fchmodat(dirfd: int, path_ptr: i64, mode: int, flags: int) -> i64
        if path_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-ENODEV)
        var path: [128]byte
        val up: *byte = *byte(path_ptr)
        val n = shim_copy_path_terminated(up, path, 128)
        if n < 0
            return i64(-ENAMETOOLONG)
        val perm = mode & 0xFFF
        var msg: [131]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CHMOD_C)
        msg[1] = byte((perm >> 8) & 0xFF)
        msg[2] = byte(perm & 0xFF)
        var i = 0
        while i < n
            msg[3 + i] = path[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 3 + n, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-EIO)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-ENOENT)
        i64(0)

### Epoll → inet subscription helpers

`epoll_inet_kind_for_fd` returns the inet-side `kind` byte
(1=UDP, 2=TCP) for a watched POSIX fd, or 0 if it's not a
socket — files / pipes / stdio don't talk to inet, so the SUB
roundtrip is skipped for them and they fall back to the
always-ready stopgap in `epoll_query_ready`.

    epoll_inet_kind_for_fd(target_fd: int) -> int
        val k = posix_fd_kind(target_fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            return 1
        if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return 2
        0

Send `INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB` for a watched socket fd. Inet's
subscriber table is keyed on (sender_tid, kind, inet_fd,
epoll_inst); duplicates are no-ops so we don't have to
remember whether we've already subscribed. Errors are
swallowed — the worst case is that wake-on-edge degrades to
the polling fallback (which `sys_epoll_pwait` keeps doing on
timeouts anyway).

    epoll_inet_subscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        val kind = epoll_inet_kind_for_fd(target_fd)
        if kind == 0
            return
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB)
        msg[1] = byte(kind)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 2, posix_fd_target(target_fd))
        msg[4] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 1)

    epoll_inet_unsubscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        val kind = epoll_inet_kind_for_fd(target_fd)
        if kind == 0
            return
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB)
        msg[1] = byte(kind)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 2, posix_fd_target(target_fd))
        msg[4] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 1)

`epoll_inst_close_inet` is called when the epoll fd itself
closes — INST_CLOSE drops every subscriber inet was holding for
this (sender_tid, inst) pair in one IPC, instead of looping
through entries with UNSUB.

    epoll_inst_close_inet(inst: int)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [2]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE)
        msg[1] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 1)


`epoll_unix_subscribe` / `_unsubscribe` / `_inst_close_unix`
mirror the inet helpers for AF_UNIX-backed fds. Wire layout:
`[cmd(1), unix_fd(1), inst(1)]`. The unix server's
subscriber table is keyed on (sender_tid, unix_fd, inst).

    epoll_unix_subscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        if posix_fd_kind(target_fd) != POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            return
        val target = posix_fd_target(target_fd)
        if target < 0
            return
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [3]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_SUB)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 1)

    epoll_unix_unsubscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        if posix_fd_kind(target_fd) != POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            return
        val target = posix_fd_target(target_fd)
        if target < 0
            return
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [3]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB)
        msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 1)

    epoll_inst_close_unix(inst: int)
        val port = unix_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [2]byte
        msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE)
        msg[1] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 1)


`epoll_vfs_subscribe` mirrors the inet helper for VFS-backed fds
(POSIX_FD_FILE — pipes, regular files, TTYs). VFS resolves the
shipped handle to its `oft_idx` and keys the subscription on
`(oft_idx, sender_tid, inst)` so two fds aliasing the same OFT
(e.g. dup'd pipe ends) share one wake.

The wire layout matches `INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB` after the cmd byte:
  [cmd(1), handle_be(4), inst(1)]
which is `vfs_msg_write_i32`-format (big-endian) for the handle
to stay consistent with the rest of the VFS protocol.

    epoll_vfs_subscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        if posix_fd_kind(target_fd) != POSIX_FD_FILE
            return
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        val handle = posix_fd_target(target_fd)
        var msg: [6]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_EPOLL_SUB_C)
        msg[1] = byte((handle >> 24) & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte((handle >> 16) & 0xff)
        msg[3] = byte((handle >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[4] = byte(handle & 0xff)
        msg[5] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 6, reply, 1)

    epoll_vfs_unsubscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        if posix_fd_kind(target_fd) != POSIX_FD_FILE
            return
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        val handle = posix_fd_target(target_fd)
        var msg: [6]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB_C)
        msg[1] = byte((handle >> 24) & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte((handle >> 16) & 0xff)
        msg[3] = byte((handle >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[4] = byte(handle & 0xff)
        msg[5] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 6, reply, 1)

    epoll_inst_close_vfs(inst: int)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [2]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE_C)
        msg[1] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 1)


`epoll_tty_subscribe` covers POSIX_FD_STDIN. The TTY server
keys subscriptions on (console, sender_tid, inst); we hardcode
console=0 since slix's per-process tty assignment is single-
console today.

    epoll_tty_subscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        if posix_fd_kind(target_fd) != POSIX_FD_STDIN
            return
        val port = tty_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [3]byte
        msg[0] = byte(TTY_CMD_EPOLL_SUB_C)
        msg[1] = byte(0)        // console
        msg[2] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 1)

    epoll_tty_unsubscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        if posix_fd_kind(target_fd) != POSIX_FD_STDIN
            return
        val port = tty_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [3]byte
        msg[0] = byte(TTY_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB_C)
        msg[1] = byte(0)
        msg[2] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 1)

    epoll_inst_close_tty(inst: int)
        val port = tty_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [2]byte
        msg[0] = byte(TTY_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE_C)
        msg[1] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 1)


Combined sub/unsub helpers — single call site for the
`epoll_ctl` path, no need to teach it about the kind/owner
split. Each underlying helper short-circuits if the fd doesn't
belong to it, so calling both is safe and idempotent.

    epoll_subscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        epoll_inet_subscribe(target_fd, inst)
        epoll_vfs_subscribe(target_fd, inst)
        epoll_tty_subscribe(target_fd, inst)
        epoll_unix_subscribe(target_fd, inst)

    epoll_unsubscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        epoll_inet_unsubscribe(target_fd, inst)
        epoll_vfs_unsubscribe(target_fd, inst)
        epoll_tty_unsubscribe(target_fd, inst)
        epoll_unix_unsubscribe(target_fd, inst)

    epoll_inst_close_all(inst: int)
        epoll_inst_close_inet(inst)
        epoll_inst_close_vfs(inst)
        epoll_inst_close_tty(inst)
        epoll_inst_close_unix(inst)


SYS_EPOLL_CREATE1. Allocates a fresh epoll instance and parks
it in a `POSIX_FD_EPOLL` slot. `flags` would carry
`EPOLL_CLOEXEC` / `EPOLL_NONBLOCK`; we honour O_NONBLOCK in
the slot but exec/CLOEXEC isn't tracked yet — see the
posix_fd_bridge memory.

SYS_EVENTFD2 (slix-musl syscall 156, sits next to the
epoll triplet at 153/154/155). Allocates an
eventfd backing slot with `initval` as the starting counter.
`flags` honors:

- `EFD_NONBLOCK` (0x800 = 2048) — same bit as `O_NONBLOCK`,
  recorded on the slot's flags so read/write pick the
  EAGAIN path on starvation.
- `EFD_CLOEXEC`  (0x80000 = 524288) — slix doesn't track CLOEXEC
  yet; accepted and ignored (matches the existing accept4 stance).
- `EFD_SEMAPHORE` (1) — read decrements the counter by 1 instead
  of returning the full count and zeroing it.

Returns the allocated POSIX fd, or -EMFILE if no slot is free.

    val POSIX_EFD_SEMAPHORE = 1
    val POSIX_EFD_CLOEXEC   = 524288
    val POSIX_EFD_NONBLOCK  = 2048

    sys_eventfd2(initval: int, flags: int) -> i64
        val pfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_EVENTFD, 0)
        if pfd < 0
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + pfd
        posix_fd_table[idx].eventfd_count = i64(initval)
        var sem = 0
        if (flags & POSIX_EFD_SEMAPHORE) != 0
            sem = 1
        posix_fd_table[idx].eventfd_semaphore = sem
        var fd_flags = 0
        if (flags & POSIX_EFD_NONBLOCK) != 0
            fd_flags = fd_flags | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        posix_fd_set_flags(pfd, fd_flags)
        i64(pfd)


SYS_TIMERFD_CREATE / SETTIME / GETTIME (slix-musl 356/357/358).
Periodic / one-shot kernel-managed timer fd. Read returns the
number of expirations since the last read; epoll EPOLLIN fires
when expirations > 0.

Slix runs the scheduler at 100 Hz, so sub-10 ms precision rounds
down to whole ticks. `CLOCK_REALTIME` and `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` are
both backed by `uptime()` here — slix has no wall-clock-vs-
monotonic distinction, so the two clockids resolve to the same
tick stream.  Programs that depend on a real wall clock (e.g.
DST handling) won't get one until a real RTC bridge lands.

Time conversion: 1 tick = 10 ms = 10_000_000 ns. We round down
on the nsec → tick conversion, which means a `tv_nsec=5_000_000`
(5 ms) request lands at "next whole tick" and may fire up to
10 ms late.  Programs needing finer precision lose nothing they
weren't going to lose to scheduler quanta anyway.

`flags` (TFD_NONBLOCK = 2048, TFD_CLOEXEC = 524288) — same bits
as O_NONBLOCK / O_CLOEXEC. Non-blocking is honoured; CLOEXEC
accept-and-ignore (matches accept4 / eventfd2).

`itimerspec` is a 32-byte LP64 struct: it_interval{tv_sec(8),
tv_nsec(8)} + it_value{tv_sec(8), tv_nsec(8)}.

    val POSIX_TFD_NONBLOCK  = 2048
    val POSIX_TFD_CLOEXEC   = 524288
    val POSIX_TFD_TIMER_ABSTIME = 1
    val POSIX_CLOCK_REALTIME  = 0
    val POSIX_CLOCK_MONOTONIC = 1

    shim_itimerspec_to_ticks(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        // Read tv_sec (low 8B) + tv_nsec (high 8B). Slix's tick
        // resolution is 10 ms so we don't need the high half of
        // tv_sec for any sane timer; clamp to int range.
        var sec_lo = 0
        var bi = 0
        while bi < 4
            sec_lo = sec_lo | ((int(p[off + bi]) & 0xff) << (bi * 8))
            bi += 1
        var nsec_lo = 0
        bi = 0
        while bi < 4
            nsec_lo = nsec_lo | ((int(p[off + 8 + bi]) & 0xff) << (bi * 8))
            bi += 1
        if sec_lo < 0
            sec_lo = 0
        if nsec_lo < 0
            nsec_lo = 0
        sec_lo * 100 + (nsec_lo / 10000000)

    shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(p: *byte, off: int, ticks: int)
        // Write tv_sec (8B LE) + tv_nsec (8B LE). Use i64 for the
        // shift-and-mask loop so byte-extraction past bit 31 doesn't
        // hit the i32 shift-by-≥-width undefined-behaviour path some
        // sysl backends fold into a no-op.
        val sec = i64(ticks / 100)
        val ns = i64((ticks % 100) * 10000000)
        var bi = 0
        while bi < 8
            p[off + bi] = byte(int((sec >> i64(bi * 8))) & 0xff)
            bi += 1
        bi = 0
        while bi < 8
            p[off + 8 + bi] = byte(int((ns >> i64(bi * 8))) & 0xff)
            bi += 1


Catch up `expirations` to current uptime: every full `interval`
between `next_tick` and now becomes one expiration. Returns
without doing anything if the timer is disarmed or hasn't fired
yet. Called lazily from sys_read / epoll_query_ready so the timer
state is always coherent at the moment the user observes it.

    timerfd_advance(idx: int)
        if posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick == 0
            return
        val now = int(uptime())
        if now < posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick
            return
        val interval = posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_interval_ticks
        if interval <= 0
            // One-shot timer: a single expiration, then disarm.
            val cur1 = posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_expirations
            posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_expirations = cur1 + i64(1)
            posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick = 0
            return
        // Periodic: catch up by however many full intervals have
        // elapsed past `next_tick`.  +1 covers the boundary tick
        // itself.
        val past = now - posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick
        val fired = (past / interval) + 1
        val cur2 = posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_expirations
        posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_expirations = cur2 + i64(fired)
        val nt = posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick
        posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick = nt + fired * interval


    sys_timerfd_create(clockid: int, flags: int) -> i64
        if clockid != POSIX_CLOCK_REALTIME
            if clockid != POSIX_CLOCK_MONOTONIC
                return i64(-EINVAL)
        val pfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_TIMERFD, 0)
        if pfd < 0
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        var fd_flags = 0
        if (flags & POSIX_TFD_NONBLOCK) != 0
            fd_flags = fd_flags | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        posix_fd_set_flags(pfd, fd_flags)
        i64(pfd)


    sys_timerfd_settime(fd: int, flags: int, new_value: i64, old_value: i64) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_TIMERFD
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if new_value == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val pid_t = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val tidx = pid_t * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        // Catch up before snapshotting old_value so any
        // unobserved expirations are accounted to the previous
        // configuration.
        timerfd_advance(tidx)
        // Stash old value if requested (interval, then remaining
        // time-to-fire).
        if old_value != i64(0)
            val oldp: *byte = *byte(old_value)
            shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(oldp, 0,
                posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_interval_ticks)
            var remaining = 0
            if posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick > 0
                remaining = posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick - int(uptime())
                if remaining < 0
                    remaining = 0
            shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(oldp, 16, remaining)
        val newp: *byte = *byte(new_value)
        val it_interval_ticks = shim_itimerspec_to_ticks(newp, 0)
        val it_value_ticks = shim_itimerspec_to_ticks(newp, 16)
        // it_value == 0 disarms the timer (Linux semantics).
        if it_value_ticks <= 0
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_interval_ticks = 0
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick = 0
            return i64(0)
        // TFD_TIMER_ABSTIME: it_value is an absolute tick (treating
        // tv_sec*100+tv_nsec/1e7 as ticks-since-boot).  Otherwise
        // it_value is relative to now.  Slix collapses both clocks
        // to uptime() so the meaning of "absolute" matches whatever
        // the caller expects from CLOCK_MONOTONIC.
        if (flags & POSIX_TFD_TIMER_ABSTIME) != 0
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick = it_value_ticks
        else
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick = int(uptime()) + it_value_ticks
        posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_interval_ticks = it_interval_ticks
        // Reset expirations to 0 on rearm (Linux: setttime starts
        // fresh; the read counter is wiped).
        posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations = i64(0)
        i64(0)


    sys_timerfd_gettime(fd: int, curr_value: i64) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_TIMERFD
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if curr_value == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val pid_t = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val tidx = pid_t * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        // Catch up so the snapshot reflects the live timer state.
        timerfd_advance(tidx)
        val cp: *byte = *byte(curr_value)
        shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(cp, 0,
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_interval_ticks)
        var remaining = 0
        if posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick > 0
            remaining = posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick - int(uptime())
            if remaining < 0
                remaining = 0
        shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(cp, 16, remaining)
        i64(0)


### POSIX time syscalls

`clock_gettime` / `gettimeofday` / `clock_getres` / `nanosleep` /
`clock_nanosleep`. All clocks (REALTIME, MONOTONIC, BOOTTIME,
PROCESS_CPUTIME, THREAD_CPUTIME, *_RAW, *_COARSE) collapse to
the kernel's `uptime()` tick counter at 100 Hz — slix has no
wallclock or per-thread CPU accounting yet, but apps that
just need a monotonic timestamp (the overwhelming majority)
keep working. timerfd already takes the same shortcut.

    sys_clock_gettime(clockid: int, tp: i64) -> i64
        if tp == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val p: *byte = *byte(tp)
        shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(p, 0, int(uptime()))
        i64(0)

    sys_clock_getres(clockid: int, tp: i64) -> i64
        if tp == i64(0)
            return i64(0)               // Linux: NULL is allowed for clock_getres
        val p: *byte = *byte(tp)
        // Resolution = 10ms = 10_000_000 ns. Reuse the helper
        // by passing 1 tick.
        shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(p, 0, 1)
        i64(0)

`gettimeofday` predates `clock_gettime`. Layout differs by one
field: `tv_usec` (microseconds) instead of `tv_nsec`. The
`tz` arg has been deprecated since the 1990s — Linux ignores
it but accepts the pointer. We do the same.

    sys_gettimeofday(tv: i64, tz: i64) -> i64
        if tv == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val p: *byte = *byte(tv)
        val ticks = int(uptime())
        val sec = i64(ticks / 100)
        val usec = i64((ticks % 100) * 10000)
        var bi = 0
        while bi < 8
            p[bi] = byte(int((sec >> i64(bi * 8))) & 0xff)
            bi += 1
        bi = 0
        while bi < 8
            p[8 + bi] = byte(int((usec >> i64(bi * 8))) & 0xff)
            bi += 1
        // tz: write {0, 0} if non-null. Most callers pass NULL
        // these days but glibc still accepts it.
        if tz != i64(0)
            val tzp: *byte = *byte(tz)
            var ti = 0
            while ti < 8
                tzp[ti] = byte(0)
                ti += 1
        i64(0)

`nanosleep` and `clock_nanosleep` block for the requested
duration, rounded up to the next 10ms tick. We don't have
signals so the call always completes — `rem` (if non-null) is
zeroed to indicate "no time left". TIMER_ABSTIME (clock_nanosleep
flag = 1) means the request is an absolute deadline — convert
to relative by subtracting `uptime()`.

    shim_timespec_to_ticks(p: *byte) -> int
        // Read tv_sec (i64) + tv_nsec (i64), convert to a
        // tick count rounded up so a 1ns sleep yields 1 tick
        // (10 ms) instead of 0.
        var sec = i64(0)
        var i = 7
        while i >= 0
            sec = (sec << i64(8)) | i64(int(p[i]) & 0xff)
            i -= 1
        var nsec = i64(0)
        i = 7
        while i >= 0
            nsec = (nsec << i64(8)) | i64(int(p[8 + i]) & 0xff)
            i -= 1
        // ticks = sec*100 + ceil(nsec / 10_000_000)
        val tick_from_sec = int(sec) * 100
        val tick_from_ns = int((nsec + i64(9999999)) / i64(10000000))
        var t = tick_from_sec + tick_from_ns
        if t < 0
            t = 0
        t

    sys_nanosleep(req: i64, rem: i64) -> i64
        if req == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val rp: *byte = *byte(req)
        val ticks = shim_timespec_to_ticks(rp)
        if ticks > 0
            sleep(ticks)
        if rem != i64(0)
            val remp: *byte = *byte(rem)
            var ri = 0
            while ri < 16
                remp[ri] = byte(0)
                ri += 1
        i64(0)

### getrandom

Non-cryptographic xorshift64* PRNG seeded from `uptime()` plus
a per-call counter. Real `getrandom(2)` taps the kernel CSPRNG;
slix has no entropy source today, so this is best-effort
"different bytes every call" useful for non-crypto consumers
(libuv buffer cookies, malloc-debug salts, etc.). Crypto code
should not rely on this — once a real entropy source lands,
swap the seed/state generator.

`flags` is accepted but ignored: GRND_NONBLOCK (1) is naturally
satisfied (we never block), GRND_RANDOM (2) is irrelevant
without a real CSPRNG. Returns the byte count on success,
-EFAULT on bad buffer.

    var rng_state: i64 = i64(0)

    rng_step() -> i64
        if rng_state == i64(0)
            rng_state = i64(uptime()) | i64(0x9E3779B97F4A7C15)
        var x = rng_state
        x = x ^ (x << i64(13))
        x = x ^ (i64(u64(x) >> u64(7)))
        x = x ^ (x << i64(17))
        rng_state = x
        x

    sys_getrandom(buf: i64, len: int, flags: int) -> i64
        if buf == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        if len < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val p: *byte = *byte(buf)
        var written = 0
        while written < len
            val r = rng_step()
            var bi = 0
            while bi < 8 && written + bi < len
                p[written + bi] = byte(int((r >> i64(bi * 8))) & 0xff)
                bi += 1
            written += bi
        i64(written)


    sys_clock_nanosleep(clockid: int, flags: int, req: i64, rem: i64) -> i64
        if req == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val rp: *byte = *byte(req)
        var ticks = shim_timespec_to_ticks(rp)
        // TIMER_ABSTIME = 1: req is an absolute time. Convert to
        // relative by subtracting current uptime.
        if (flags & 1) != 0
            val now_t = int(uptime())
            ticks = ticks - now_t
            if ticks < 0
                ticks = 0
        if ticks > 0
            sleep(ticks)
        if rem != i64(0)
            val remp: *byte = *byte(rem)
            var ri = 0
            while ri < 16
                remp[ri] = byte(0)
                ri += 1
        i64(0)


SYS_PREAD64 (260) / SYS_PWRITE64 (268). Positional read/write
on file fds. POSIX semantics: do the I/O at `offset` without
disturbing the fd's current position. Used by sqlite, page-
cache layers, log writers — any code that holds multiple
positions on one fd or does parallel-style I/O.

We synthesize the positional behavior by saving the fd's
current pos via SEEK_CUR, seeking to the requested offset,
doing the read/write, then restoring pos. 3-4 IPC round-trips
per call but POSIX-correct. A future VFS_CMD_PREAD/PWRITE
extension would cut to 1.

Only POSIX_FD_FILE is supported; sockets / fifos return
-ESPIPE (Linux behavior).

    sys_pread64(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int, offset: i64) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_FILE
            return i64(-ESPIPE)
        if offset < i64(0)
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        // Save current pos; SEEK_CUR=1 with offset=0 just reads it.
        val saved = sys_lseek(fd, i64(0), 1)
        if saved < i64(0)
            return saved
        val sk = sys_lseek(fd, offset, 0)            // SEEK_SET
        if sk < i64(0)
            return sk
        val n = sys_read(fd, buf, len)
        // Restore pos best-effort. If the seek-back fails, prefer
        // returning the read result over the seek error — most apps
        // never re-read from this fd, so a subtle pos drift is less
        // bad than swallowing the data.
        sys_lseek(fd, saved, 0)
        n

    sys_pwrite64(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int, offset: i64) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_FILE
            return i64(-ESPIPE)
        if offset < i64(0)
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val saved = sys_lseek(fd, i64(0), 1)
        if saved < i64(0)
            return saved
        val sk = sys_lseek(fd, offset, 0)
        if sk < i64(0)
            return sk
        val n = sys_write(fd, buf, len)
        sys_lseek(fd, saved, 0)
        n


SYS_WRITEV (372) / SYS_READV (274). POSIX vectored I/O over an
`iovec[]` array. Each entry is `{iov_base: *void, iov_len: size_t}`
= 16 bytes on LP64. We walk the iovs and call sys_write/sys_read
per segment.

Linux semantics: short write/read on the first iov returns the
short count immediately (don't continue with later iovs). Errors
on the first iov return -errno; later-iov errors return whatever
was already transferred. Zero-length iovs are skipped.

`SHIM_IOV_MAX = 8` cap matches sendmsg/recvmsg.

    sys_writev(fd: int, iov_ptr: i64, iovcnt: int) -> i64
        if iovcnt < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iovcnt > SHIM_IOV_MAX
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iovcnt == 0
            return i64(0)
        if iov_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val iov: *byte = *byte(iov_ptr)
        var total: i64 = i64(0)
        var ii = 0
        while ii < iovcnt
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val base = shim_read_ptr(iov, ent_off)
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len < 0
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            if seg_len > 0
                if base == i64(0)
                    return i64(-EFAULT)
                val n = sys_write(fd, base, seg_len)
                if n < i64(0)
                    if total > i64(0)
                        return total
                    return n
                total = total + n
                if int(n) < seg_len
                    return total
            ii += 1
        total

    sys_readv(fd: int, iov_ptr: i64, iovcnt: int) -> i64
        if iovcnt < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iovcnt > SHIM_IOV_MAX
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if iovcnt == 0
            return i64(0)
        if iov_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val iov: *byte = *byte(iov_ptr)
        var total: i64 = i64(0)
        var ii = 0
        while ii < iovcnt
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val base = shim_read_ptr(iov, ent_off)
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len < 0
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            if seg_len > 0
                if base == i64(0)
                    return i64(-EFAULT)
                val n = sys_read(fd, base, seg_len)
                if n < i64(0)
                    if total > i64(0)
                        return total
                    return n
                if n == i64(0)
                    return total
                total = total + n
                if int(n) < seg_len
                    return total
            ii += 1
        total


`brk(addr)` — manage the process program break. The slix-musl
linker script (slix/test/slix-prog.ld) places `_heap_start` at a
fixed virtual address `POSIX_BRK_BASE` and `_heap_end` at
`POSIX_BRK_LIMIT`; both correspond to physical pages already
mapped by `vm_create_process_pt`, so brk never has to allocate
pages — it just bookkeeps the current break per pid.

Linux semantics: `brk(0)` returns the current break. `brk(new)`
attempts to set the break to `new`; on success it returns `new`,
on failure (out of range) it returns the OLD break unchanged.
Musl's lite_malloc relies on the equality check
`__syscall(SYS_brk, brk+req) == brk+req` to detect success.

    val POSIX_BRK_BASE: i64  = i64(0x60040000)
    val POSIX_BRK_LIMIT: i64 = i64(0x60080000)

    var posix_break_addr: [16]i64
    var posix_break_last_gen: [16]int

    posix_brk_ensure_init(pid: int)
        val gen = kernel_get_process_main_tid(pid)
        if posix_break_last_gen[pid] != gen
            posix_break_addr[pid] = POSIX_BRK_BASE
            posix_break_last_gen[pid] = gen

    sys_brk(new_brk: i64) -> i64
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return i64(0)
        if pid >= 16
            return i64(0)
        posix_brk_ensure_init(pid)
        val cur = posix_break_addr[pid]
        if new_brk == i64(0)
            return cur
        if new_brk < POSIX_BRK_BASE
            return cur
        if new_brk > POSIX_BRK_LIMIT
            return cur
        posix_break_addr[pid] = new_brk
        new_brk


    sys_epoll_create1(epoll_flags: int) -> i64
        val inst = epoll_alloc_inst()
        if inst < 0
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        val pfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_EPOLL, inst)
        if pfd < 0
            epoll_inst_release(inst)
            return i64(-EMFILE)
        posix_fd_set_flags(pfd, epoll_flags & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
        i64(pfd)

`epoll_event` field readers. LP64 layout is `events: u32 @0`,
padding @4, `data: u64 @8`. We need byte-level reads because the
user pointer might not be aligned.

    shim_read_le_u32(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        (int(p[off]) & 0xff) | ((int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff) << 8) | ((int(p[off + 2]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(p[off + 3]) & 0xff) << 24)

    shim_write_le_u32(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte(v & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 2] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        p[off + 3] = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)

    shim_read_le_i64(p: *byte, off: int) -> i64
        var v = i64(0)
        var i = 7
        while i >= 0
            v = (v << i64(8)) | i64(int(p[off + i]) & 0xff)
            i -= 1
        v

    shim_write_le_i64(p: *byte, off: int, v: i64)
        var i = 0
        while i < 8
            p[off + i] = byte(int((v >> i64(8 * i))) & 0xff)
            i += 1

SYS_EPOLL_CTL. ADD finds a free entry (and rejects if `fd` is
already registered, like Linux); MOD updates an existing
entry's events/data; DEL drops it. Returns 0 on success, errno
on failure. We don't validate that `fd` is one of the kinds we
can actually poll (sockets, pipes, files) because epoll_pwait
will just see "always not ready" for unknown kinds — same
end-user effect as Linux's "polled but never fires" for fds
without poll support.

    sys_epoll_ctl(epfd: int, op: int, target_fd: int, ev_ptr: i64) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(epfd) != POSIX_FD_EPOLL
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val inst = posix_fd_target(epfd)
        if inst < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        var events_in = 0
        var data_in = i64(0)
        if op != POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_DEL
            if ev_ptr == i64(0)
                return i64(-EFAULT)
            val up: *byte = *byte(ev_ptr)
            events_in = shim_read_le_u32(up, 0)
            data_in = shim_read_le_i64(up, 8)
        if op == POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_ADD
            // Reject duplicate fd registrations.
            var i = 0
            while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
                if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use != 0
                    if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].watched_fd == target_fd
                        return i64(-EEXIST)
                i += 1
            i = 0
            while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
                if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use == 0
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use = 1
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].watched_fd = target_fd
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].events = events_in
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].data_lo = data_in
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_reported = 0
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].armed = 1
                    // Start at 0; the first POLL will sync against
                    // the server's current value. If the fd already
                    // had fires before ADD, the first scan sees seq
                    // advanced and (correctly) treats the current
                    // ready bits as a fresh edge.
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_seen_seq = 0
                    epoll_subscribe(target_fd, inst)
                    return i64(0)
                i += 1
            return i64(-ENOSPC)
        if op == POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_MOD
            var i = 0
            while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
                if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use != 0
                    if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].watched_fd == target_fd
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].events = events_in
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].data_lo = data_in
                        // MOD re-arms a one-shot entry and resets the
                        // edge-trigger memory so the next ready check
                        // can fire even if the bits look the same as
                        // before — matches Linux's MOD-rearm semantics.
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_reported = 0
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].armed = 1
                        // Treat MOD as a fresh-edge boundary: forget
                        // the cached seq so the next POLL re-syncs.
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_seen_seq = 0
                        return i64(0)
                i += 1
            return i64(-ENOENT)
        if op == POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_DEL
            var i = 0
            while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
                if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use != 0
                    if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].watched_fd == target_fd
                        epoll_unsubscribe(target_fd, inst)
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use = 0
                        return i64(0)
                i += 1
            return i64(-ENOENT)
        i64(-EINVAL)

Query a single watched fd's readiness. Returns the bits the
shim should report, masked against the user's requested events
+ EPOLLERR/EPOLLHUP (which are always reported regardless of
what the user asked for, per Linux semantics). Also writes the
per-fd `fire_seq` (returned by both INET_CMD_POLL and
VFS_CMD_POLL) into `*seq_out` so `epoll_scan_once` can detect
"this specific fd actually fired since the last scan" without
the per-instance bulk last_reported clear that over-fires
multi-fd EPOLLET loops.

    epoll_query_ready(target_fd: int, want: int, seq_out: *int) -> int
        *seq_out = 0
        val k = posix_fd_kind(target_fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return POSIX_EPOLLERR
        if k == POSIX_FD_UNIX_SOCKET
            val target = posix_fd_target(target_fd)
            if target < 0
                return POSIX_EPOLLERR
            val port = unix_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return 0
            var msg: [2]byte
            msg[0] = byte(UNIX_CMD_POLL)
            msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
            var reply: [7]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 7)
            if r < 0
                return POSIX_EPOLLERR
            if reply[0] != 0
                return POSIX_EPOLLERR
            val raw = ((int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2]) & 0xff)
            *seq_out = (int(reply[3]) & 0xff) | ((int(reply[4]) & 0xff) << 8) | ((int(reply[5]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(reply[6]) & 0xff) << 24)
            return raw & (want | POSIX_EPOLLERR | POSIX_EPOLLHUP)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET || k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return 0
            var msg: [4]byte
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_POLL)
            if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
                msg[1] = byte(1)
            else
                msg[1] = byte(2)
            shim_write_u16(msg, 2, posix_fd_target(target_fd))
            var reply: [7]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 7)
            if r < 0
                return POSIX_EPOLLERR
            if reply[0] != 0
                return POSIX_EPOLLERR
            val raw = shim_read_u16(reply, 1)
            *seq_out = (int(reply[3]) & 0xff) | ((int(reply[4]) & 0xff) << 8) | ((int(reply[5]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(reply[6]) & 0xff) << 24)
            // Mask: caller's requested events plus error/hangup
            // which are always reported.
            return raw & (want | POSIX_EPOLLERR | POSIX_EPOLLHUP)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FILE
            // VFS_CMD_POLL: shim ships the handle, vfs reports the
            // OFT-derived bits (regular files always-ready,
            // pipes per buffer count + peer-end state, TTYs
            // output-only).
            val port = vfs_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return 0
            val handle = posix_fd_target(target_fd)
            var msg: [5]byte
            msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_POLL_C)
            msg[1] = byte((handle >> 24) & 0xff)
            msg[2] = byte((handle >> 16) & 0xff)
            msg[3] = byte((handle >> 8) & 0xff)
            msg[4] = byte(handle & 0xff)
            var reply: [7]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 7)
            if r < 0
                return POSIX_EPOLLERR
            if reply[0] != 0
                return POSIX_EPOLLERR
            val raw = (int(reply[1]) & 0xff) | ((int(reply[2]) & 0xff) << 8)
            *seq_out = (int(reply[3]) & 0xff) | ((int(reply[4]) & 0xff) << 8) | ((int(reply[5]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(reply[6]) & 0xff) << 24)
            return raw & (want | POSIX_EPOLLERR | POSIX_EPOLLHUP)
        if k == POSIX_FD_STDIN
            // Ask the tty server for readiness on console 0 plus
            // its per-console fire_seq. Reply mirrors VFS_CMD_POLL
            // so the existing 7-byte parse layout is reused.
            val port_t = tty_get_port()
            if port_t < 0
                return 0
            var msg: [2]byte
            msg[0] = byte(TTY_CMD_POLL_C)
            msg[1] = byte(0)
            var reply: [7]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port_t, msg, 2, reply, 7)
            if r < 0
                return 0
            if reply[0] != 0
                return 0
            val raw = (int(reply[1]) & 0xff) | ((int(reply[2]) & 0xff) << 8)
            *seq_out = (int(reply[3]) & 0xff) | ((int(reply[4]) & 0xff) << 8) | ((int(reply[5]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(reply[6]) & 0xff) << 24)
            return raw & (want | POSIX_EPOLLERR | POSIX_EPOLLHUP)
        if k == POSIX_FD_STDOUT || k == POSIX_FD_STDERR
            return want & POSIX_EPOLLOUT
        if k == POSIX_FD_EVENTFD
            // Readable when count > 0; writable when count <
            // u64-1 (i.e. add of 1 wouldn't overflow). Counter is
            // i64 internally with i64::MAX representing "near max"
            // — a tighter bound than Linux's u64::MAX-1, but the
            // gap is irrelevant for any practical use.
            val pid_e = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val eidx = pid_e * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + target_fd
            val cnt = posix_fd_table[eidx].eventfd_count
            var ev = 0
            if cnt > i64(0)
                ev = ev | POSIX_EPOLLIN
            if cnt < i64(0x7fffffffffffffff)
                ev = ev | POSIX_EPOLLOUT
            return ev & (want | POSIX_EPOLLERR | POSIX_EPOLLHUP)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TIMERFD
            // Catch up before reporting so a fired-but-unread
            // timer registers as readable on the entry-pass scan.
            val pid_t = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val tidx = pid_t * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + target_fd
            timerfd_advance(tidx)
            var ev = 0
            if posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations > i64(0)
                ev = ev | POSIX_EPOLLIN
            return ev & (want | POSIX_EPOLLERR | POSIX_EPOLLHUP)
        0

`epoll_scan_once` walks the instance's entries, queries inet
once per active entry, and writes ready events into
`events_array`. EPOLLET filters down to the rising edges since
the last report (`last_reported`); EPOLLONESHOT disarms the
entry after firing once (re-armed by `EPOLL_CTL_MOD`). Returns
the number of events written.

    epoll_scan_once(inst: int, out: *byte, max_events: int) -> int
        var produced = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
            if produced >= max_events
                break
            if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use != 0
                if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].armed != 0
                    val tfd = epoll_table[inst].entries[i].watched_fd
                    val want = epoll_table[inst].entries[i].events
                    var seq = 0
                    val ready = epoll_query_ready(tfd, want, &seq)
                    // Per-fd edge detection: when the server-side
                    // counter advances past our cached value, the
                    // fd actually fired since the last scan. Reset
                    // `last_reported` so EPOLLET sees the live bits
                    // as rising again — without clobbering siblings
                    // in the same epoll instance whose counters
                    // didn't move.
                    if seq != epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_seen_seq
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_reported = 0
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_seen_seq = seq
                    var deliver = ready
                    if (want & POSIX_EPOLLET) != 0
                        // Only the bits that just became ready since
                        // the last report. If the user fully drained
                        // the fd they'd have called recv until -EAGAIN
                        // (we don't simulate that — readiness is
                        // computed each time from the live socket
                        // state), so any rising edge fires once.
                        val rising = ready & ~epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_reported
                        deliver = rising
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_reported = ready
                    if deliver != 0
                        val off = produced * POSIX_EPOLL_EVENT_SIZE
                        shim_write_le_u32(out, off, deliver)
                        shim_write_le_i64(out, off + 8,
                            epoll_table[inst].entries[i].data_lo)
                        produced += 1
                        if (want & POSIX_EPOLLONESHOT) != 0
                            // Disarm — entry stays in_use=1 so a
                            // subsequent EPOLL_CTL_MOD can re-arm it,
                            // but no further fires until then.
                            epoll_table[inst].entries[i].armed = 0
            i += 1
        produced

SYS_EPOLL_PWAIT. Phase A2: blocks via the kernel notify
primitive (or `sleep_or_notify` for finite timeouts) instead of
the old sleep-1-tick loop. inet and VFS each maintain a per-fd
subscriber list that fires `notify_send_to(tid, 1)` on every
readiness boundary, so a wait wakes within a syscall hop of the
change. `sigmask` is ignored (no signal delivery yet).

The earlier per-instance `last_reported` bulk clear has been
replaced with per-entry seq comparison inside `epoll_scan_once`:
each POLL reply carries the server's monotonic per-fd fire
counter, and only entries whose counter advanced since our last
scan get their `last_reported` reset. `notify_read_self()` is
still drained here so the kernel's notify_pending gauge clears
and `notify_wait()` can block again on the next iteration.

    sys_epoll_pwait(epfd: int, events_array: i64, max_events: int, timeout_ms: int) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(epfd) != POSIX_FD_EPOLL
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val inst = posix_fd_target(epfd)
        if inst < 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if max_events <= 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if events_array == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val out: *byte = *byte(events_array)
        var ticks_left = -1
        if timeout_ms >= 0
            ticks_left = (timeout_ms + 9) / 10
        var iter = 0
        while iter < 100000             // runaway-loop guard
            iter += 1
            // Drain any pending notify so notify_wait() can block
            // cleanly on the next loop iteration. The seq-based
            // edge detection inside epoll_scan_once handles the
            // EPOLLET clearing now — no per-instance bulk reset.
            val _ = notify_read_self()
            val produced = epoll_scan_once(inst, out, max_events)
            if produced > 0
                return i64(produced)
            if timeout_ms == 0
                return i64(0)
            if ticks_left == 0
                return i64(0)
            if timeout_ms < 0
                // Block until inet/VFS (or someone) wakes us. Spurious
                // wakes loop back to the scan above — no-op cost.
                notify_wait()
            else
                // sleep_or_notify(ticks_left) wakes on either deadline
                // or notify. Spurious wakes lose at most one extra
                // pass; we record uptime() before/after to charge the
                // actual elapsed ticks to ticks_left so a chain of
                // spurious wakes still respects the deadline.
                val before = uptime()
                sleep_or_notify(ticks_left)
                val after = uptime()
                val elapsed = after - before
                if elapsed >= ticks_left
                    ticks_left = 0
                else
                    ticks_left -= elapsed
        i64(0)

SYS_PPOLL. Linux ppoll(2): wait for any of `nfds` file descriptors
to become ready for the requested events, or until `tmo_p`
elapses. Musl's `poll(2)` wrapper routes here when `SYS_poll`
isn't defined (slix-musl's case for both arches).

Layout of `struct pollfd` (LP64, packed): fd(i32 LE @0),
events(i16 LE @4), revents(i16 LE @6) — 8 bytes per entry. The
event bits (POLLIN/OUT/ERR/HUP) match `POSIX_EPOLL*` 1:1, so the
existing `epoll_query_ready` helper drops in directly.

Strategy: short-interval poll. Each iteration calls
`epoll_query_ready` for each fd, fills revents, and counts
fds with non-zero revents. If any fired or `timeout_ms == 0`, we
return. Otherwise sleep one tick and loop. We don't pre-subscribe
each fd via `INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB` — that would tighten latency but
adds bookkeeping (transient inst, unsub on exit) that's not
worth it for the resolver's 5-second timeouts. 10 ms latency is
well below DNS round-trip.

`tmo_p` is a `struct timespec { i64 tv_sec; i64 tv_nsec; }`. NULL
means wait forever. `sigmask` and `sigsetsize` (a3, a4) are
ignored — slix has no signals.

    sys_ppoll(fds_ptr: i64, nfds: int, ts_ptr: i64) -> i64
        if nfds < 0 || nfds > 32
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if nfds > 0 && fds_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        var timeout_ms = -1
        if ts_ptr != i64(0)
            val ts: *byte = *byte(ts_ptr)
            val tv_sec = shim_read_le_i64(ts, 0)
            val tv_nsec = shim_read_le_i64(ts, 8)
            if tv_sec < i64(0) || tv_nsec < i64(0) || tv_nsec >= i64(1000000000)
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            timeout_ms = int(tv_sec * i64(1000) + tv_nsec / i64(1000000))
        val fds: *byte = *byte(fds_ptr)
        // Wall-clock deadline (in 10 ms ticks). Using a deadline rather
        // than decrementing per-iteration avoids early termination when
        // sleep_or_notify wakes after >1 tick due to scheduling latency
        // (which would happen with the old `ticks_left -= (after - before)`
        // pattern under contention — eg. 126 iters could exhaust a 250-tick
        // budget while only 1.26 s of wall time had passed).
        var deadline_tick = i64(0)
        if timeout_ms > 0
            deadline_tick = i64(uptime()) + i64((timeout_ms + 9) / 10)
        var iter = 0
        while iter < 1000000
            iter += 1
            val _ = notify_read_self()
            var ready_count = 0
            var i = 0
            while i < nfds
                val off = i * 8
                val fd = shim_read_le_u32(fds, off)
                val events = (int(fds[off + 4]) & 0xff) | ((int(fds[off + 5]) & 0xff) << 8)
                var revents = 0
                // fd < 0 (sentinel "skip this slot") or out-of-range
                // (no such slot in this process) → revents = 0. Sysl's
                // int width can be 32 or 64 in context, so combine
                // both bounds rather than relying on sign.
                if fd >= 0 && fd < POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
                    var seq = 0
                    revents = epoll_query_ready(fd, events, &seq)
                fds[off + 6] = byte(revents & 0xff)
                fds[off + 7] = byte((revents >> 8) & 0xff)
                if revents != 0
                    ready_count += 1
                i += 1
            if ready_count > 0
                return i64(ready_count)
            if timeout_ms == 0
                return i64(0)
            if timeout_ms > 0
                if i64(uptime()) >= deadline_tick
                    return i64(0)
            // Re-poll every tick (10 ms). For waits ≥ ~50 ms this is
            // imperceptible; the resolver case typically lands within
            // 1–5 ticks of the response arriving.
            sleep_or_notify(1)
        i64(0)
